operated without a human pilot onboard, often used for surveillance or military operations.", "negative_surface_forms": ["manned aircraft", "satellite"]}, {"term_type": "concept", "confidence": 0.94, "surface_forms": ["hybrid tactics", "grey zone tactics"], "canonical_term": "Hybrid Tactics", "canonical_description": "Military strategies that blend conventional, irregular, and cyber warfare methods to achieve objectives.", "negative_surface_forms": ["conventional warfare", "nuclear tactics"]}, {"term_type": "concept", "confidence": 0.93, "surface_forms": ["network-centric warfare"], "canonical_term": "Network-Centric Warfare", "canonical_description": "A military doctrine that leverages information technology to link various military assets for improved situational awareness and effectiveness.", "negative_surface_forms": ["trench warfare", "guerrilla warfare"]}, {"term_type": "concept", "confidence": 0.96, "surface_forms": ["exclusive economic zone", "EEZ"], "canonical_term": "Exclusive Economic Zone", "canonical_description": "A sea zone prescribed by the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea, over which a state has special rights regarding the exploration and use of marine resources.", "negative_surface_forms": ["territorial waters", "international waters"]}, {"term_type": "concept", "confidence": 0.95, "surface_forms": ["continental shelf", "extended continental shelf"], "canonical_term": "Continental Shelf", "canonical_description": "The extended perimeter of each continent, which is submerged under relatively shallow seas and gulfs.", "negative_surface_forms": ["abyssal plain", "mid-ocean ridge"]}, {"term_type": "process", "confidence": 0.95, "surface_forms": ["search and rescue operations", "SAR operations"], "canonical_term": "Search and Rescue Operations", "canonical_description": "Missions conducted to locate and assist people in distress or imminent danger, often in remote or hazardous environments.", "negative_surface_forms": ["combat operations", "reconnaissance missions"]}, {"term_type": "process", "confidence": 0.94, "surface_forms": ["military modernization", "modernization programs"], "canonical_term": "Military Modernization", "canonical_description": "The process of updating and improving military capabilities, including technology, equipment, and organizational structures.", "negative_surface_forms": ["demilitarization", "disarmament"]}, {"term_type": "concept", "confidence": 0.94, "surface_forms": ["arms race", "regional arms race"], "canonical_term": "Arms Race", "canonical_description": "A competition between nations for superiority in the development and accumulation of weapons.", "negative_surface_forms": ["arms control", "disarmament"]}], "extraction_metadata": {"language": "English", "total_terms": 16, "source_length": 4820, "domain_indicators": ["international relations", "security studies", "military strategy", "Arctic policy"]}}, "logprobs": [], "annotations": []}]}], "status": "completed", "metadata": {}, "reasoning": {"effort": null, "summary": null}, "background": false, "created_at": 1754950176, "truncation": "disabled", "temperature": 0.0, "tool_choice": "auto", "instructions": null, "service_tier": "default", "top_logprobs": 0, "max_tool_calls": null, "prompt_cache_key": null, "max_output_tokens": null, "safety_identifier": null, "incomplete_details": null, "parallel_tool_calls": true, "previous_response_id": null}	{}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	0.00000000	USD	24890	\N	0	\N	success	\N	\N	{}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	9ceb5049-6be6-4b08-a840-8f7a2d1d2a07	{}	f	development	2025-08-11 22:09:36.245473	2025-08-11 22:10:01.150884	1	OpenAI::Models::Responses::Response
10	OpenaiApiCall	ApiTracking::trackedApiClient	responses.create	gpt-4.1-2025-04-14	gpt-4.1-2025-04-14	{"args": [], "kwargs": {"text": "Lexicon::TermExtractionResult", "input": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a lexicon extraction specialist for the Enliterator system.\\nYour task is to extract canonical terms from content and generate:\\n1. Canonical terms (normalized, proper casing)\\n2. Surface forms (aliases, alternate spellings, abbreviations)\\n3. Negative surface forms (common confusions, things this is NOT)\\n4. Canonical descriptions (neutral, factual, 1-2 lines)\\n\\nFocus on domain-specific terms, proper nouns, concepts, and technical vocabulary.\\nFor each term, provide multiple surface forms if they exist in the text or are commonly used.\\nCanonical descriptions should be informative but neutral in tone.\\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Extract canonical terms, surface forms, and descriptions from the following content:\\n\\nChina’s Arctic Strategy – a\\nComprehensive Approach in\\nTimes of Great Power Rivalry\\n\\nRESEARCH ARTICLE\\n\\nMATTI PURANEN\\n\\nSANNA KOPRA\\n\\n*Author affiliations can be found in the back matter of this article\\n\\nABSTRACT\\n\\nThere are growing signs that great power rivalry is making a comeback in the Arctic\\nregion, and China, a self-proclaimed “near-Arctic state,” has shown growing interest\\nin expanding its activity in it. This article investigates China’s Arctic intentions in the\\ncontext  of  the  recently  emerging  notion  of  “total  national  security”  in  the  country,\\nand  suggests  that  individual  aspects  of  China’s  Arctic  strategy  –  including  its  Arctic\\nmilitary strategy – should not be analyzed on their own but should, rather, be seen as a\\nmanifestation of China’s rather unique total security conception in a regional context.\\nDrawing  insights  from  original,  official,  Chinese-language  sources,  discussions  on\\nChina’s Arctic strategy among Chinese security experts and academic communities,\\nand  by  observing  recent  Chinese  economic,  political,  and  scientific  activities  in  the\\nArctic, the article demonstrates that China’s Arctic strategy follows what we describe\\nas  a  “comprehensive  approach”  consisting  of  four  main  arms:  political,  economic,\\nscientific,  and  military.  This  strategy  is  designed  to  advance  the  party-state’s\\ninfluence in the Arctic through simultaneously moulding discourses and challenging\\nlegal  concepts  of  the  Arctic  (political),  boosting  its  economic  interests  in  the  Arctic\\n(economic), fusing civil-military scientific and technological innovation (scientific), and\\npreparing for a military presence in the Arctic (military). Finally, the article concludes\\nthat as the significance of the Arctic for China’s long-term security strategy is guided\\nby  Xi  Jinping’s  thought  on  total  national  security,  future  analyses  of  China’s  Arctic\\nintentions should pay particular attention to this evolving concept.\\n\\nCORRESPONDING AUTHOR:\\nMatti Puranen\\n\\nFinnish National Defence\\nUniversity, FI\\n\\nmatti.puranen@mil.fi\\n\\nKEYWORDS:\\nChina; Arctic; strategy;\\ninternational relations;\\ngeopolitics\\n\\nTO CITE THIS ARTICLE:\\nPuranen, M., & Kopra, S.\\n(2023). China’s Arctic Strategy\\n– a Comprehensive Approach\\nin Times of Great Power\\nRivalry. Scandinavian Journal\\nof Military Studies, 6(1), pp.\\n239–253. DOI: https://doi.\\norg/10.31374/sjms.196\\n\\nSCANDINAVIAN JOURNAL OFMILITARY STUDIES\\fINTRODUCTION\\n\\n240\\n\\nThe strategic importance of the Circumpolar North is increasing as Arctic sea ice melts even\\nmore rapidly than expected due to accelerating climate change (see, for example, Guarino et\\nal., 2020). Since new Arctic shipping routes and economic opportunities in resource extraction\\nwill  open  in  the  coming  years,  it  is  hardly  surprising  that  Chinese  strategic  calculations\\nconcerning  the  Arctic  have  also  grown  steadily  during  the  last  decade.  Officially  China  now\\ndefines itself as a “near-Arctic state” with ambitious plans to establish itself as a “polar great\\npower” by 2035 (State Oceanic Administration, 2014). At the same time, the security situation\\nof the Arctic region, once viewed as an “international zone of peace” (see Gorbachev, 1987),\\nhas deteriorated; the long-term regional impacts of Russia’s attack on Ukraine in early 2022,\\nmeanwhile, are yet to be fully felt.\\n\\nBesides its economic potential, China increasingly sees the Arctic region as a security issue; the\\nChinese top leadership, however, has remained notoriously silent on exactly how China plans to\\nadvance and protect its interests there. China’s first Arctic policy white paper, published in 2018,\\nsimply states that China “shoulders the important mission” in promoting security within the\\nArctic region but refrains from providing any further details (State Council Information Office,\\n2018). Official jargon aside, numerous Chinese experts in the fields of international relations\\nand  security  studies  emphasize  the  importance  of  the  Polar  regions,  urging  the  Chinese\\nleadership to begin developing the capabilities required for securing Chinese interests within\\nthese new domains of great power competition. Notably, one of the most authoritative of such\\nvoices, The Science of Military Strategy, a textbook published by the Chinese National Defense\\nUniversity (NDU) in 2020, states that the Arctic region “represents the main direction in which\\nour national interests are expanding,” and that the region is bound to provide new missions for\\nthe Chinese military forces as well (Xiao 2020, pp. 166–167).1 The views proposed in the book\\nseries are seen by scholars as representative of the views of the Chinese military leadership (see\\nFravel, 2017). The Science of Military Strategy states, notably, that in expanding towards the\\nArctic, China must follow an extremely cautious grand strategic approach serving the nation’s\\nlarger foreign policy objectives. In such an approach, unnecessary demonstrations of military\\npower  in  the  region  might  only  evoke  “international  contradictions”  and  harm  China’s  great\\npower  relations  (Xiao,  2020,  pp.  166–167).  Other  scholars  even  warn  of  the  emergence  of\\n“Arctic China threat theories” (中国的北极威胁论), which the Western states are seen as being\\nspreading on purpose, potentially hindering the prospects of China’s Arctic advance (Liu, 2022).\\n\\nChina’s  Arctic  ambiguity  is  thus  likely  a  very  conscious  choice.  China  –  notwithstanding  its\\n“near-Arctic”  self-identification  –  is  located  far  away  from  the  Arctic  regions,  and  most  of\\nthe Arctic Ocean falls under the jurisdiction of the five Arctic littoral states (Canada, Denmark\\nvia  Greenland,  Norway,  Russia,  and  the  United  States)  with  sovereign  rights  in  exploiting\\nlucrative  Arctic  resources.  China  needs  friendly  or  at  least  cooperative  relations  with  them\\nto be able to utilize these mineral and energy sources, not to mention land-based resources.\\nIn  communicating  its  Arctic  intentions,  hence,  the  Chinese  leadership  has  been  extremely\\ncautious, emphasizing the cooperative and peaceful nature of its Arctic policy and shying away\\nfrom any hard security topics – aware that its statements and actions are under tight scrutiny\\nby the international community, especially by the Arctic states themselves.\\n\\nThe  vagueness  of  official  Chinese  rhetoric  combined  with  general  uncertainty  about  the\\nintentions of the Chinese Arctic strategy has left ample room to be filled by the imagination of\\ninternational scholars and practitioners. Some observers believe that China is developing Arctic\\ndual-use ports to serve as bases for its nuclear submarines on patrol under the Polar ice cap\\n(see, for example, Brady, 2019; Goldstein, 2019; Ghoshal, 2020). In the most “alarmist” visions,\\nas in the words of the former U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo, China’s expansion could even\\ntransform the Arctic into “a new South China Sea, fraught with militarization and competing\\nterritorial  claims”  (Pompeo,  2019).  While  Pompeo’s  vision  may  be  a  little  exaggerated,  it\\nseems  reasonable  to  believe  that  China’s  growing  regional  presence  will  significantly  shape\\nthe  political  and  economic  futures  of  the  Arctic  (see  Kauppila  &  Kopra,  2022;  Kopra,  2022).\\nAgainst this backdrop, it is hardly surprising that much recent literature explores China’s Arctic\\ninterests, political and economic activities, and contributions to regional governance (see, for\\nexample, Bennett & Eiterjord, 2022; Fravel, Lavelle & Odgaard, 2021; Kauppila, 2022; Kobzeva\\n\\n1\\n\\nThe book has its own subchapter on military operations within the Polar regions.\\n\\nPuranen and Kopra  Scandinavian Journal of Military Studies  DOI: 10.31374/sjms.196\\f241\\n\\n& Todorov, 2023; Koivurova & Kopra, 2020; Odgaard, 2021; Sørensen, 2021). Apart from a few\\nexceptions (e.g. Brady, 2019; Lanteigne, 2020; MacDonald, 2021; Staun & Sørensen..."}], "model": "gpt-4.1", "temperature": 0.0}}	{"id": "resp_689a6a39bcb881a3b3f6237582cde8b10811cfec3a3564db", "text": {"format_": {"name": "TermExtractionResult", "type": "json_schema", "schema": {"type": "object", "required": ["extracted_terms", "extraction_metadata"], "properties": {"extracted_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["canonical_term", "surface_forms", "negative_surface_forms", "canonical_description", "term_type", "confidence"], "properties": {"term_type": {"enum": ["concept", "entity", "place", "event", "process", "attribute", "general"], "type": "string"}, "confidence": {"type": "number"}, "surface_forms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "canonical_term": {"type": "string"}, "canonical_description": {"type": "string"}, "negative_surface_forms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}}, "additionalProperties": false}}, "extraction_metadata": {"type": "object", "required": ["total_terms", "source_length", "language", "domain_indicators"], "properties": {"language": {"type": "string"}, "total_terms": {"type": "integer"}, "source_length": {"type": "integer"}, "domain_indicators": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}}, "additionalProperties": false}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "strict": true, "description": null}, "verbosity": "medium"}, "user": null, "error": null, "model": "gpt-4.1-2025-04-14", "store": true, "tools": [], "top_p": 1.0, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2269, "total_tokens": 3728, "output_tokens": 1459, "input_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 0}}, "object": "response", "output": [{"id": "msg_689a6a3b5d5081a3b221b2681735e6b50811cfec3a3564db", "role": "assistant", "type": "message", "status": "completed", "content": [{"text": "{\\n  \\"extracted_terms\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\"canonical_term\\": \\"Arctic Strategy\\",\\n      \\"surface_forms\\": [\\"China’s Arctic strategy\\", \\"Arctic strategy\\", \\"Chinese Arctic strategy\\"],\\n      \\"negative_surface_forms\\": [\\"Antarctic strategy\\", \\"Tropical strategy\\"],\\n      \\"canonical_description\\": \\"A set of policies and actions formulated by a state, in this case China, to advance its interests and presence in the Arctic region.\\",\\n      \\"term_type\\": \\"concept\\",\\n      \\"confidence\\": 0.98\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"canonical_term\\": \\"Great Power Rivalry\\",\\n      \\"surface_forms\\": [\\"great power rivalry\\", \\"great power competition\\", \\"great power relations\\"],\\n      \\"negative_surface_forms\\": [\\"small state rivalry\\", \\"regional rivalry\\"],\\n      \\"canonical_description\\": \\"Competition and strategic tension between major world powers, often influencing regional and global politics.\\",\\n      \\"term_type\\": \\"concept\\",\\n      \\"confidence\\": 0.95\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"canonical_term\\": \\"Near-Arctic State\\",\\n      \\"surface_forms\\": [\\"near-Arctic state\\", \\"self-proclaimed near-Arctic state\\"],\\n      \\"negative_surface_forms\\": [\\"Arctic state\\", \\"non-Arctic state\\"],\\n      \\"canonical_description\\": \\"A term used by China to describe its proximity and interest in the Arctic, despite not being geographically located within the Arctic Circle.\\",\\n      \\"term_type\\": \\"concept\\",\\n      \\"confidence\\": 0.92\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"canonical_term\\": \\"Total National Security\\",\\n      \\"surface_forms\\": [\\"total national security\\", \\"total security conception\\"],\\n      \\"negative_surface_forms\\": [\\"partial security\\", \\"military-only security\\"],\\n      \\"canonical_description\\": \\"A comprehensive security doctrine in China that integrates political, economic, military, and other aspects to safeguard national interests.\\",\\n      \\"term_type\\": \\"concept\\",\\n      \\"confidence\\": 0.96\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"canonical_term\\": \\"Comprehensive Approach\\",\\n      \\"surface_forms\\": [\\"comprehensive approach\\", \\"comprehensive strategy\\"],\\n      \\"negative_surface_forms\\": [\\"single-issue approach\\", \\"narrow approach\\"],\\n      \\"canonical_description\\": \\"A strategy that integrates multiple domains—political, economic, scientific, and military—to achieve national objectives.\\",\\n      \\"term_type\\": \\"concept\\",\\n      \\"confidence\\": 0.93\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"canonical_term\\": \\"Party-State\\",\\n      \\"surface_forms\\": [\\"party-state\\", \\"Chinese party-state\\"],\\n      \\"negative_surface_forms\\": [\\"multi-party state\\", \\"private sector\\"],\\n      \\"canonical_description\\": \\"A political system in which the ruling party and the state apparatus are closely integrated, as in the case of China.\\",\\n      