12	OpenaiApiCall	ApiTracking::trackedApiClient	responses.create	gpt-4.1-2025-04-14	gpt-4.1-2025-04-14	{"args": [], "kwargs": {"text": "Lexicon::TermExtractionResult", "input": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a lexicon extraction specialist for the Enliterator system.\\nYour task is to extract canonical terms from content and generate:\\n1. Canonical terms (normalized, proper casing)\\n2. Surface forms (aliases, alternate spellings, abbreviations)\\n3. Negative surface forms (common confusions, things this is NOT)\\n4. Canonical descriptions (neutral, factual, 1-2 lines)\\n\\nFocus on domain-specific terms, proper nouns, concepts, and technical vocabulary.\\nFor each term, provide multiple surface forms if they exist in the text or are commonly used.\\nCanonical descriptions should be informative but neutral in tone.\\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Extract canonical terms, surface forms, and descriptions from the following content:\\n\\nCHAPTER  7\\n\\nChina’s  Arctic  Strategy:  From  “Follow-Up”\\nto  “Creative  Involvement”?\\n\\nXu  Qingchao\\n\\nIntroduction\\n\\nA  voice  in  the  West  believes  that  “for  the  Arctic,  China  seeks  to  pursue\\nan  economic  development  strategy  for  the  region  that  requires  that  the\\nArctic  be  open  to  Chinese  development  and  that  China  is  given  equal\\nstanding  to  other  Arctic  nations.  This  is  the  win-win  strategy  that  Beijing\\nis  currently  implementing  in  the  Arctic”  (Conley,  2018).  China’s  Arctic\\nPolicy  White  Paper  was  published  in  January  2018,  which  “caused  much\\nconsternation  internationally,  as  it  deﬁned  China  as  a  ‘near-Arctic  state’\\nand  presented  the  idea  of  a  ‘Polar  Silk  Road’  (PSR)  linked  to  the  Belt\\nand  Road  Initiative  (BRI)”  (Klimenko,  2018),  allowing  journalists  to\\ndevelop  a  narrative  about  the  country’s  interest  and  actions  in  the  polar\\nsphere  (Moscato,  2018)  “The  release  of  the  White  Paper  may  have  cleared\\nsome  misconceptions  about  China’s  Arctic  interest,  but  it  lacks  concrete\\n\\nX.  Qingchao  (B)\\nUniversity  of  Chinese  Academy  of  Sciences,  Beijing,  China\\ne-mail:  xuqingchao@ucas.ac.cn\\n\\n©  The  Author(s),  under  exclusive  license  to  Springer  Nature\\nSwitzerland  AG  2024\\nP.  Midford  et  al.  (eds.),  Navigating  East  Asian  Maritime  Conﬂicts:\\nTechnological  Change,  Environmental  Challenges,  Global  and  Regional\\nResponses,  Palgrave  Studies  in  Maritime  Politics  and  Security,\\nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-51989-5_7\\n\\n151\\n\\n\\f152\\n\\nX. QINGCHAO\\n\\nsteps  or  measures  that  China  intends  to  implement  in  its  Arctic  policy”\\n(Lim,  2018).  However,  “much  remains  uncertain  on  aspects  of  China’s\\nkey  interests  that  are  not  addressed  in  the  White  Paper,  in  particular  on\\nthe  role  of  the  Arctic  in  its  national  security  and  defence  strategy”  (Lim,\\n2018).\\n\\nIn  accordance  with  positioning  China  as  a  “strategic  rival”  of  the\\nUnited  States,  a  provocative  speech  by  US  Secretary  of  State  Mike\\nPompeo  of  the  Trump  administration  argued  that  “We’re  entering  a  new\\nage  of  strategic  engagement  in  the  Arctic  …  There  are  only  Arctic  States\\nand  Non-Arctic  States.  No  third  category  exists,  and  claiming  other-\\nwise  entitles  China  to  exactly  nothing”  (Pompeo,  2019).  In  response\\nto  this  view,  some  critics  argued  that,  “the  key  to  U.S.  policy  toward\\nChina’s  Arctic  inﬂuence  and  activities  should  begin  with  solid  assessment\\nof  China’s  concrete  capabilities  instead  of  speculations  about  its  inten-\\ntions  and  potential”  (Sun,  2020).  It  is  worth  noting  that  in  recent  years\\nneither  China’s  “Near-Arctic  State”  self-identity  in  the  Arctic  nor  the\\nPSR  conﬁrmed  in  China’s  Arctic  Policy  White  Paper  have  been  further\\nimplemented  in  paper  or  in  practice.\\n\\nIn  this  context,  a  question  is  raised  here:  Is  there  any  change  occur-\\nring  in  China’s  policy  toward  the  changing  Arctic?  This  study  aims  to\\nexamine  the  emerging  shift  of  China’s  Arctic  policy  from  the  past  to\\nthe  future.  For  this  purpose,\\nit  outlines  China’s  “follow-up”  strategy\\nfor  the  Arctic  from  1999  to  2019  ﬁrst,  then  it  identiﬁes  consequential\\ngeo-strategic  dates  for  the  Arctic  in  the  twenty-ﬁrst  century.  Prospec-\\ntive  scenarios  of  Arctic  security  dilemmas  involving  China  and  potential\\noptions  available  for  China  are  highlighted\\nin  the  following  section.\\nFinally,  taking  the  practice  of  China’s  climate  diplomacy  into  account,  it\\ntries  to  explain  China’s  emerging  alternative  Arctic  strategy  characterized\\nby  “creative  involvement.”\\n\\nChina’s  “Follow-Up”  Strategy\\nfor  the  Arctic  from  1999  to  2019\\n\\nincreased  visibly  and  substantially,  particularly\\n\\nSince  the  end  of  the  twentieth  century,  Chinese  presence  in  the  Arctic\\nhas\\nits  scientiﬁc  and\\neconomic  activities,  taking  advantage  of  the  rights  and  interests  granted\\nby  the  Svalbard  Treaty.  In  2020,  a  turning  point  for  China’s  Arctic\\nengagement  appeared  due  to  increased  uncertainties  in  the  Arctic  geo-\\nis  very\\nstrategic  environment\\n\\nin  the  context  of  COVID-19.  Thus,\\n\\nin\\n\\nit\\n\\n\\f7 CHINA’S ARCTIC STRATEGY: FROM “FOLLOW-UP” … 153\\n\\nhelpful  to  take  China’s  Arctic  involvement  from  1999  to  2019  as  a  distinct\\nperiod  to  view  China’s  Arctic  policy  in  practice.\\n\\nDuring  this  period,  milestones  in  China’s  Arctic  involvement  include:\\n(1)  From  1999  to  the  end  of  October  2019,  China  conducted  10  Arctic\\nexpeditions  in  total;  (2)  In  2004,  China’s  ﬁrst  Arctic  observation  station,\\nthe  Yellow  River  Station,  was  established  in  Svalbard;  (3)  In  2013,  China\\ngained  permanent  observer  status  in  the  Arctic  Council;  (4)  In  2015,  the\\nArctic  (originally  “polar  regions”  in  Chinese)  was  connected  for  the  ﬁrst\\ntime  to  China’s  national  security,  in  Article  32  of  China’s  National  Secu-\\nrity  Law;  (5)  In  January  2017,  Chinese  President  Xi  Jinping  positioned\\nthe  polar  regions  (both  the  Arctic  and  Antarctic)  as  “new  frontiers”  along\\nwith  the  deep  ocean,  outer  space  and  cyberspace;  (6)  Also  in  2017,  China\\nand  Russia  proposed  the  concept  of  the  “Polar  Silk  Road”  (“Ice  Silk\\nRoad”/“Arctic  Silk  Road”)  during  Chinese  President  Xi’s  ofﬁcial  visit\\nto  Moscow,  connecting  China’s  BRI  with  Russia’s  Eurasian  Economic\\nUnion  (EEU)  plan  through  bilateral  cooperation  in  the  Russian  Arctic\\nregion;  (7)  In  January  2018,  China  published  China’s  Arctic  Policy  White\\nPaper;  (8)  In  October  2018,  the  China–Iceland  Arctic  Research  Obser-\\nvatory  (CIAO)  formally  opened;  (9)  and  in  2019,  China’s  ﬁrst  self-built\\npolar  icebreaker  Xuelong  2  was  launched  to  boost  China’s  polar  research\\nand  expedition  capabilities.\\n\\nBased  on  the  timeline  of  China’s  involvement  in  the  Arctic,  China’s\\noverall  strategy  for  the  Arctic  can  be  deﬁned  as  a  “follow-up”  strategy.\\nAccording  to  the  Modern  Chinese  Dictionary,  “follow-up”  (Genjin\\nin\\nChinese)  literally  has  four  meanings,  which  are:  follow  and  straightfor-\\nward;  quote  or  imitate  others;  step-by-step;  and  move  forward  as  expected\\nuntil  completion.  In  this  context,  China’s  “follow-up”  strategy  for  the\\nArctic  refers  to  a  gradual,  step-by-step  and  non-coercive  approach  to\\nArctic  affairs.  Within  its  capacity,  China  prioritizes  science  diplomacy  in\\nthe  face  of  dramatic  climate  change  and  promotes  economic  and  trade\\ncooperation  with  other  Arctic  stakeholders  for  common  development.\\nChina’s  competitive  advantages  in  addressing  Arctic  affairs  vary  with  its\\neconomic  development  and  so  does  its  “follow-up”  strategy.\\n\\nThe  “follow-up”  strategy  has  two  variants:  “passive  follow-up”  and\\n“active  follow-up”.  Comparatively,  from  1999  to  2012,  China  adopted\\na  modest  “passive  follow-up”  strategy  for  the  Arctic.  In  the  mid-1990s,\\nIceland  attempted  to  persuade  China  to  be\\nin  Arctic  affairs\\nduring  a  special  visit  to  Beijing,  without  eliciting  expected  responses.\\nThis  indicates  that  the  Arctic  was  not  on  China’s  agenda  at  that  time.\\n\\ninvolved\\n\\n\\f154\\n\\nX. QINGCHAO\\n\\nHowever,  after  2013,  China  switched  to  an  “active  follow-up”  strategy\\nafter  obtaining  its  observer  status  in  the  Arctic  Council.  In  international\\nmedia,  China  has  been  portrayed  as  a  proactive,  assertive,  and  ambitious\\nArctic  player.\\n\\nFrom  a  holistic  perspective,  China’s  “follow-up”  strategy  in  the  Arctic\\nhas  been  closely  related  to  China’s  domestic  economic  development\\nstrategy  and  its  diplomatic  transition  especially  since  2009,  resulting  from..."}], "model": "gpt-4.1", "temperature": 0.0}}	{"id": "resp_689a6a4ecc048191b7880aba80e0e36e02e4b7dab51c8170", "text": {"format_": {"name": "TermExtractionResult", "type": "json_schema", "schema": {"type": "object", "required": ["extracted_terms", "extraction_metadata"], "properties": {"extracted_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["canonical_term", "surface_forms", "negative_surface_forms", "canonical_description", "term_type", "confidence"], "properties": {"term_type": {"enum": ["concept", "entity", "place", "event", "process", "attribute", "general"], "type": "string"}, "confidence": {"type": "number"}, "surface_forms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "canonical_term": 