nternational Geophysical Year’ or IGY)"], "canonical_term": "International Geophysical Year", "canonical_description": "A global scientific project that took place in 1957–1958, originally the third International Polar Year, focusing on geophysical phenomena.", "negative_surface_forms": ["International Polar Year"]}, {"term_type": "entity", "confidence": 0.98, "surface_forms": ["Community Adaptation and Vulnerability in Arctic Regions", "CAVIAR", "CAVIAR project", "IPY project #157"], "canonical_term": "Community Adaptation and Vulnerability in Arctic Regions", "canonical_description": "A research project under IPY 2007–2008 focused on community adaptation and vulnerability in Arctic regions, involving interdisciplinary and community-based approaches.", "negative_surface_forms": []}, {"term_type": "entity", "confidence": 0.97, "surface_forms": ["International Council for Science", "ICSU"], "canonical_term": "International Council for Science", "canonical_description": "An international non-governmental organization that coordinates scientific activity and co-sponsored the International Polar Year 2007–2008.", "negative_surface_forms": []}, {"term_type": "entity", "confidence": 0.97, "surface_forms": ["World Meteorological Organization", "WMO"], "canonical_term": "World Meteorological Organization", "canonical_description": "A specialized agency of the United Nations responsible for meteorology, co-sponsor of the International Polar Year 2007–2008.", "negative_surface_forms": []}, {"term_type": "concept", "confidence": 0.95, "surface_forms": ["vulnerability", "adaptation risks"], "canonical_term": "vulnerability", "canonical_description": "A concept in social and environmental sciences referring to the susceptibility of a community or system to harm from exposure to stresses.", "negative_surface_forms": []}, {"term_type": "concept", "confidence": 0.95, "surface_forms": ["resilience"], "canonical_term": "resilience", "canonical_description": "The capacity of a community or system to absorb disturbance and reorganize while undergoing change, maintaining essential function and structure.", "negative_surface_forms": []}, {"term_type": "concept", "confidence": 0.95, "surface_forms": ["adaptive capacity"], "canonical_term": "adaptive capacity", "canonical_description": "The ability of a system or community to adjust to potential damage, take advantage of opportunities, or respond to consequences.", "negative_surface_forms": []}, {"term_type": "process", "confidence": 0.94, "surface_forms": ["community-based vulnerability assessment"], "canonical_term": "community-based vulnerability assessment", "canonical_description": "An approach to evaluating vulnerability that starts with the interests and observations of local communities, emphasizing local knowledge and participation.", "negative_surface_forms": []}, {"term_type": "place", "confidence": 0.96, "surface_forms": ["polar regions", "the Arctic", "Antarctica", "the North"], "canonical_term": "polar regions", "canonical_description": "The areas surrounding the Earth's North and South Poles, including the Arctic and Antarctic, characterized by extreme environmental conditions.", "negative_surface_forms": []}, {"term_type": "concept", "confidence": 0.95, "surface_forms": ["social sciences", "social research", "social/human scientists", "social science field"], "canonical_term": "social sciences", "canonical_description": "Academic disciplines that study society and human behavior, increasingly integrated into polar research.", "negative_surface_forms": ["natural sciences", "geophysics"]}, {"term_type": "entity", "confidence": 0.95, "surface_forms": ["indigenous people", "polar indigenous people", "polar residents"], "canonical_term": "indigenous people", "canonical_description": "Original inhabitants of the polar regions, whose knowledge and participation are increasingly recognized in polar research.", "negative_surface_forms": []}, {"term_type": "entity", "confidence": 0.98, "surface_forms": ["Grete K. Hovelsrud", "G. K. Hovelsrud"], "canonical_term": "Grete K. Hovelsrud", "canonical_description": "A researcher and editor associated with the Center for International Climate and Environmental Research - Oslo (CICERO), co-editor of the referenced book.", "negative_surface_forms": []}, {"term_type": "entity", "confidence": 0.98, "surface_forms": ["Barry Smit", "B. Smit"], "canonical_term": "Barry Smit", "canonical_description": "A researcher and editor from the University of Guelph, Department of Geography, co-editor of the referenced book.", "negative_surface_forms": []}, {"term_type": "entity", "confidence": 0.97, "surface_forms": ["Center for International Climate and Environmental Research - Oslo", "CICERO"], "canonical_term": "Center for International Climate and Environmental Research - Oslo", "canonical_description": "A Norwegian research institute focused on climate and environmental issues, affiliated with Grete K. Hovelsrud.", "negative_surface_forms": []}, {"term_type": "entity", "confidence": 0.97, "surface_forms": ["University of Guelph"], "canonical_term": "University of Guelph", "canonical_description": "A Canadian university, home to Barry Smit and the Department of Geography.", "negative_surface_forms": []}, {"term_type": "entity", "confidence": 0.97, "surface_forms": ["Springer Science+Business Media", "Springer"], "canonical_term": "Springer Science+Business Media", "canonical_description": "An international publishing company specializing in academic books and journals, publisher of the referenced book.", "negative_surface_forms": []}], "extraction_metadata": {"language": "English", "total_terms": 16, "source_length": 6542, "domain_indicators": ["polar research", "climate science", "social sciences", "Arctic studies"]}}, "logprobs": [], "annotations": []}]}], "status": "completed", "metadata": {}, "reasoning": {"effort": null, "summary": null}, "background": false, "created_at": 1754950255, "truncation": "disabled", "temperature": 0.0, "tool_choice": "auto", "instructions": null, "service_tier": "default", "top_logprobs": 0, "max_tool_calls": null, "prompt_cache_key": null, "max_output_tokens": null, "safety_identifier": null, "incomplete_details": null, "parallel_tool_calls": true, "previous_response_id": null}	{}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	0.00000000	USD	21492	\N	0	\N	success	\N	\N	{}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	9ceb5049-6be6-4b08-a840-8f7a2d1d2a07	{}	f	development	2025-08-11 22:10:54.482402	2025-08-11 22:11:15.997612	1	OpenAI::Models::Responses::Response
18	OpenaiApiCall	ApiTracking::trackedApiClient	responses.create	gpt-4.1-2025-04-14	gpt-4.1-2025-04-14	{"args": [], "kwargs": {"text": "Lexicon::TermExtractionResult", "input": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a lexicon extraction specialist for the Enliterator system.\\nYour task is to extract canonical terms from content and generate:\\n1. Canonical terms (normalized, proper casing)\\n2. Surface forms (aliases, alternate spellings, abbreviations)\\n3. Negative surface forms (common confusions, things this is NOT)\\n4. Canonical descriptions (neutral, factual, 1-2 lines)\\n\\nFocus on domain-specific terms, proper nouns, concepts, and technical vocabulary.\\nFor each term, provide multiple surface forms if they exist in the text or are commonly used.\\nCanonical descriptions should be informative but neutral in tone.\\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Extract canonical terms, surface forms, and descriptions from the following content:\\n\\nArticle\\nComparative Analysis of Arctic-Related Strategies at the\\nNational Level: Competition and Collaboration\\n\\nXiaoxue Ma 1, Liguang Chen 1, Weijie Wu 1, Yang Liu 2, Weiliang Qiao 3,*\\n\\nand Laihao Ma 3\\n\\n1\\n\\n2\\n\\nPublic Administration and Humanities College, Dalian Maritime University, Dalian 116026, China;\\nmaxx1020@dlmu.edu.cn (X.M.); chenliguang@dlmu.edu.cn (L.C.); ggwj422@dlmu.edu.cn (W.W.)\\nSchool of Maritime Economics and Management, Dalian Maritime University, Dalian 116026, China;\\nliuyang0120@dlmu.edu.cn\\n\\n3 Marine Engineering College, Dalian Maritime University, Dalian 116026, China; malaihao@dlmu.edu.cn\\n* Correspondence: xiaoqiao_fang@dlmu.edu.cn\\n\\nAbstract: The melting of ice creates favorable conditions for the development of the Arctic by\\nelevating the Arctic’s geostrategic value and stoking national competition. As a result, many states,\\nboth within and beyond the Arctic region, have recently released Arctic strategies or updated existing\\nones, clarifying their roles, interests, priorities, and policies for Arctic international cooperation. In this\\nstudy, a database is generated with a collection of Arctic strategies from 16 states, and then grounded\\ntheory is applied to code the texts in these strategy documents. Using this code, word-frequency\\nanalysis, cross-tabulation, correlation analysis, and cooperative-network analysis are implemented to\\nexplore competition and collaboration in the Arctic among these 16 states. The results indicate that\\nArctic states are signiﬁcantly concerned with governance, economic and social development, and\\nsecurity and stability, while environmental protection and scientiﬁc research are given more attention\\nby non-Arctic states. In addition, the highest value of the Pearson correlation coefﬁcient is identiﬁed\\nas 0.85 for collaboration and Arctic public security affairs, which indicates that collaboration on Arctic\\nsecurity will dominate the development of Arctic affairs in the near future.\\n\\nKeywords: Arctic strategy; grounded theory; comparative analysis; collaborative analysis\\n\\nCitation: Ma, X.; Chen, L.; Wu, W.;\\n\\nLiu, Y.; Qiao, W.; Ma, L. Comparative\\n\\nAnalysis of Arctic-Related Strategies\\n\\nat the National Level: Competition\\n\\nand Collaboration. Systems 2023, 11,\\n\\n413. https://doi.org/10.3390/\\n\\nsystems11080413\\n\\nAcademic Editor: Mitsuru Kodama\\n\\nReceived: 11 July 2023\\n\\nRevised: 6 August 2023\\n\\nAccepted: 9 August 2023\\n\\nPublished: 10 August 2023\\n\\nCopyright: © 2023 by the authors.\\n\\nLicensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland.\\n\\nThis article is an open access article\\n\\ndistributed under\\n\\nthe terms and\\n\\nconditions of the Creative Commons\\n\\nAttribution (CC BY) license (https://\\n\\ncreativecommons.org/licenses/by/\\n\\n4.0/).\\n\\n1. Introduction\\n\\nThe Arctic region is warming 3–4 times as fast as the rest of the world, and the impacts\\nof climate change and rising temperatures are felt more dramatically in the Arctic than\\nanywhere else on the planet. There is almost a sixty-percent chance of an effectively ice-free\\nArctic Ocean in the 2030s, which is much earlier than the average projection obtained from\\nglobal climate models [1]. As global temperatures continue to rise and sea ice continues to\\nmelt, the challenges of resource development in the Arctic are gradually diminishing, while\\nits strategic importance is growing. The Arctic region has abundant oil and gas resources.\\nThe United States Geological Survey has assessed the area north of the Arctic Circle and\\nconcluded that the region may have approximately 30% of the world’s undiscovered gas\\nand 13% of the world’s undiscovered oil. The abundant oil and gas resources in the Arctic\\nregion serve as a signiﬁcant driving force for countries to prioritize their attention on\\nthe Arctic. Climate change has various impacts on human activities, including shipping\\nindustries and indigenous peoples [2]. Climate change in the Arctic led several observers,\\nscientists, media, and government ofﬁcials to consider the possibility of developing new\\nshipping routes along Arctic routes, as these routes shorten the distance between Europe\\nand Asia [3]. The Northern Sea Route (NSR) will reduce travel distance by approximately\\n40% [4–6], contributing to savings of 3–5% in fuel costs when compared to the conventional\\nSuez Canal Route (SCR) [7].\\n\\nThere are risks and opportunities in the Arctic. With the advent of the post-Cold War\\nera, the Arctic has emerged as a region for sustainable future growth, and various forms\\n\\nSystems 2023, 11, 413. https://doi.org/10.3390/systems11080413\\n\\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/journal/systems\\n\\nsystems\\fSystems 2023, 11, 413\\n\\n2 of 18\\n\\nof cooperation and conﬂict coexist as mutual interests between coastal states [8]. In the\\nArctic, indigenous peoples are again seeing their food security threatened and cultural\\ncontinuity in danger of disruption [9]. Arctic climate change will have global impacts\\nbeyond the Arctic region. The higher Arctic temperatures promote thawing and erosion of\\nthe polar permafrost, which can result in the release of large amounts of carbon dioxide\\nand methane, providing signiﬁcant propulsion to global warming [10]. Increases in the\\nglobal sea level due to the melting of Arctic land ice are another important impact of\\nArctic warming [11–13], and this ice melt is expected to weaken the ocean’s meridional\\noverturning circulation, with implications for regional climate change and global ocean\\nheat and carbon uptake [14]. The difference-in-difference (DID) method was utilized to\\nestimate the inﬂuence of Russia’s Arctic policy on the development of ports along the\\nNSR [15].\\n\\nThe Arctic is of great geostrategic and economic importance and attracts attention from\\nboth Arctic and non-Arctic states. Currently, numerous states have formulated policies\\nand propositions regarding participation in Arctic governance. The Arctic policy of non-\\nArctic states has attracted wide attention around the world. Xinmin Ma examined China’s\\ninaugural White Paper through the lens of international law and concluded that China’s\\nArctic policy is based on international law and pursues common interests [16]. Similarly,\\nthere are discrepancies in the Arctic policies released by the same states on different issues.\\nWhile most non-Arctic nations have formulated only a single Arctic policy, Arctic states\\nhave developed multiple policies on the region. In addition to the Arctic strategy issued by\\nthe White House, the Department of Energy has also released its own Arctic strategy to\\nguide its operations in the region. The initial comparative studies and analyses of Arctic\\nstrategies were published in the early 2010s [17]. Comparative analysis was conducted for\\nthe conceptual framework and key priorities of the Arctic policy of the Russian Federation\\nand other circumpolar powers based on a study of their national Arctic strategies [18].\\nKim H J aimed to analyze the Korean government’s Master Plan from both the legal and\\npolicy perspectives, as well as to make constructive comments for its improvement [19].\\nWeingartner K A et al. examined how the distinct styles and preferences of Presidents\\nObama and Trump in the US Arctic policy-making process interact with growing climate\\nchange and defence challenges in the region [20]. Some scholars have studied the Arctic\\nstrategies of several Arctic states. By utilizing conceptually informed thematic content\\nanalysis, we illustrate the creation, circulation, and utilization of resource-related affects,\\nmainly desire, hope, pride, and denial, in shaping the Arctic resource policies of states [21].\\nA growing number of countries are turning their attention to the region. There has\\nbeen extensive research on Arctic policy by both Arctic and non-Arctic states. In previous\\nstudies, more emphasis has been placed on Arctic policy research in individual countries,\\nand few existing studies have addressed the allocation of attention in policy texts to a\\ngiven topic and the correlations between different issues. At the same time, the current\\nresearch on Arctic policy has primarily..."}], "model": "gpt-4.1", "temperature": 0.0}}	{"id": "resp_689a6a8437708190996dd1a57982fc7e0905d932a0e2aef8", "text": {"format_": {"name": "TermExtractionResult", "type": "json_schema", "schema": {"type": "object", "required": ["extracted_terms", "extraction_metadata"], "properties": {"extracted_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["canonical_term", "surface_forms", "negative_surface_forms", "canonical_description", "term_type", "confidence"], "properties": {"term_type": {"enum": ["concept", "entity", "place", "event", "process", "attribute", "general"], "type": "string"}, "confidence": {"type": "number"}, "surface_forms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "canonical_term": {"type": "string"}, "canonical_description": {"type": "string"}, "negative_surface_forms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}}, "additionalProperties": false}}, "extraction_metadata": {"type": "object", "required": ["total_terms", "source_length", "language", "domain_indicators"], "properties": {"language": {"type": "string"}, "total_terms": {"type": "integer"}, "source_length": {"type": "integer"}, "domain_indicators": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}}, "additionalProperties": false}}, "additionalProperties": false}, "strict": true, "description": null}, "verbosity": "medium"}, "user": null, "error": null, "model": "gpt-4.1-2025-04-14", "store": true, "tools": [], "top_p": 1.0, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2048, "total_tokens": 3676, "output_tokens": 1628, "input_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 0}}, "object": "response", "output": [{"id": "msg_689a6a84f1b8819082e5fd60237701fc0905d932a0e2aef8", "role": "assistant", "type": "message", "status": "completed", "content": [{"text": "{\\n  \\"extracted_terms\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\"canonical_term\\": \\"Arctic strategy\\",\\n      \\"surface_forms\\": [\\"Arctic strategy\\", \\"Arctic strategies\\", \\"national Arctic strategies\\", \\"Arctic policy\\", \\"Arctic policies\\"],\\n      \\"negative_surface_forms\\": [\\"Antarctic strategy\\", \\"tropical strategy\\"],\\n      \\"canonical_description\\": \\"A formal plan or set of policies developed by a state or organization to address interests, priorities, and actions in the Arctic region.\\",\\n      \\"term_type\\": \\"concept\\",\\n      \\"confidence\\": 0.98\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"canonical_term\\": \\"Arctic region\\",\\n      \\"surface_forms\\": [\\"Arctic region\\", \\"the Arctic\\", \\"Arctic\\", \\"Arctic Circle\\", \\"Arctic Ocean\\"],\\n      \\"negative_surface_forms\\": [\\"Antarctic region\\", \\"tropics\\"],\\n      \\"canonical_description\\": \\"The area surrounding the North Pole, including the Arctic Ocean and parts of countries within the Arctic Circle, characterized by extreme cold and unique ecosystems.\\",\\n      \\"term_type\\": \\"place\\",\\n      \\"confidence\\": 0.99\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"canonical_term\\": \\"Northern Sea Route\\",\\n      \\"surface_forms\\": [\\"Northern Sea Route\\", \\"NSR\\", \\"Arctic routes\\"],\\n      \\"negative_surface_forms\\": [\\"Suez Canal Route\\", \\"Panama Canal\\"],\\n      \\"canonical_description\\": \\"A shipping lane along the Russian Arctic coast from the Kara Sea to the Bering Strait, offering a shorter route between Europe and Asia.\\",\\n      \\"term_type\\": \\"entity\\",\\n      \\"confidence\\": 0.97\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"canonical_term\\": \\"Suez Canal Route\\",\\n      \\"surface_forms\\": [\\"Suez Canal Route\\", \\"SCR\\"],\\n      \\"negative_surface_forms\\": [\\"Northern Sea Route\\", \\"Panama Canal\\"],\\n      \\"canonical_description\\": \\"The traditional maritime shipping route connecting Europe and Asia via the Suez Canal.\\",\\n      \\"term_type\\": \\"entity\\",\\n      \\"confidence\\": 0.95\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"canonical_term\\": \\"grounded theory\\",\\n      \\"surface_forms\\": [\\"grounded theory\\"],\\n      \\"negative_surface_forms\\": [\\"thematic analysis\\", \\"case study\\"],\\n      \\"canonical_description\\": \\"A qualitative research methodology that involves constructing theories through methodical gathering and analysis of data.\\",\\n      \\"term_type\\": \\"concept\\",\\n      \\"confidence\\": 0.96\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\"canonical_term\\": \\"difference-in-difference method\\",\\n      \\"surface_forms\\": [\\"difference-in-difference method\\", \\"DID method\\"],\\n      \\"negative_surface_forms\\": [\\"regression analysis\\", \\"correlation analysis\\"],\\n      \\"canonical_description\\": \\"A statistical technique used to estimate causal relationships by comparing the changes in outcomes over time between a trea