34	OpenaiApiCall	ApiTracking::trackedApiClient	responses.create	gpt-4.1-2025-04-14	gpt-4.1-2025-04-14	{"args": [], "kwargs": {"text": "Lexicon::TermExtractionResult", "input": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a lexicon extraction specialist for the Enliterator system.\\nYour task is to extract canonical terms from content and generate:\\n1. Canonical terms (normalized, proper casing)\\n2. Surface forms (aliases, alternate spellings, abbreviations)\\n3. Negative surface forms (common confusions, things this is NOT)\\n4. Canonical descriptions (neutral, factual, 1-2 lines)\\n\\nFocus on domain-specific terms, proper nouns, concepts, and technical vocabulary.\\nFor each term, provide multiple surface forms if they exist in the text or are commonly used.\\nCanonical descriptions should be informative but neutral in tone.\\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Extract canonical terms, surface forms, and descriptions from the following content:\\n\\nDefense & Security Analysis\\n\\nISSN: (Print) (Online) Journal homepage: www.tandfonline.com/journals/cdan20\\n\\nRussia’s Arctic icebreaking capability: an assessment\\n\\nNurlan Aliyev\\n\\nTo cite this article: Nurlan Aliyev (2024) Russia’s Arctic icebreaking capability: an assessment,\\nDefense & Security Analysis, 40:3, 405-429, DOI: 10.1080/14751798.2024.2365542\\n\\nTo link to this article:  https://doi.org/10.1080/14751798.2024.2365542\\n\\nPublished online: 23 Jul 2024.\\n\\nSubmit your article to this journal\\n\\nArticle views: 271\\n\\nView related articles\\n\\nView Crossmark data\\n\\nFull Terms & Conditions of access and use can be found at\\nhttps://www.tandfonline.com/action/journalInformation?journalCode=cdan20\\n\\n\\fDEFENSE & SECURITY ANALYSIS\\n2024, VOL. 40, NO. 3, 405–429\\nhttps://doi.org/10.1080/14751798.2024.2365542\\n\\nRussia’s Arctic icebreaking capability: an assessment\\nNurlan Aliyev\\n\\nFaculty of Social Science, University of Economics and Human Sciences in Warsaw, Warsaw, Poland\\n\\nKEYWORDS\\nIcebreakers; Arctic; Russia’s\\nArctic strategy; dual -use\\ncapabilities; navy; civilian\\nicebreakers\\n\\nABSTRACT\\nThis paper explores the Russian icebreaker ﬂeet capabilities,\\nproblems, and perspectives. It also analyses Russian civilian and\\nmilitary icebreakers, and their dual-use capabilities. The paper\\nresponds to a question grounded in Russia’s Arctic policy: How\\ncritical is icebreaking capability to Russia’s Arctic strategy? Based\\non the ﬁndings, the paper concludes that the inﬂuences of\\nclimate change and Russia’s foreign and external security policy\\naﬀect the development of its icebreaker ﬂeet in the Arctic. In this\\nrespect, temperatures of the ice melting in the Arctic and delays\\nin building new icebreakers, because of access to the technology\\ncaused by the sanctions, are stressed. On the other hand, Russia’s\\nconfrontation with the West following the invasion of Ukraine\\nand the sanctions over it, increases the allocation of government\\nﬁnance to support local shipbuilding companies. Moreover, the\\npaper argues that Russia’s economic diﬃculties and shipbuilding\\ndelays should enhance the dual-use capabilities of the Russian\\nicebreakers in the coming years.\\n\\nSince Soviet times the Russian icebreaker ﬂeet has been described, both in and outside of\\nRussia, as one of the main factors of Russia’s strength in the Arctic. Interest in Russia’s\\nicebreaker ﬂeet has grown since the 2010s again – especially in the last few years – as\\nMoscow’s economic and security involvement in the region has increased. Moreover,\\nRussia’s 2022 invasion of Ukraine would further aﬀect the Russian economic projects\\nin the Arctic, including the Northern Sea Route and its icebreaker ﬂeet. But amidst\\nthe deterioration of the Russian economy, the dual-use capabilities of the icebreakers\\nmay increase. Concerns regarding Russia’s power projection into the Arctic and\\nTrans-Atlantic, worries about Russia’s purported intentions to control the Northern\\nSea Route, makes it necessary to develop a better understanding of the Russian icebreaker\\nﬂeet’s capabilities and intention.\\n\\nIn this respect, this paper aims to explore the Russian icebreaker ﬂeet capabilities,\\nproblems, and perspectives on its development. The paper explores the use of icebreakers\\nby the Russian military and also, how civilian icebreakers and infrastructure are exploited\\nby the military. The paper identiﬁes research questions grounded in Russia’s Arctic\\npolicy: How critical is its icebreaking capability to Russia’s Arctic strategy? Based on\\nthe paper’s ﬁndings, it is clear that the inﬂuences of climate change and Russia’s\\nforeign and external security policy aﬀect the development of its icebreaker ﬂeet in the\\n\\nCONTACT Nurlan Aliyev\\n© 2024 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group\\n\\naliyevnurlan13@gmail.com\\n\\n@anurlan\\n\\n\\f406\\n\\nN. ALIYEV\\n\\nArctic. On the other hand, Russia’s confrontation with the West, with its invasion of\\nUkraine and the sanctions over it, will force an increase in government ﬁnance to dom-\\nestic shipbuilding companies. Furthermore, Russia’s economic diﬃculties and shipbuild-\\ning delays may enhance the dual-use capabilities of old and new Russian icebreakers in\\nthe coming years.\\n\\nIn methodological terms, the paper employs qualitative methods. The research uses\\ncontent analysis regarding oﬃcial documents, political and military elite speeches, inter-\\nviews, and reports to explain Russia’s oﬃcial positions and policies regarding both the\\nmilitary and civilian icebreaker ﬂeets, and their use in the Arctic. Development of the ice-\\nbreakers, their problems, eﬀects of the invasion of Ukraine on the construction of ice-\\nbreakers, and their dual-use capabilities are analysed in this paper. Primary and\\nsecondary sources are used to analyse developments regarding the Russian icebreakers\\nin the Arctic. Interviews and reports from Russian and Western media are also used.\\nThe paper uses oﬃcial information, reports, and interviews.\\n\\nHistory\\n\\nNo nation is interested in icebreakers as much as Russia. (Vice Admiral Stepan Osipovich\\nMakarov)1\\nSince the end of the nineteenth century when the ﬁrst Russian icebreaker “Yermak”\\nwas built, icebreakers have been an essential part of Russia’s Arctic strategy.2 In 1916,\\nthe ﬁrst non-freezing port in the Arctic was founded: Romanov-on-Murman (the\\npresent name is Murmansk). By 1915, the ﬁrst Russian icebreaker shipbuilding pro-\\ngramme was created.3 The ﬁrst Russian icebreakers, including Yermak, were built in\\nEnglish shipyards. In 1932, the USSR established the Main Directorate for the Northern\\nSea Route, which took over the order of new icebreakers.4 From 1938–1941, in Leningrad\\nand Nikolaev, the ﬁrst four Arctic icebreakers (coal-ﬁred steam icebreakers) in the USSR\\nwere built with the lead ship “I. Stalin” (the other three were the “V. Molotov,” “L. Kaga-\\nnovich” and “A. Mikoyan”). The planned creation of domestic diesel-electric icebreakers\\nin the late 1930s was postponed.5 They were supplied later from the USA under the Lend-\\nLease programme. Three icebreakers, “Southwind,” “Northwind,” and “Westwind” were\\ntransferred to the USSR in 1944 and early 1945.6 Under the names “Admiral Makarov,”\\n“Captain Belousov,” and “North Pole” they operated under the ﬂag of the USSR for\\nseveral years and were returned to the United States in 1955. Thus, American vessels\\nwere the ﬁrst diesel-electric icebreakers in the Soviet Union. The ﬁrst Soviet diesel-elec-\\ntric icebreaker “Moskva” was built in Finland in 1960.7 Soviet-Finnish co-operation in\\nshipbuilding began in 1932. The serial construction of icebreakers for the Soviet\\nUnion began in 1952, when a Helsinki shipyard received orders for two sea icebreakers\\nof 10,500 hp each. The peak of icebreaker construction for the USSR occurred from 1950\\nto the 1980s.8 Since the mid-1970s, 21 icebreakers of projects 1101, 1105, 1108 and 1191\\nwere built in Finland for the USSR, and 20 of them are still in service.9\\n\\nAfter WW2, the USSR was actively engaged in the development of atomic energy.\\nFirstly, for military purposes, to create nuclear weapons, but simultaneously the propo-\\nsals on peaceful use of nuclear energy were received with the utmost interest by the\\nSoviets. This resulted in the ﬁrst nuclear-powered icebreaker in the world, the Lenin,\\n\\n\\fDEFENSE & SECURITY ANALYSIS\\n\\n407\\n\\nbeing launched in 1959.10 It was not only an indicator of achievement of the Soviets’\\nscience and industrial capabilities; it was also promoted by the Communist leadership\\nas a symbol of achievement of Communism. Such approaches by the elite regarding\\nnuclear-powered icebreakers can still be found in contemporary Russia. In the 1990s,\\nthe icebreaker ﬂeet was strongly aﬀected by the economic crisis and a generally dimin-\\nished focus on the Arctic by Moscow. The maximum number of the Russian icebreakers\\nserviced in the Arctic, both nuclear and diesel-electric, reached 18 in 1989, but in 2002\\nthere were 12 operational icebreakers (7 nuclear and 5 diesel-electric icebreakers).11\\nHowever, since the mid of 2000s the situa..."}], "model": "gpt-4.1", "temperature": 0.0}}	{"id": "resp_689a6b12e6548193a0d9598bfc9c3449009901945f196216", "text": {"format_": {"name": "TermExtractionResult", "type": "json_schema", "schema": {"type": "object", "required": ["extracted_terms", "extraction_metadata"], "properties": {"extracted_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "object", "required": ["canonical_term", "surface_forms", "negative_surface_forms", "canonical_description", "term_type", "confidence"], "properties": {"term_type": {"enum": ["concept", "entity", "place", "event", "process", "attribute", "general"], "type": "string"}, "confidence": {"type": "number"}, "surface_forms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "canonical_term": {"ty