36	OpenaiApiCall	ApiTracking::trackedApiClient	responses.create	gpt-4.1-2025-04-14	gpt-4.1-2025-04-14	{"args": [], "kwargs": {"text": "Lexicon::TermExtractionResult", "input": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a lexicon extraction specialist for the Enliterator system.\\nYour task is to extract canonical terms from content and generate:\\n1. Canonical terms (normalized, proper casing)\\n2. Surface forms (aliases, alternate spellings, abbreviations)\\n3. Negative surface forms (common confusions, things this is NOT)\\n4. Canonical descriptions (neutral, factual, 1-2 lines)\\n\\nFocus on domain-specific terms, proper nouns, concepts, and technical vocabulary.\\nFor each term, provide multiple surface forms if they exist in the text or are commonly used.\\nCanonical descriptions should be informative but neutral in tone.\\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Extract canonical terms, surface forms, and descriptions from the following content:\\n\\nNORAD: Deepened China-Russia military cooperation\\nincreases threats to U.S. homeland\\nSherman, Jason . Sherman, Jason.\\n\\nProQuest document link\\n\\nFULL TEXT\\n\\nFebruary 18, 2025 at 2:40 PM\\n\\n The likelihood of direct military conflict between the U.S. and its top adversaries is growing, fueled by deepening\\n\\nstrategic cooperation between China, Russia, North Korea and Iran, according to the head of the North American\\n\\nAerospace Defense Command (NORAD) and U.S. Northern Command (NORTHCOM).\\n\\nAir Force Gen. Gregory Guillot warned that these adversaries are expanding their military capabilities and\\n\\nstrengthening ties in ways that directly threaten the U.S. homeland in Feb. 13 written testimony prepared for the\\n\\nSenate Armed Services Committee. He described a broad coordinated effort among America's adversaries that could\\n\\nescalate a regional conflict into a global one.\\n\\n\\"The likelihood of direct conflict between the United States and one of its four principal adversaries is increasing,\\"\\n\\nGuillot testified. \\"Strategic cooperation among these adversaries has grown substantially since the beginning of the\\n\\nUkraine war, increasing the risk that war with one adversary could quickly expand into war with an enemy coalition.\\"\\n\\nThe testimony underscores how U.S. defense officials are recalibrating their threat assessments to account for not\\n\\njust China's growing military power but also the potential for coordinated operations with Russia and North Korea.\\n\\nThe shift marks a significant evolution in strategic concerns as adversaries that have historically acted independently\\n\\nare now leveraging each other's military assets and expertise to challenge the U.S. and its allies.\\n\\nOne of the major developments highlighted by Guillot is the closer military collaboration between China and Russia.\\n\\nIn the summer of 2024, he said China deployed bomber aircraft to a Russian Arctic airbase for the first time and\\n\\nconducted a joint bomber patrol over the Bering Sea alongside Russian heavy bombers.\\n\\n\\"Such 'access transfer' accelerates and extends China's ability to threaten North America in the air domain and raises\\n\\nthe specter of coordinated military operations in the event of a strategic conflict,\\" Guillot testified.\\n\\nThe move represents a shift in China's military posture, he said. While Beijing has historically focused its military\\n\\nmodernization on regional conflicts, its expanded presence in the Arctic and North Pacific suggests a broader\\n\\nstrategic intent. If China gains routine access to Russian airbases and naval ports in the Arctic, it could significantly\\n\\nshorten the time required to launch strikes against North American targets, according to the four-star general.\\n\\nMeanwhile, North Korea has also demonstrated its willingness to support Russia's war in Ukraine, with Pyongyang\\n\\nreportedly supplying military personnel and munitions to Moscow. Guillot cautioned this growing partnership raises\\n\\nconcerns about potential technology transfers that could accelerate North Korea's weapons development --\\n\\nparticularly its strategic missile and cyber capabilities.\\n\\nWhile the Pentagon has long tracked China's nuclear modernization, Guillot's testimony pointed to an increasing\\n\\nthreat from conventional Chinese weapon systems that can strike U.S. territory. Over the past year, China has:\\n\\n* Deployed warships to the Bering Sea for the fourth consecutive year, operating within cruise missile range of critical\\n\\ninfrastructure in Alaska.\\n\\n* Expanded its fleet of Shang III-class nuclear-powered guided-missile submarines (SSGNs) which in the coming years\\n\\ncould provide Beijing a clandestine land-attack capability against the U.S. West Coast.\\n\\n* Integrated air-refuellable H-6N bombers into joint Sino-Russian bomber patrols extending China's ability to conduct\\n\\nlong-range strikes without needing forward bases.\\n\\n\\f* Developed conventionally armed intercontinental ballistic missiles (ICBMs) which could allow China to strike U.S.\\n\\nterritory without crossing the nuclear threshold.\\n\\nGuillot emphasized China's advancements in these areas \\"increase the likelihood that an armed conflict would\\n\\ninclude direct strikes on the homeland.\\" The ability to hold U.S. infrastructure at risk with conventional weapons\\n\\nprovides Beijing with an escalation option that falls between cyberattacks and nuclear strikes, complicating U.S.\\n\\nstrategic decision-making.\\n\\nChina's development of new nuclear delivery systems also poses fresh challenges for U.S. early warning and missile\\n\\ndefense systems. According to Guillot, China is refining its ability to field nuclear-armed ICBMs equipped with\\n\\nhypersonic glide vehicles (HGVs) or capable of following a fractional orbital bombardment system (FOBS) trajectory.\\n\\nThese systems which can approach North America from the south rather than the traditional northern vector are\\n\\ndesigned to evade U.S. early warning radars.\\n\\n\\"Such systems, if fielded, would erode strategic stability by degrading our ability to provide granular and actionable\\n\\nwarning of an inbound attack,\\" Guillot warned.\\n\\nDETAILS\\n\\nSubject:\\n\\nLocation:\\n\\nTestimony; Modernization; Missiles; Cooperation\\n\\nNorth Korea; China; Beijing China; Russia; Bering Sea; United States--US; Arctic region;\\n\\nUkraine; North America\\n\\nPublication title:\\n\\nInsideDefense.com's SitRep; Arlington\\n\\nPublication year:\\n\\n2025\\n\\nPublication date:\\n\\nFeb 19, 2025\\n\\nPublisher:\\n\\nInside Washington Publishers\\n\\nPlace of publication:\\n\\nArlington\\n\\nCountry of publication:\\n\\nUnited States, Arlington\\n\\nPublication subject:\\n\\nMilitary\\n\\nSource type:\\n\\nTrade Journal\\n\\nLanguage of publication:\\n\\nEnglish\\n\\nDocument type:\\n\\nNews\\n\\nProQuest document ID:\\n\\n3168296968\\n\\nDocument URL:\\n\\nhttps://nps.idm.oclc.org/login?url=https://www.proquest.com/trade-journals/norad-\\n\\ndeepened-china-russia-military-cooperation/docview/3168296968/se-\\n\\n2?accountid=12702\\n\\nCopyright:\\n\\nCopyright Inside Washington Publishers Feb 19, 2025\\n\\n\\fFull text availability:\\n\\nThis publication may be subject to restrictions within certain markets, including\\n\\ncorporations, non-profits, government institutions, and public libraries. In those cases\\n\\nrecords will be visible to users, but not full text.\\n\\nLast updated:\\n\\n2025-02-19\\n\\nDatabase:\\n\\nAdvanced Technologies &Computer Science Collection,ProQuest Central\\n\\nLINKS\\nLinking Service\\n\\nDatabase copyright (cid:211) 2025 ProQuest LLC. All rights reserved.\\n\\nTerms and Conditions   Contact ProQuest\\n\\n\\n"}], "model": "gpt-4.1", "temperature": 0.0}}	{"id": "resp_689a6b24e498819db30a2f2a6c90ea7d0f74430fadb584db", "text": {"format_": {"name": "TermExtractionResult", "type": "json_schema", "schema": {"type": "object", "required": ["extracted_t