sed Baltic\n\nconnector pipeline damage. Retrieved from https://yle.fi/a/74-20056827\n\nZhang, Z., & Liu, H. (2021). Current New Tasks and Topics in the Interdisciplinary Training of International\n\nLaw Talents – Reflections Based on “Strategic New Frontiers” such as Deep Sea, Polar Regions, Outer\nSpace, and Cyber. [当前国际法跨学科人才培养的新任务新课题——基于深海、极地、外空、网络等'战\n略新疆域'的思考]. Xueshu Qianyan, 61(5).\n\nZhao, X. (2020, May 12). Scientific Understanding of the New Domains. [科学认知新型领域]. Zhongguo\n\nJunwang. Retrieved from http://www.81.cn/theory/2020-05/12/content_9811642.htm\n\nZhou, J. (2019). China’s Core Interests and Dilemma in Foreign Policy Practice. Pacific Focus, 34(1), 31–54.\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1111/pafo.12131\n\nTO CITE THIS ARTICLE:\nPuranen, M., & Kopra, S.\n(2023). China’s Arctic Strategy\n– a Comprehensive Approach\nin Times of Great Power\nRivalry. Scandinavian Journal\nof Military Studies, 6(1), pp.\n239–253. DOI: https://doi.\norg/10.31374/sjms.196\n\nSubmitted: 10 January 2023\nAccepted: 04 December 2023\nPublished: 26 December 2023\n\nCOPYRIGHT:\n© 2023 The Author(s). This is an\nopen-access article distributed\nunder the terms of the Creative\nCommons Attribution 4.0\nInternational License (CC-BY\n4.0), which permits unrestricted\nuse, distribution, and\nreproduction in any medium,\nprovided the original author\nand source are credited. See\nhttp://creativecommons.org/\nlicenses/by/4.0/.\n\nScandinavian Journal of\nMilitary Studies is a peer-\nreviewed open access journal\npublished by Scandinavian\nMilitary Studies.\n\nPuranen and Kopra  Scandinavian Journal of Military Studies  DOI: 10.31374/sjms.196\n	skipped	{"skipped_at": "2025-08-11T22:38:35.102Z", "skip_reason": "All 15 terms were duplicates of already-processed terms"}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	4583cf4cc4339d04b7053b8beb543a21c57d528977a811f0ba6bab1a568fdeb6	\N	markitdown	gpt-4.1	tier_1	17732	70928	{"routing_info":{"status":"tier_1","model":"gpt-4.1","estimated_tokens":17732,"limit":380000,"reason":"Content fits in Tier 1 model (gpt-4.1)","cost_tier":"standard"},"original_metadata":{"service":"Ingest::TokenAwareMarkitdownService","file_path":"/Volumes/jer4TBv3/enliterator/arctic_research/China's Arctic Strategy_Puranen.pdf","file_size_bytes":1186370,"content_length_chars":70928,"estimated_tokens":17732,"timestamp":"2025-08-11T22:07:12Z","supported_formats":[".pdf",".docx",".pptx",".xlsx",".md",".txt",".html",".rtf",".odt",".jpg",".jpeg",".png",".bmp",".gif",".svg"]},"detection_result":{"media_type":"document","markitdown_supported":true,"mime_type":"application/pdf","file_extension":".pdf"},"processed_at":"2025-08-11T22:07:12Z"}
6	1	\N	\N	\N	3b8e5e83fdec3cdc56660e8af9d30b7feca6ebcf8c93cde8262cf449e5b6bf40	/Volumes/jer4TBv3/enliterator/arctic_research/China's Arctic Strategy_Qingchao.pdf	\N	text	completed	338727	CHAPTER  7\n\nChina’s  Arctic  Strategy:  From  “Follow-Up”\nto  “Creative  Involvement”?\n\nXu  Qingchao\n\nIntroduction\n\nA  voice  in  the  West  believes  that  “for  the  Arctic,  China  seeks  to  pursue\nan  economic  development  strategy  for  the  region  that  requires  that  the\nArctic  be  open  to  Chinese  development  and  that  China  is  given  equal\nstanding  to  other  Arctic  nations.  This  is  the  win-win  strategy  that  Beijing\nis  currently  implementing  in  the  Arctic”  (Conley,  2018).  China’s  Arctic\nPolicy  White  Paper  was  published  in  January  2018,  which  “caused  much\nconsternation  internationally,  as  it  deﬁned  China  as  a  ‘near-Arctic  state’\nand  presented  the  idea  of  a  ‘Polar  Silk  Road’  (PSR)  linked  to  the  Belt\nand  Road  Initiative  (BRI)”  (Klimenko,  2018),  allowing  journalists  to\ndevelop  a  narrative  about  the  country’s  interest  and  actions  in  the  polar\nsphere  (Moscato,  2018)  “The  release  of  the  White  Paper  may  have  cleared\nsome  misconceptions  about  China’s  Arctic  interest,  but  it  lacks  concrete\n\nX.  Qingchao  (B)\nUniversity  of  Chinese  Academy  of  Sciences,  Beijing,  China\ne-mail:  xuqingchao@ucas.ac.cn\n\n©  The  Author(s),  under  exclusive  license  to  Springer  Nature\nSwitzerland  AG  2024\nP.  Midford  et  al.  (eds.),  Navigating  East  Asian  Maritime  Conﬂicts:\nTechnological  Change,  Environmental  Challenges,  Global  and  Regional\nResponses,  Palgrave  Studies  in  Maritime  Politics  and  Security,\nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-51989-5_7\n\n151\n\n\f152\n\nX. QINGCHAO\n\nsteps  or  measures  that  China  intends  to  implement  in  its  Arctic  policy”\n(Lim,  2018).  However,  “much  remains  uncertain  on  aspects  of  China’s\nkey  interests  that  are  not  addressed  in  the  White  Paper,  in  particular  on\nthe  role  of  the  Arctic  in  its  national  security  and  defence  strategy”  (Lim,\n2018).\n\nIn  accordance  with  positioning  China  as  a  “strategic  rival”  of  the\nUnited  States,  a  provocative  speech  by  US  Secretary  of  State  Mike\nPompeo  of  the  Trump  administration  argued  that  “We’re  entering  a  new\nage  of  strategic  engagement  in  the  Arctic  …  There  are  only  Arctic  States\nand  Non-Arctic  States.  No  third  category  exists,  and  claiming  other-\nwise  entitles  China  to  exactly  nothing”  (Pompeo,  2019).  In  response\nto  this  view,  some  critics  argued  that,  “the  key  to  U.S.  policy  toward\nChina’s  Arctic  inﬂuence  and  activities  should  begin  with  solid  assessment\nof  China’s  concrete  capabilities  instead  of  speculations  about  its  inten-\ntions  and  potential”  (Sun,  2020).  It  is  worth  noting  that  in  recent  years\nneither  China’s  “Near-Arctic  State”  self-identity  in  the  Arctic  nor  the\nPSR  conﬁrmed  in  China’s  Arctic  Policy  White  Paper  have  been  further\nimplemented  in  paper  or  in  practice.\n\nIn  this  context,  a  question  is  raised  here:  Is  there  any  change  occur-\nring  in  China’s  policy  toward  the  changing  Arctic?  This  study  aims  to\nexamine  the  emerging  shift  of  China’s  Arctic  policy  from  the  past  to\nthe  future.  For  this  purpose,\nit  outlines  China’s  “follow-up”  strategy\nfor  the  Arctic  from  1999  to  2019  ﬁrst,  then  it  identiﬁes  consequential\ngeo-strategic  dates  for  the  Arctic  in  the  twenty-ﬁrst  century.  Prospec-\ntive  scenarios  of  Arctic  security  dilemmas  involving  China  and  potential\noptions  available  for  China  are  highlighted\nin  the  following  section.\nFinally,  taking  the  practice  of  China’s  climate  diplomacy  into  account,  it\ntries  to  explain  China’s  emerging  alternative  Arctic  strategy  characterized\nby  “creative  involvement.”\n\nChina’s  “Follow-Up”  Strategy\nfor  the  Arctic  from  1999  to  2019\n\nincreased  visibly  and  substantially,  particularly\n\nSince  the  end  of  the  twentieth  century,  Chinese  presence  in  the  Arctic\nhas\nits  scientiﬁc  and\neconomic  activities,  taking  advantage  of  the  rights  and  interests  granted\nby  the  Svalbard  Treaty.  In  2020,  a  turning  point  for  China’s  Arctic\nengagement  appeared  due  to  increased  uncertainties  in  the  Arctic  geo-\nis  very\nstrategic  environment\n\nin  the  context  of  COVID-19.  Thus,\n\nin\n\nit\n\n\f7 CHINA’S ARCTIC STRATEGY: FROM “FOLLOW-UP” … 153\n\nhelpful  to  take  China’s  Arctic  involvement  from  1999  to  2019  as  a  distinct\nperiod  to  view  China’s  Arctic  policy  in  practice.\n\nDuring  this  period,  milestones  in  China’s  Arctic  involvement  include:\n(1)  From  1999  to  the  end  of  October  2019,  China  conducted  10  Arctic\nexpeditions  in  total;  (2)  In  2004,  China’s  ﬁrst  Arctic  observation  station,\nthe  Yellow  River  Station,  was  established  in  Svalbard;  (3)  In  2013,  China\ngained  permanent  observer  status  in  the  Arctic  Council;  (4)  In  2015,  the\nArctic  (originally  “polar  regions”  in	{"filename": "China's Arctic Strategy_Qingchao.pdf", "file_size": 338727, "document_type": "strategic_document"}	{}	\N	2025-08-11 21:46:06.803076	2025-08-11 22:38:35.103456	extracted	{"terms_count": 16, "extracted_at": "2025-08-11T22:10:40.560Z"}	CHAPTER  7\n\nChina’s  Arctic  Strategy:  From  “Follow-Up”\nto  “Creative  Involvement”?\n\nXu  Qingchao\n\nIntroduction\n\nA  voice  in  the  West  believes  that  “for  the  Arctic,  China  seeks  to  pursue\nan  economic  development  strategy  for  the  region  that  requires  that  the\nArctic  be  open  to  Chinese  development  and  that  China  is  given  equal\nstanding  to  other  Arctic  nations.  This  is  the  win-win  strategy  that  Beijing\nis  currently  implementing  in  the  Arctic”  (Conley,  2018).  China’s  Arctic\nPolicy  White  Paper  was  published  in  January  2018,  which  “caused  much\nconsternation  internationally,  as  it  deﬁned  China  as  a  ‘near-Arctic  state’\nand  presented  the  idea  of  a  ‘Polar  Silk  Road’  (PSR)  linked  to  the  Belt\nand  Road  Initiative  (BRI)”  (Klimenko,  2018),  allowing  journalists  to\ndevelop  a  narrative  about  the  country’s  interest  and  actions  in  the  polar\nsphere  (Moscato,  2018)  “The  release  of  the  White  Paper  may  have  cleared\nsome  misconceptions  about  China’s  Arctic  interest,  but  it  lacks  concrete\n\nX.  Qingchao  (B)\nUniversity  of  Chinese  Academy  of  Sciences,  Beijing,  China\ne-mail:  xuqingchao@ucas.ac.cn\n\n©  The  Author(s),  under  exclusive  license  to  Springer  Nature\nSwitzerland  AG  2024\nP.  Midford  et  al.  (eds.),  Navigating  East  Asian  Maritime  Conﬂicts:\nTechnological  Change,  Environmental  Challenges,  Global  and  Regional\nResponses,  Palgrave  Studies  in  Maritime  Politics  and  Security,\nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-51989-5_7\n\n151\n\n\f152\n\nX. QINGCHAO\n\nsteps  or  measures  that  China  intends  to  implement  in  its  Arctic  policy”\n(Lim,  2018).  However,  “much  remains  uncertain  on  aspects  of  China’s\nkey  interests  that  are  not  addressed  in  the  White  Paper,  in  particular  on\nthe  role  of  the  Arctic  in  its  national  security  and  defence  strategy”  (Lim,\n2018).\n\nIn  accordance  with  positioning  China  as  a  “strategic  rival”  of  the\nUnited  States,  a  provocative  speech  by  US  Secretary  of  State  Mike\nPompeo  of  the  Trump  administration  argued  that  “We’re  entering  a  new\nage  of  strategic  engagement  in  the  Arctic  …  There  are  only  Arctic  States\nand  Non-Arctic  States.  No  third  category  exists,  and  claiming  other-\nwise  entitles  China  to  exactly  nothing”  (Pompeo,  2019).  In  response\nto  this  view,  some  critics  argued  that,  “the  key  to  U.S.  policy  toward\nChina’s  Arctic  inﬂuence  and  activities  should  begin  with  solid  assessment\nof  China’s  concrete  capabilities  instead  of  speculations  about  its  inten-\ntions  and  potential”  (Sun,  2020).  It  is  worth  noting  that  in  recent  years\nneither  China’s  “Near-Arctic  State”  self-identity  in  the  Arctic  nor  the\nPSR  conﬁrmed  in  China’s  Arctic  Policy  White  Paper  have  been  further\nimplemented  in  paper  or  in  practice.\n\nIn  this  context,  a  question  is  raised  here:  Is  there  any  change  occur-\nring  in  China’s  policy  toward  the  changing  Arctic?  This  study  aims  to\nexamine  the  emerging  shift  of  China’s  Arctic  policy  from  the  past  to\nthe  future.  For  this  purpose,\nit  outlines  China’s  “follow-up”  strategy\nfor  the  Arctic  from  1999  to  2019  ﬁrst,  then  it  identiﬁes  consequential\ngeo-strategic  dates  for  the  Arctic  in  the  twenty-ﬁrst  century.  Prospec-\ntive  scenarios  of  Arctic  security  dilemmas  involving  China  and  potential\noptions  available  for  China  are  highlighted\nin  the  following  section.\nFinally,  taking  the  practice  of  China’s  climate  diplomacy  into  account,  it\ntries  to  explain  China’s  emerging  alternative  Arctic  strategy  characterized\nby  “creative  involvement.”\n\nChina’s  “Follow-Up”  Strategy\nfor  the  Arctic  from  1999  to  2019\n\nincreased  visibly  and  substantially,  particularly\n\nSince  the  end  of  the  twentieth  century,  Chinese  presence  in  the  Arctic\nhas\nits  scientiﬁc  and\neconomic  activities,  taking  advantage  of  the  rights  and  interests  granted\nby  the  Svalbard  Treaty.  In  2020,  a  turning  point  for  China’s  Arctic\nengagement  appeared  due  to  increased  uncertainties  in  the  Arctic  geo-\nis  very\nstrategic  environment\n\nin  the  context  of  COVID-19.  Thus,\n\nin\n\nit\n\n\f7 CHINA’S ARCTIC STRATEGY: FROM “FOLLOW-UP” … 153\n\nhelpful  to  take  China’s  Arctic  involvement  from  1999  to  2019  as  a  distinct\nperiod  to  view  China’s  Arctic  policy  in  practice.\n\nDuring  this  period,  milestones  in  China’s  Arctic  involvement  include:\n(1)  From  1999  to  the  end  of  October  2019,  China  conducted  10  Arctic\nexpeditions  in  total;  (2)  In  2004,  China’s  ﬁrst  Arctic  observation  station,\nthe  Yellow  River  Station,  was  established  in  Svalbard;  (3)  In  2013,  China\ngained  permanent  observer  status  in  the  Arctic  Council;  (4)  In  2015,  the\nArctic  (originally  “polar  regions”  in  Chinese)  was  connected  for  the  ﬁrst\ntime  to  China’s  national  security,  in  Article  32  of  China’s  National  Secu-\nrity  Law;  (5)  In  January  2017,  Chinese  President  Xi  Jinping  positioned\nthe  polar  regions  (both  the  Arctic  and  Antarctic)  as  “new  frontiers”  along\nwith  the  deep  ocean,  outer  space  and  cyberspace;  (6)  Also  in  2017,  China\nand  Russia  proposed  the  concept  of  the  “Polar  Silk  Road”  (“Ice  Silk\nRoad”/“Arctic  Silk  Road”)  during  Chinese  President  Xi’s  ofﬁcial  visit\nto  Moscow,  connecting  China’s  BRI  with  Russia’s  Eurasian  Economic\nUnion  (EEU)  plan  through  bilateral  cooperation  in  the  Russian  Arctic\nregion;  (7)  In  January  2018,  China  published  China’s  Arctic  Policy  White\nPaper;  (8)  In  October  2018,  the  China–Iceland  Arctic  Research  Obser-\nvatory  (CIAO)  formally  opened;  (9)  and  in  2019,  China’s  ﬁrst  self-built\npolar  icebreaker  Xuelong  2  was  launched  to  boost  China’s  polar  research\nand  expedition  capabilities.\n\nBased  on  the  timeline  of  China’s  involvement  in  the  Arctic,  China’s\noverall  strategy  for  the  Arctic  can  be  deﬁned  as  a  “follow-up”  strategy.\nAccording  to  the  Modern  Chinese  Dictionary,  “follow-up”  (Genjin\nin\nChinese)  literally  has  four  meanings,  which  are:  follow  and  straightfor-\nward;  quote  or  imitate  others;  step-by-step;  and  move  forward  as  expected\nuntil  completion.  In  this  context,  China’s  “follow-up”  strategy  for  the\nArctic  refers  to  a  gradual,  step-by-step  and  non-coercive  approach  to\nArctic  affairs.  Within  its  capacity,  China  prioritizes  science  diplomacy  in\nthe  face  of  dramatic  climate  change  and  promotes  economic  and  trade\ncooperation  with  other  Arctic  stakeholders  for  common  development.\nChina’s  competitive  advantages  in  addressing  Arctic  affairs  vary  with  its\neconomic  development  and  so  does  its  “follow-up”  strategy.\n\nThe  “follow-up”  strategy  has  two  variants:  “passive  follow-up”  and\n“active  follow-up”.  Comparatively,  from  1999  to  2012,  China  adopted\na  modest  “passive  follow-up”  strategy  for  the  Arctic.  In  the  mid-1990s,\nIceland  attempted  to  persuade  China  to  be\nin  Arctic  affairs\nduring  a  special  visit  to  Beijing,  without  eliciting  expected  responses.\nThis  indicates  that  the  Arctic  was  not  on  China’s  agenda  at  that  time.\n\ninvolved\n\n\f154\n\nX. QINGCHAO\n\nHowever,  after  2013,  China  switched  to  an  “active  follow-up”  strategy\nafter  obtaining  its  observer  status  in  the  Arctic  Council.  In  international\nmedia,  China  has  been  portrayed  as  a  proactive,  assertive,  and  ambitious\nArctic  player.\n\nFrom  a  holistic  perspective,  China’s  “follow-up”  strategy  in  the  Arctic\nhas  been  closely  related  to  China’s  domestic  economic  development\nstrategy  and  its  diplomatic  transition  especially  since  2009,  resulting  from\ntwo  crucial  background  events.  One  is  the  international  ﬁnancial  crisis\nthat  broke  out  in  2008/2009,  and  the  other  is  China’s  economic  rise,\nwhich  was  highlighted\nin  2010  as  China  became  the  world’s  second\nbiggest  economy  after  the  United  States.  From  the  Chinese  perspective,  it\nis  high  time  to  pursue  high-quality  development  based  on  the  enormous\nwealth  accumulation  from  its  reform  and  opening-up  policy  pursued  since\n1979.  Accordingly,  China  adjusted  its  foreign  policy  in  order  to  adapt\nto  a  changing  international  environment.  Out  of  internal  demands  and\nexternal  stimulus,  and  despite  China’s  limited  capacity  for  Arctic  partic-\nipation  and  unfavorable  public  opinion  abroad,  China  determined  to  go\nNorth.\n\nicebreakers,\n\nCompared  with  the  United  States  and  Russia,  China’s  limited  capacity\nfor  Arctic  participation  makes  China  not  yet  a  “new  Polar  Power”  in  terms\nof  icebreakers,  research  stations,  submarines,  military  bases,  or  allies.  As  a\nreport  goes,  the  United  States  has  6  military  bases  in  the  Arctic  (Knick-\nmeyer,  2022).  Russia  is  said  to  have  19  military  bases  in  the  Arctic,  and\nthe  United  States  has  2  icebreakers  in  service  and  plans  6  new  icebreakers\n(Gilmour,  2018).  “Russia  ofﬁcially  has  46\nincluding  4\nnuclear-powered  units”,  and  “the  order  of  battle  of  the  Northern  Fleet\ncomprises  41  submarines”  (Boulègue,  2019).  Based  on  Wikipedia,  the\nUnited  States  has  3  research  stations,  while  Russia  has  9.  According  to  the\nArctic  Security  Brieﬁng  Papers  of  2018,  Russia  operates  up  to  12  nuclear\nballistic  missile  submarines  (SSBNs)  and  49  attack  submarines,  and  the\nUnited  States  has  14  SSBNs  not  patrolling  in  the  Arctic  and  operates  54\nnuclear-powered  attack  submarines  capable  of  operating  under  ice  with\nregular  forays  into  the  Arctic  (Ernie,  2018).  Regarding  allies,  Russia  has\nnone  in  the  Arctic  region,  but  at  the  time  of  this  writing  the  United\nStates  already  has  ﬁve  (Canada,  Norway,  Denmark,  Iceland,  and  Finland)\nwith  Sweden  recently  added  as  the  sixth;  While  China  has  2  icebreakers,\n2  research  stations,  no  submarines,  no  military  bases  and  no  allies  in  the\nArctic.  Thus  a  huge  and  stark  gap  exists  between  China  and  these  two\nArctic  states  regarding  Arctic  capacities.\n\n\f7 CHINA’S ARCTIC STRATEGY: FROM “FOLLOW-UP” … 155\n\nIn  practice,  China’s  “follow-up”  strategy  rests  on  three  pillars.  The  ﬁrst\npillar  is  science  diplomacy.  Through  scientiﬁc  expeditions  to  the  Arctic  and\nsincere  efforts  in  international  organizations,  like  the  International  Arctic\nScience  Committee  (IASC),  Chinese  scientists  are  playing  the  role  of  door\nopeners  to  make  China’s  science  diplomacy  a  success  in  the  Arctic.  China\nhas  made  great  progress  in  certain  disciplines  related  to  the  Arctic  over\nthe  past  two  decades.  For  instance,  Chinese  scientists  have  established\nthe  basic  theoretical  framework  in  cryosphere  science,  leading  the  writing\nof  the  fourth  and  the  ﬁfth  reports  by  the  Intergovernmental  Panel  on\nClimate  Change  (IPCC).  As  Nalan  Koc  from  Norway  articulated  before\nthe  start  of  the  Arctic  Frontiers  2020  to  the  Chinese  media,  “China  is\nan  important  contributor  to  the  Arctic  research  knowledge  base”  (Zhu,\n2020).\n\nThe  second  pillar  is  economic  diplomacy.  Chinese  economic  activities\nparticularly  related  to  the  energy  and  tourism  sectors  in  the  Arctic  have\nbeen  conducted  in  line  with  a  grand  blueprint.  Among  these,  robust\nSino-Russia  economic  cooperation  in  the  region  has  been  the  highlight.\nAccording  to  Sanna  Kopra,  in  June  2017  the  Arctic  was  incorporated\ninto  China’s  BRI  as  one  of  the  “blue  economic  passages”  and  China\nrenamed  Arctic  shipping  lanes  as  the  PSR.  “From  2005–2017,  China\nhas  invested  over  $1.4  trillion  in  the  economies  of  the  Arctic  nations\n(including  Finland  and  Sweden),  $89.2  billion  of  which  was  in  infras-\ntructure,  assets,  cooperative  agreements,  ﬁnancing  agreements,  or  other\nprojects  located  within  the  60  degrees  north  boundary  …”  (Rosen  and\nThuringer,  2017).  As  Guggenheim  Partners  observes,  “with  a  population\nof  more  than  12  million  people  and  an  annual  economy  of  more  than\nUS$450  billion,  the  Arctic  represents  one  of  the  last  great  economic  fron-\ntiers.  Today,  the  economies  of  the  Arctic  are  responsible  for  a  signiﬁcant\nshare  of  the  world’s  food,  minerals,  and  energy”  (Guggenheim  Partners,\n2016).\n\nThe  third  pillar  is  public  diplomacy.  As  part  of  China’s  public  diplo-\nmacy,  academic  exchanges  on  different  platforms  among  universities  and\ninstitutes  from  China  and  other  Arctic  stakeholders  are  booming.  Along\nwith  promoting  traditional  governmental  relations,  public  diplomacy  has\nbecome  an  alternative  for  China  to  strengthen  its  relations  with  Arctic\nstates  bilaterally  and  multilaterally.  For  instance,  “panda  diplomacy”  has\nbeen  considered  for  decades  to  be  effective  for  China  to  develop  closer\nrelations  with  the  outside  world.  However,  it  should  not  be  seen  as  a\npanacea  suitable  for  all  cases,  given  negative  feedback  from  some  Arctic\n\n\f156\n\nX. QINGCHAO\n\nstates.  Through  connecting  its  own  reindeer  Ewenki  people  in  the  North-\neast  of  China  with  cross-border  indigenous  peoples  in  the  Arctic,  China\nhas  both  new  opportunities  as  well  as  challenges  in  developing  its  public\ndiplomacy  in  the  Arctic  region  in  a  productive  manner.\n\nGeo-Strategically  Consequential  Dates\nfor  the  Arctic  in  the  Twenty-First  Century\n\nOver  the  past  decade,  there  were  two  consequential  geostrategic  dates  for\nthe  Arctic  (Conley  and  Melino,  2020):  One  was  May  28,  2008,  when  the\n“Arctic  Five”,  namely,  Russia,  Canada,  Norway,  Denmark  (Greenland),\nand  the  United  States,  signed  the  Ilulissat  Declaration,  which  reafﬁrmed\nthat  the  Arctic  would  be  governed  by  the  UN  Convention  on  the  Law\nof  the  Sea  (UNCLOS).  And  the  other  was  May  15,  2013,  when  China\nbecame  an  observer  to  the  Arctic  Council,  an  intergovernmental  forum\ndesigned  to  protect  the  Arctic  environment  and  to  explore  the  sustain-\nable  development  of  the  Arctic  region.  Since  then,  China,  a  self-declared\n“near-Arctic  state”,  has  substantially  accelerated  its  economic  and  scien-\nin  subsequent  years,\ntiﬁc  presence  across  the  circumpolar  Arctic,  and\nChina  started  to  collaborate  with  Russia  in  military  exercises  in  the  Arctic\n(Conley  and  Melino,  2020).  This  analysis  obviously  served  as  a  reﬂection\nof  the  reality  of  Arctic  geopolitics  in  the  past  several  years  due  to  their\nrecognition  of  great  power  competition  in  the  Arctic  region.  It  is  fair  to\nsay  that  the  importance  of  the  “Arctic  Five”  and  China  are  exaggerated.\nas  China  was  imaged  as  a  “near-peer  competitor”  of  the  United  States  in\nthe  Arctic.\n\nIn  this  sense,  as  a  revision  and  supplement  to  related  works,  four  geo-\nstrategically  consequential  dates  for  the  Arctic  need  to  be  mentioned  here\nin  order  to  elucidate  the  changing  Arctic  geopolitics.  Each  and  every\nbig  event  marked  a  turning  point  for  Arctic  geopolitics:  (1)  In  August\n2007,  Russians  planted  their  ﬂag  in  the  seabed  at  the  North  Pole  during\nArtur  Chilingarov’s  expedition;  (2)  In  March  2014,  when  the  Ukraine\ncrisis  broke  out  and  Crimea  was  annexed  to  Russia;  (3)  In  May  2019,\nas  mentioned  previously,  Pompeo  gave  a  speech  in  Finland,  launching\na  new  era  of  power  competition  in  the  Arctic  region;  (4)  In  February\n2022,  Russia  launched  its  self-deﬁned  “special  military  operation”  against\nUkraine.\n\nBig  Event  1:  On  August  2,  2007,  a  pair  of  Russian  submersibles\ndropped  4267  meters  to  the  bottom  of  the  Arctic  Ocean  and  planted  a\n\n\f7 CHINA’S ARCTIC STRATEGY: FROM “FOLLOW-UP” … 157\n\nRussian  ﬂag  at  the  North  Pole.  This  action  disturbed  the  overall  peaceful\nand  stable  geopolitical  pattern  in  the  Arctic  after  the  end  of  the  Cold  War\nand  provoked  strong  reactions  and  serious  concerns  from  other  Arctic\nstates.  A  deputy  spokesman  for  the  US  State  Department  responded\nthat  Russia’s  claim  to  Arctic  sovereignty  in  this  manner  had  no  legal\nforce.  Then  the  Canadian  Foreign  Minister  criticized  it  as  not  supporting\nRussia’s  territorial  claims  in  the  Arctic.  Arctic  states  such  as  Denmark  and\nNorway,  disapproving  of  Russia’s  approach,  responded  by  taking  concrete\nsteps  to  strengthen  their\njurisdiction  and  monitoring  of  their  Arctic\nterritories.  In  this  atmosphere,  Arctic  states  including  Canada,  Norway,\nDenmark,  and  the  United  States  submitted  their  boundary  claims  to  the\nUN  Commission  on  the  Limits  of  the  Continental  Shelf  (UNCLCS),\nalthough  the  United  States  had  never  ratiﬁed  the  UNCLOS.\n\nInterestingly,  the  reverberations  from  this  event  also  reached  China.\n“In  2007,  Chinese  strategic  thinkers  started  to  take  a  geopolitical  interest\nin  the  Arctic  after  Russian  explorers  planted  a  national  ﬂag  on  the  seabed\nat  the  North  Pole.  Since  then,  a  gradual  awakening  has  taken  place  among\nChinese  Government  ofﬁcials  and  social  science  researchers  regarding  the\nneed  to  prepare  for  the  day  when  most  of  the  Arctic  will  be  accessible,\nat  least  during  the  summer”  (Jakobson  and  Peng,  2012).  Meanwhile,\nin  the  ﬁeld  of  International  Relations,  the  ﬁrst  academic  article  on  the\nArctic  in  Chinese  was  also  published  in  2007,  focusing  on  the  “Arctic\nRace”  (Wu,  2007),  aroused  by  Russia’s  ﬂag-planting  event.  Considering\nthat  China  had  already  begun  applying  for  observer  status  in  the  Arctic\nCouncil,  the  reaction  of  Chinese  academic  and  policy  circles  at  the  time\nwas  not,  in  retrospect,  simply  a  coincidence.  Russia’s  ﬂag-planting  in  the\nArctic  Ocean  stimulated  the  interest  of  countries  outside  the  region  such\nas  China,  Japan,  South  Korea,  India,  and  Singapore,  and  the  eventual\nadmission  of  these  countries  simultaneously  into  the  Arctic  Council  in\nMay  2013,  clearly  reﬂected  geopolitical  considerations  as  well.\n\nBig  Event  2:  On  March  18,  2014,  the  former  Autonomous  Republic\nof  Crimea  of  Ukraine  was  annexed  to  the  Russian  Federation,  namely\nthe  “Crimean  Incident”.  As  a  response,  the  United  States  and  the  Euro-\npean  Union  simultaneously  announced  a  new  round  of  sanctions  against\nRussia.  The  deteriorating  relations  between  Russia,  the  United  States\nand  the  European  Union  intensiﬁed  international  speculation  about  the\nreturn  of  a  “new  Cold  War”.  In  the  long  run,  the  geopolitical  changes\ninduced  by  the  Crimean  Incident  are  closely  related  to  the  development\nand  general  evolution  of  the  global  geopolitical  pattern.  In  the  Arctic\n\n\f158\n\nX. QINGCHAO\n\nregion,  this  incident  and  the  subsequent  Western  sanctions  forced  Russia\nto  lose  the  US  and  European  markets  for  its  liqueﬁed  natural  gas  (LNG)\nprojects,  and  turn  to  the  East,  accelerating\nits  cooperation  with  East\nAsian  countries,  especially  with  China,  in  areas  including  the  utilization\nof  the  Northern  Sea  Route,  energy  exploration  and  related  infrastructure\nconstruction.  Among  these,  the  success  of  the  Yamal  project  is  regarded  as\nthe  biggest  merit  of  China–Russia  Arctic  cooperation.  For  Russia,  China\nis  the  best  partner  for  developing  Siberia  and  realizing  its  strategic  mission\nin  the  Far  East.  In  turn,  “in  a  very  short  time,  China  has  become  a  full-\nﬂedged  participant  in  the  economic  and  political  processes  in  the  Arctic”\n(Miheeva,  2019),  which  largely  owes  to  Russia’s  leverage.\n\nAlthough  the  two  countries  may  face  new  competition  with  respect  to\nthe  governance  of  the  Northern  Sea  Route  in  the  future,  it  is  undeni-\nable  that  China–Russia  cooperation  based  on  mutual  respect  in  the  Arctic\nregion  is  so  far  pragmatic  and  effective.  The  approaches  of  China  and\nRussia  in  the  Arctic  are  also  strong  geopolitical  stimuli  to  other  Arctic\nstakeholders.  China  and  Russia  are  both  now  considered  to  be  “strategic\ncompetitors”  that  pose  a  long-term  threat  to  the  United  States  in  the\nArctic.  The  Crimean  Incident  is  the  bond  between  China  and  Russia  for\ncloser  Arctic  cooperation  and  the  fuse  for  the  deterioration  of  relations\nbetween  Russia  and  the  West.  Compared  to  the  event  of  Russia’s  ﬂag-\nplanting  in  the  Arctic  in  2007,  the  Crimean  Incident  has  had  a  much  more\nprofound  impact  on  Arctic  geopolitics  as  it  demonstrated  that  military\nmight  was  revived  as  a  way  to  deal  with  disputes.\n\nBig  Event  3:  On  May  6,  2019,  Pompeo  gave  a  speech  in  Finland,\nchanging  the  long-standing  perception  of  the  Arctic  as  a  region  of  “high\nNorth,  low  tension”  after  the  end  of  the  Cold  War.  Inﬂuential  conse-\nquences  of  this  speech  are  reﬂected  not  only  in  the  shift  of  the  paradigm\nof  Arctic  security  research  but  also  in  the  position,  policy,  and  path  of\nthe  United  States  against  China  and  Russia\nits  national  strategies.\nBy  the  end  of  Trump’s  presidency,  the  US  government  was  actively\ntreating  China  as  a  perceived  enemy.  The  United  States  pressured  Nordic\nstates,  requiring  a  deﬁnition  of  “Chinese  threat  in  the  Arctic;”  this  led  to\nthe  Nordic  states  wavering  in  their  stance  on  China’s  Arctic  participation.\n“The  Faroe  Islands  have  turned  into  a  new  and  unlikely  battleground\nin  the  technological  Cold  War  between  the  United  States  and  China”\n(Satariano,  2019)  as  the  United  States  urged  the  archipelago  not  to\n\nin\n\n\f7 CHINA’S ARCTIC STRATEGY: FROM “FOLLOW-UP” … 159\n\ngive  the  5G  network  contract  to  Huawei,  the  Chinese  telecommunica-\ntions  giant  (regarding  Japan’s  parallel  strategy  of  presenting  China  as  a\nrevisionist  power  in  the  Arctic,  see  Midford’s  chapter  in  this  volume).\n\nPompeo’s  speech  and  the  US  confrontational  policy  moves  also  can\nbe  found  in  the  Biden  administration’s  Arctic  policy.  In  his  speech  on\nUS  foreign  policy  on  February  4,  2021,  US  President  Joseph  R.  Biden\ndeclared  that  in  working  with  American  allies  and  partners  “we’ll  also  take\non  directly  the  challenges  posed  to  our  prosperity,  security,  and  demo-\ncratic  values  by  our  most  serious  competitor,  China”  (The  White  House,\n2021a).  “To  the  People’s  Republic  of  China,  we’ll  compete  with  conﬁ-\ndence;  we’ll  cooperate  wherever  we  can;  we’ll  contest  where  we  must”\n(Blinken,  2022).  Thus,  dealing  with  the  so-called  “China  Challenge”  is\nstill  a  priority  for  US  policy  toward  the  Arctic  in  Biden’s  administration,\nand  the  two-wheel  model  of  China–US  relations  driven  by  competition\nand  cooperation  in  the  Arctic  is  roughly  predictable.\n\nBig  Event  4:  On  February  24,  2022,  Russia  launched  its  self-deﬁned\n“special  military  operation”  against  Ukraine.  In  the  context  of  the  Russia–\nUkraine  conﬂict,  the  increasing  high  tension  between  Russia  and  the\nWest  stands  out  with  Western  sanctions  on  Russia  and  Russia’s  counter-\nsanctions  on  the  West.  As  of  the  writing  of  this  chapter,  the  spillover  effect\nof  the  Russia–Ukraine  conﬂict  into  the  Arctic  is  exactly  what  deserves\nsincere  concern.\n\nFirst,  the  spillover  effect  of  Russia–Ukraine  conﬂict  on  the  Arctic\ngovernance  system:  the  system  would  be  split  into  two  parts,  one  domi-\nnated  by  the  West  and  the  other  by  Russia.  The  facts  are  as  follows:  In\nMarch  2022,  the  other  seven  Arctic  states  (A7)  as  well  as  members  of\nthe  Arctic  Council  excluded  Russia  from  the  Arctic  governance  system,\nincluding  the  Arctic  Council,  Arctic  Economic  Council,  Nordic  Council\nof  Ministers,  Northern  Dimensions,  Barents  Euro-Arctic  Council.  As  a\nresponse,  in  May  2022,  Russia  withdrew  from  the  Council  of  the  Baltic\nSea  States.\n\nSecond,  the  spillover  effect  of  Russia–Ukraine  conﬂict  on  Arctic  secu-\nrity  framework:  NATO  began  to  expand  in  the  north.  On  May  18,  2022,\nFinland  and  Sweden  formally  submitted  their  applications  for  NATO\nmembership.  The  accession  protocols  for  Finland  and  Sweden  were  signed\non  July  5,  2022,  after  completion  of  accession  talks.  On  April  4,  2023,\nFinland  formally  became  a  member  of  NATO.  However,  Swedish  NATO\nmembership  was  delayed  until  recently  due  to  delayed  ratiﬁcation  by\nHungary  and  Turkey.\n\n\f160\n\nX. QINGCHAO\n\nThird,  the  spillover  effect  of  Russia–Ukraine  conﬂict  on  Arctic  inter-\nis\nnational  scientiﬁc  and  technological  cooperation:  such  cooperation\nbeing  politicalized  and  securitized.  In  March  2022,  the  IASC  proclaimed\nits  support  for  the  Arctic  Council’s  standpoint  on  the  Russia–Ukraine\nconﬂict.  In  April  2022,  the  document  France’s  Polar  Strategy  for  2030:\nBalancing  the  Extremes  declared  France’s  decision  to  halt  its  plan  to  co-\norganize  the  4th  edition  of  the  Arctic  Science  Ministerial  with  Russia  in\n2023.  In  June  2022,  the  Guidance  On  Scientiﬁc  and  Technological  Cooper-\nation  with  the  Russian  Federation  for  US  Government  and  US  Government\nAfﬁliated  Organizations  stated  that  the  United  States  had  taken  “active\nmeasures  to  limit  bilateral  science  and  technology  research  cooperation\nwith  the  Russian  government”  (The  White  House,  2022b)  in  which  the\nArctic-related  science  and  technology  research  cooperation  is  included.\n\nProspective  Scenarios  of  Arctic  Security  Dilemmas\nInvolving  China  and  Potential  Chinese  Options\n\nConfronting  increased  uncertainties  in  the  Arctic  security  strategy  envi-\nronment  analyzed  above,  three  scenarios  regarding  possible  Arctic  security\ndilemmas  that  are  plausible  in  the  future  are  summarized  here,  inducing\npotential  options  for  China—an  important  Arctic  stakeholder.\n\nScenario  1:  An  emerging  China–US–Russia  strategic  triangle\nin\nThere  have  been  discussions  about  the  Arctic  strategic  triangle\nrecent  years.  The  “New  Arctic  Strategic  Triangle  Environment”  in  which\n“the  primary  security  requirements  of  the  three  most  powerful  states  are\nnow  overlapping  in  the  Arctic  region”  (Huebert,  2019).  In  the  meantime,\n“the  potential  for  Russia  and  China  to  cooperate  in  opposing  US  inter-\nests”  (Pincus,  2020)  in  the  Arctic  was  highly  emphasized  in  academia.\nSome  identify  the  existing  pressure  points  in  the  Sino-Russian  strategic\npartnership,  and  advise  the  United  States  to  exploit  these  to  prevent\nenergy  collaboration  between  China  and  Russia  from  becoming  mili-\ntary  collaboration  (Leddy  and  Lurie,  2020).  They  further  suggest  that\nthe  United  States  cooperate  with  China  where  possible  and  compete\nwhen  necessary.  The  US  National  Strategy  for  the  Arctic  Region  issued\nin  October  2022  emphasizes  that  China  “seeks  to  increase  its  inﬂuence  in\nthe  Arctic  through  an  expanded  slate  of  economic,  diplomatic,  scientiﬁc,\nand  military  activities”  (The  White  House,  2022a).  In  this  context,  the\nassumption  that  the  formation  of  the  China–US–Russia  strategic  triangle\nis  taking  place  in  the  Arctic  region  seems  not  to  be  an  illusion.\n\n\f7 CHINA’S ARCTIC STRATEGY: FROM “FOLLOW-UP” … 161\n\nScenario  2:  A  new  Cold  War  between  China–Russia  and  US-NATO  in\n\nthe  Arctic  region\n\nIn  May  2020,  the  Trump  administration  published  a  report  entitled\nUnited  States  Strategic  Approach  to  the  People’s  Republic  of  China , which\nin  some  social  media  in  China  is  considered  to  be  a  declaration  of  a  new\nCold  War  between  the  United  States  and  China  against  the  background\nof  the  “Trade  War”,  the  “Science  and  Technology  War”  and  the  “Finan-\ncial  War”  between  these  two  countries.  In  recent  years,  the  decoupling\nmeasures  taken  by  the  United  States  with  respect  to  China  have  dramat-\nically  expanded  to  the  ﬁeld  of  cultural  communications  and  exchange.\nMoreover,  in  a  2020  Nordic  foreign  and  security  policy  report  Nordic\nstates  also  expressed  concerns  about  the  rivalry  among  great  powers  as\nwell  as  the  return  of  Cold  War  in  the  Arctic  given  that  the  United  States\nconsiders  China’s  presence  in  the  Arctic  as  a  threat  to  its  national  inter-\nests  (Bjarnason,  2020).  Considering  the  spillover  effect  of  Russia–Ukraine\nconﬂict  on  the  Arctic  as  noted  previously,  especially  the  “hybrid  tensions\nbetween  Russia  and  the  other  Arctic  states  (...)  rampant  in  the  2020s”\n(Kauppila  and  Kopra,  2022),  a  new  Cold  War  between  China–Russia  and\nUS-NATO  in  the  Arctic  region  cannot  be  dismissed.\n\nScenario  3:  A  period  with  China  being  a  solo  player  in  geopolitical  chaos\nIn  the  wake  of  the  Russia–Ukraine  conﬂict,  a  period  of  geopolitical\nchaos  in  the  Arctic  seems  to  replace  the  high  geopolitical  stability  that\nHeininen  highly  appreciated  as  exceptional  in  world  politics  and  IR.  The\nfuture  of  the  Arctic  Council  is  caught  up  in  a  heated  debate.  The  A7  have\nreinforced  their  solidarity  within  the  Arctic  Council  excluding  Russia.\nRussia  accordingly  amended  its  state  Arctic  policy  to  2035  prioritizing  its\n“national  interest”  and  removing  cooperation  within  the  Arctic  Council\n(Humpert,  2023).  At  the  same  time,  countries  including  South  Africa,\nBrazil,  and  Russia  are  shaping  common  interests  in  Arctic  collaboration.\nIn  this  case,  China  is  dragged  into  another  dilemma.  The  Arctic  Council\nhas  been  a  primary  platform  for  Chinese  participation  in  Arctic  affairs\nsince  2013;  China  joined  all  six  of  its  working  groups  as  an  observer.  Then\nhow  should  China  position  itself  in  the  virtually  “new”  Arctic  Council\nwithout  Russia?  Is  China  still  legal  as  an  observer  of  the  Arctic  Council?\nShould  China  join  Russia’s  growing  circle  of  friends  aiming  to  explore\ncooperation  in  the  Arctic  Zone  of  Russian  Federation  (AZRF)?  Or  should\nChina  be  a  solo  player  maintaining  the  freedom  of  choice  in  hand?\n\nThese  three  scenarios  of  Arctic  security  actually  represent  three  types\nof  dilemmas  for  China  when  dealing  with  Arctic  issues.  In  terms  of  the\n\n\f162\n\nX. QINGCHAO\n\ndegree  of  China’s  engagement  in  Arctic  global  governance  in  the  years  to\ncome,  there  are  three  potential  options  for  China,  each  accompanied  by\ntheir  own  unavoidable  duality.\n\nOption  1:  A  position  to  the  left—China  continues  to  increase  investment\n\nin  the  Arctic  and  seeks  leadership  in  Arctic  global  governance\n\nIn  this  approach,  China  would  be  assertive  on  those  security  issues\nrelated  to  the  Arctic.  On  traditional  security  issues,  according  to  the  spirit\nof  the  UN  Charter  and  basic  principles  of  international  law,  China  would\nmake  its  own  voice  be  heard  in  the  international  arena,  providing  solu-\ntions  for  tough  problems  and  not  excluding  creating  new  international\nmechanisms  and  platforms  to  discuss  security  issues.  On  non-traditional\nsecurity\nfocusing  on  the  Arctic  region,  China  would  play  a\nleading  role  in  international  rules-making,  such  as  fulﬁlling  her  interna-\ntional  promise  to  become  carbon  neutral,  leading  the  global  response  to\nclimate  change  effectively,  advocating  public  health  emergency  treatment,\norganizing  joint  search-and-rescue,  etc.\n\nissues,\n\nConsidering  China’s  capacity  for  Arctic  involvement  at  the  moment,\nthis  might  be  a  “great  leap”.  From  a  positive  perspective,  as  its  overall\nnational  strength  continues  to  grow,  China  might  gain  an  institutional\nsay  in  Arctic  affairs,  which  would  help  China  play  a  greater  and  more\nconstructive  role  in  Arctic  sustainable  development.  At  the  same  time,\nArctic  states  and  other  stakeholders  would  get  a  free  ride  on  China’s  high-\nquality  development  in  a  more  reasonable  way,  and  the  prospect  of  an\nexpected  “Arctic  community  of  shared  future”  may  be  foreseeable.\n\nOn  the  negative  side,  the  cost  for  China  to  obtain  and  maintain  lead-\nership  in  the  Arctic  might  grow  beyond  its  expectations  since  China’s\nleadership  would  not  likely  be  welcomed  by  other  Arctic  stakeholders,\nespecially  by  the  Arctic  states.  In  that  case,  the  Arctic  geopolitics  would\nbe  changed  fundamentally  and  not  favorably  for  China  as  well.  On  the\none  hand,  only  with  the  internal  support  of  China’s  economic  and  social\nprosperity  and  progress  can  China  mobilize  the  resources  and  capabilities\nneeded  to  navigate  the  Arctic.  On  the  other  hand,  China  must  realize\nthe  cost  of  imagined  power  transfer  in  the  Arctic  as  the  so-called  “Thucy-\ndides  Trap”  describes,  which  cannot  be  completely  ruled  out.  Meanwhile,\nChina  may  face  the  risk  of  being  entangled  in  great  power  competition  in\nthe  Arctic,  leading  to  disastrous  results.\n\nOption  2:  A  position  to  the  right —China  withdraws  from  the  Arctic  to\n\navoid  unbearable  geopolitical  risks\n\n\f7 CHINA’S ARCTIC STRATEGY: FROM “FOLLOW-UP” … 163\n\nin  the  Arctic  region\n\nIn  this  case,  China  would  carry  out  a  comprehensive  strategic  contrac-\ntion,  consciously  avoiding  the  potential  risk  of  a  conﬂict  with  the  United\nStates  and  the  West\nin  the  political,  diplomatic,\neconomic,  and  military  ﬁelds.  Under  adverse  circumstances,  China  might\neven  unilaterally  withdraw\nfrom  the  Arctic\nCouncil  and  the  Arctic  Ocean  Fisheries  Agreement ,  and  those  projects\nwith  Chinese  investment.  Due  to  the  limited  scope  of  the  Arctic  Council\nwithout  Russia,  China  owns  the  opportunity  to  quit  when  the  Arctic\nCouncil  is  at  a  historical  turning  point.  However,  such  a  move  would\nactually  equal  a  freeze  in  policy  in  China–Arctic  relations.\n\nfrom  Arctic  affairs,  even\n\nChina’s  withdrawal  would  be  altruistic  as  it  would  probably  have  bene-\nﬁcial  consequences  for  stakeholders  in  the  Arctic,  especially  the  Arctic\nstates.  They  would  no  longer  worry  about  the  so-called  “Chinese  threat  in\nthe  Arctic”  or  deal  with  the  “China  Challenge”  they  are  trying  to  deﬁne,\nthus  relatively  ensuring  mutual  security.  In  the  same  way,  China  would\nnot  worry  about  the  risk  of  so-called  “strategic  overdraft”,  or  the  attack\nby  adverse  international  opinions.\n\nIn  the  context  of  climate  change,  the  atmosphere,  ocean  currents,\ninformation,  and  other  security-related  elements  are  all  in  motion.  The\ninteraction  between  a  globalized  Arctic  and  a  rising  China  is  inevitable.\nIn  the  process  of  Chinese  modernization,  China  needs  a  more  abun-\ndant  resource  reserve  and  a  truly  global  strategic  vision,  while  the  Arctic\nstates  cannot  undertake  all  the  tasks  of  global  governance  and  sustain-\nable  development  of  the  Arctic  on  their  own.  Such  an  artiﬁcial  break  in\nChina–Arctic  interaction  would  not  be  beneﬁcial  for  either  side.  China\nwill  not  obtain  reliable  resources  and  energy  from  the  Arctic  region,  and\nthe  Arctic  countries  will  not  proﬁt  from  China’s  hypermarket  alike.\n\nOption  3:  A  position  on  the  middle  road—China  pursues  a  new  strategic\n\nbalance  with  “creative  involvement”\n\nChina  would  focus  on  the  issues  of  low  politics  such  as  environmental\nprotection  and  sustainable  development  through  the  Arctic  Council.\nRegarding  non-traditional  security  issues,  China  would  seek  to  provide\ninternational  public  goods  to  deal  with  common  challenges.  It  would\naccordingly  strengthen\nits  climate  diplomacy,  science  and  technology\ndiplomacy,  public  diplomacy,  etc.  China’s  creative  involvement  in  inter-\nin  the  Arctic  region  would  be  a  reasonable  option\nnational  disputes\nif  necessary.  Detailed  measures  such  as\nin  the  form\nof  large-scale  engineering  projects,  peacekeeping  operations,  diplomatic\n\ninternational  aid\n\n\f164\n\nX. QINGCHAO\n\nmediation,  and  disaster  relief  under  the  framework  of  the  United  Nations\nare  included  in  this  sense.\n\nAdhering  to  the  Chinese  traditional  philosophy  highlighted  by  the\nGolden  Mean  (Zhong  Yong  Zhi  Dao  in  Chinese),  this  approach  of  main-\ntaining  a  strategic  balance  using  creative  involvement  is  a  positive  and\nprogressive  choice  for  China.  With  the  joint  efforts  of  China  and  other\nArctic  stakeholders  based  on  common  interests  and  mutual  respect  in  an\ninterconnected  world,  a  new  Arctic  order  with  the  forces  of  all  parties\nmoving  toward  balance  would  be  forged.  China  would  play  an  important\nbut  non-dominant  role  in  this  respect,  rather  than  continuing  the  great\npower  game  under  the  Cold  War  mentality.  This  would  likely  achieve\na  win-win  or  all-win  situation,  which  is  the  most  desirable  model  for\nChina–Arctic  relation.\n\nInevitably,  however,  this  choice  is  also  ﬂawed.  As  mentioned  earlier,\ntraditional  and  non-traditional  security  appears  to  be  intertwined  with\neach  other  from  paper  to  practice,  which  would  make  China’s  efforts  to\ndivide  the  one  into  two  difﬁcult  to  achieve.  At  the  same  time,  China’s\nincreased  activities  in  the  Arctic  have  been  accompanied  by  negative  spec-\nulations  among  Arctic  states  about  China’s  strategic  intentions  in  the\nArctic.  As  a  result,  the  so-called  “Chinese  threat  in  the  Arctic”  will  remain\nstrong  and  ﬁerce  in  international  public  opinion  dominated  by  the  West,\nwhile  China’s  vision  of  seeking  a  new  geopolitical  balance  in  the  Arctic\nmay  be  in  danger  of  becoming  unbalanced.\n\nChina’s  Emerging  New  Arctic  Strategy\nCharacterized  by  “Creative  Involvement”\n\nFrom  the  perspective  of  both  its  domestic  and  international  situations,\nChina’s  “follow-up”  strategy,  which  was  applied  to  Arctic  affairs  from\n1999  to  2019,  has  become  untenable  in  the  last  couple  of  years.  On  the\none  hand,  the  “follow-up”  strategy  for  the  Arctic  cannot  meet  China’s\nnew  vision  of  development.  Domestically,  China  is  accelerating  the  forma-\ntion  of  a  new  pattern  of  development  in  which  domestic  cycles  are  the\nmainstay  and  domestic  and  international  cycles  supplement  each  other.\nThis  new  pattern  of  economic  development  is,  to  some  extent,  a  prag-\nmatic  Chinese  strategy  to  counter  the  US-initiated  trade  war,  science  and\ntechnology  war,  and  ﬁnancial  war,  and  the  waves  of  anti-globalization.\nEnsuring  the  safety  of  domestic  and  international  industrial  chains  has\n\n\f7 CHINA’S ARCTIC STRATEGY: FROM “FOLLOW-UP” … 165\n\nbecome  the  top  priority  for  China  to  promote  its  high-quality  devel-\nopment.  This  is  essentially  an  innovation-driven  development  approach,\nwhich  largely  requires  a  diplomacy  with  initiatives  and  creativity  to  match.\nDiplomacy  is  a  political  continuation  of  domestic  affairs  at  large,  which\nhas  not  been  changed  in  China  since  the  reform  and  opening-up  policy\nof  the  late  1970s.  Under  this  principle,  as  part  of  the  “strategic  new  fron-\ntier,”  the  Arctic  is  deﬁnitely  a  testing  ﬁeld  in  theory  to  justify  China’s  new\npolicy.\n\nOn  the  other  hand,  the  growing  difﬁculties  encountered  by  Chinese\ncompanies  also  signal  the  end  of  the  “follow-up”  strategy;  this  was\ninevitable.  In  2019  alone,  failures  suffered  by  Chinese  companies  rose\nsharply.  Here  are  some  typical  cases:  (1)  Continuous  controversies  over\na  proposed  railway  corridor  collaborating  with  Finland  led  to  a  nega-\ntive  evaluation  and  a  consequential  ﬁnal  suspension  on  China’s  side;  (2)\nChinese  ﬁrms  lost  a  bid  for  airport  construction  in  Greenland;  and  (3)\nChinese  companies  were  expelled  from  the  Alaska  LNG  project  esti-\nmated  at  $43  billion,  although  there  was  a  nonbinding  joint  development\nagreement  signed  during  then  US  President  Trump’s  visit  to  China  in\nNovember  2017.  “In  recent  years  the  gap  between  Chinese  preferences\nregarding  the  PSR  and  its  actual  capabilities  and  accomplishments  has\ndramatically  widened  …  (the)  impact  (of  the  PSR)  on  the  far  north  is\nfar  less  signiﬁcant  than  what  was  originally  predicted,  while  the  Russian\ninvasion  of  Ukraine  has,  if  anything,  pushed  Chinese  Arctic  policies  even\nfurther  backwards”  (Lanteigne,  2022).  Facing  the  serious  damages  to\nChina’s  legitimate  rights  and  interests  as  well  as  its  national  image  in  the\nArctic,  China’s  strategy  for  the  Arctic  is  bound  to  take  an  assertive  turn.\nin  Arctic  affairs,  theoretical\npreparations  have  emerged  from  Chinese  academia  since  2010s.  Wang\nYizhou,  a  representative  of  globalism  in  China,  proposed  the  innovative\n\nOn  China’s  diplomatic  transformation\n\n\f166\n\nX. QINGCHAO\n\nconcept  of  “creative  involvement”1  in  2011,  arousing  extensive  discus-\nsions  and  debates  about  China’s  diplomatic  transformation  both  at  home\nand  abroad.  “The  creative  involvement  can  be  implemented  in  a  selective,\nconstructive  and  restrictive  approach  with  prudent  decisions  on  timing,\nmeans  and  content,  rather  than  simply  rely  on  military  repression  or\ndirectly  confront  with  violence”  (Wang,  2012).\n\nIn  this  context,  two  types  of  behaviors  can  comprise  the  “creativeness”\nin  “creative  involvement”.  One  is  independent  initiative:  active  advocacy\ninspired  and  stimulated  by  the  internal  and  external  environment.  And\nthe  other  is  a  dependent  response:  passive  response  to  the  pressure  and\nstimulation  of  internal  and  external  environment.  The  “involvement”  in\n“creative  involvement”  can  be  divided  into  material  and  non-material\ninvolvement  under  the  inﬂuence  of  internal  and  external  environments.\nMaterial  involvement  is  mainly  the  explicit  input  of  funds,  goods,  facilities,\nequipment,  weapons,  and  technical  installations.  For  instance,  commercial\ninternational  aid  is  in  the  form  of  large  engineering  projects.  Non-material\ninvolvement  includes  implicit  input  to  the  legal  system,  rules,  values,  and\nideology  by  means  of  personnel,\nin  peace-\nkeeping  operations,  diplomatic  mediation,  and  disaster  relief  under  the\nUN  framework.  A  typological  analysis  of  creative  involvement  is  shown  in\nTable  7.1.\n\nincluding  soldiers  such  as\n\nDriven  by  both  the  pressing  situation  and\n\ninspired  by  the  “cre-\native  involvement”  theory,  China  has  made  new  changes  in  its  climate\ndiplomacy  related  to  Arctic  global  governance.  In  2016,  the  Standing\nCommittee  of  the  National  People’s  Congress  approved  China’s  accession\nto  the  Paris  Agreement  on  Climate  Change  with  proactive  implementa-\ntion  in  the  following  years.  “China  is  making  greater  and  faster  strides\nlargest\nthan  expected  away\ninvestor  in  solar  and  wind  technology  and  boasting  more  jobs  in  solar\nenergy  than  in  coal-mining.  It’s  all  part  of  a  long-term  economic  strategy\n\nfuels—becoming  the  world’s\n\nfossil\n\nfrom\n\n1  Wang  Yizhou’s  works  about  creative  involvement  include:  The  Theory  of  Contempo-\nrary  International  Politics  (Shanghai:  Shanghai  People’s  Publishing  House,  1995),  Global\nPolitics  and  China’s  Foreign  Diplomacy  (Beijing:  World  Affairs  Press,  2003),  Interna-\ntional  Relations  in  a  Globalized  Perspective  (Beijing:  Peking  University  Press,  2005).,\nCreative  Involvement:  A  New  Direction  in  China’s  Diplomacy  (Beijing:  Peking  University\nPress,  2011),  Creative  Involvement:  The  Evolution  of  China’s  Global  Role  (Beijing:  Peking\nUniversity  Press,  2013),  Ten  Issues  on  China  Foreign  Affairs  (Jiangsu  People’s  Publishing\nHouse,  2015),  and  Creative  Involvement :  The  Transition  of  China’s  Diplomacy  (Beijing:\nPeking  University  Press,  2015).\n\n\f7 CHINA’S ARCTIC STRATEGY: FROM “FOLLOW-UP” … 167\n\nTable  7.1  A  typological  analysis  of  creative  involvement\n\nInternal  environment\nExternal  environment\n\nDependent  response\n\nIndependent  initiative\n\nSource  Author  created  table\n\nMaterial  involvement\n\nNon-material  involvement\n\nMaterial  involvement  with\ndependent  response\nMaterial  involvement  with\nindependent  initiative\n\nNon-material  involvement\nwith  dependent  responsive\nNon-material  involvement\nwith  independent  initiative\n\nto  dominate  in  critical  technologies”  (Savage,  2019).  With  the  Trump\nadministration’s  decision  to  withdraw  from  the  Paris  Agreement  in  2017,\n“the  United  States  has  opened  up  the  opportunity  for  China  to  gain  a\ncompetitive  advantage  and  a  leadership  role  in  regard  to  global  climate\nissues,  as  demonstrated  by  China’s  aggressive  investments  in  renewable\nenergy,  increased  energy  efﬁciency,  and  economic  policies  that  focus  on\nlowering  energy  usage  and  greenhouse  gas  emissions”  (Gallagher  and\nZhang,  2019).  Notably,  China’s  diplomatic  investment  in  climate  actions\nhas  been  remarkable.  “By  the  end  of  2017,  China’s  domestic  carbon\nintensity  had  fallen  by  46%,  reaching  the  upper  limit  target  of  40-45%\nthree  years  ahead  of  schedule.  Renewable  energy  accounts  for  13.8%  of\nprimary  energy,  still  some  way  short  of  15%,  but  the  target  is  set  to  be\nreached  by  2020.  Forest  stock  has  increased  by  2.1  billion  cubic  meters,\nexceeding  the  2020  target.”  (The  State  Council  Information  Ofﬁce  of  the\nPeople’s  Republic  of  China,  2018).  And  “China  will  scale  up  its  Intended\nNationally  Determined  Contributions  by  adopting  more  vigorous  poli-\ncies  and  measures.  We  aim  to  have  CO2  emissions  peak  before  2030  and\nachieve  carbon  neutrality  before  2060”  (Ministry  of  Foreign  Affairs  of  the\nPeople’s  Republic  of  China,  2020).\n\nIn  January  2021,  US  President  Biden  signed  an  executive  order  to\nmake  the  United  States  rejoin  the  Paris  Agreement  on  Climate  Change.\nUnder  this  circumstance,  the  dialogue  and  cooperation  on  climate  change\nbetween  China  and  the  United  States  resumed  with  impressive  results.\nIn  November  2021,  two  joint  statements  on  addressing  climate  change\nwere  signed  by  China’s  Special  Envoy  for  Climate  Change  Xie  Zhenhua\nand  US  Special  Presidential  Envoy  for  Climate  John  Kerry.  One  is  enti-\ntled  “China–US  Joint  Statement  Addressing  the  Climate  Crisis  issued\nin  April  2021”  and  the  other  is  “China–US  Joint  Glasgow  Declaration\n\n\f168\n\nX. QINGCHAO\n\non  Enhancing  Climate  Action  in  the  2020s”.  According  to  the  latter\nintend  to  establish  a  ‘Working  Group  on\nagreement,  “the  two  sides\nEnhancing  Climate  Action  in  the  2020s,’  which  will  meet  regularly  to\naddress  the  climate  crisis  and  advance  the  multilateral  process,  focusing\non  enhancing  concrete  actions  in  this  decade”  (U.S.  Department  of  State,\n2021).  “The  documents  were  set  to  enter  the  implementation  phase  when\nthe  visit  (of  US  House  Speaker  Nancy  Pelosi  to  Taiwan  in  August  2022)\nthat  infringed  on  China’s  sovereignty  and  territorial  integrity  happened,\nforcing  China  to  suspend  the  Sino-US  climate  talks”  (Hou,  2021).  On\nthe  US  side,  “US  climate  envoy  John  Kerry  praised  China’s  efforts  at\ntackling  global  warming  and  urged  Beijing  to  resume  suspended  talks\non  the  issue,  even  as  tensions  ﬂare  with  Washington  over  the  status  of\nTaiwan”  (Varvitsioti  and  Williams,  2022).\n\nAmong  the  four  pillars  of  the  US  National  Strategy  for  the  Arctic\nRegion,  climate  change  and  environmental  protection  are  highlighted\nas  Pillar  2.  The  document  implies  that  dealing  with  climate  change  is\nsupposed  to  be  the  priority  of  the  US  Arctic  agenda  from  2022  to  2032.\nIn  the  meanwhile,  “despite  its  suspension  of  climate  talks  with  the  United\nStates,  China  has  always  been  a  proactive  contributor  to  the  global  climate\nprocess  with  persistent  endeavors  to  support  other  developing  countries”\n(Hou,  2021).  In  this  sense,  China  is  still  taking  a  leading  role  in  dealing\nwith  global  climate  crisis  with  its  “creative  involvement”.  Assuming  that\nChina  applies  the  “creative  involvement”  doctrine  the  Arctic  region,  and\nto  the  extent  that  domestic  politics  of  the  United  States  allows,  China\nand  the  United  States  might  be  able  to  cooperate  again  on  climate  change\nas  a  breakthrough  in  the  context  of  troubled  bilateral  relations.  However,\nit  is  not  to  be  ignored  that  competition  between  China  and  the  United\nStates  for  leadership  in  combating  Arctic  climate  change  is  possible.  For\nexample,  President  Biden  again  positioned  China  as  a  long-term  strategic\ncompetitor  at  the  2021  Virtual  Munich  Security  Conference,  stating  that\n“competition  with  China  is  going  to  be  stiff”  (The  White  House,  2021b).\nIn  this  sense,  in  order  to  achieve  the  win-win  situation  for  China,  the\nUnited  States,  and  the  Arctic  as  a  whole,  it  is  time  and  imperative  for\nChina  to  leverage  its  creative  involvement  strategy  in  the  Arctic  to  replace\nits  previous  “follow-up”  strategy.\n\n\f7 CHINA’S ARCTIC STRATEGY: FROM “FOLLOW-UP” … 169\n\nConclusions\n\nThis  chapter  highlights  China’s  “follow-up”  strategy  for  the  Arctic  from\n1999  to  2019,  which  refers  to  the  gradual,  step-by-step,  and  non-coercive\napproach  China  adopted  in  addressing  Arctic  affairs  within  its  capacity.  It\nprioritized  science  diplomacy  in  the  face  of  dramatic  climate  change  and\nboosted  economic  and  trade  cooperation  with  other  Arctic  stakeholders\nin  the  interests  of  common  development.  China’s  “follow-up”  strategy\nfor  the  Arctic  varied  with  its  economic  development,  which  comprises\nthree  pillars,  namely,  science  diplomacy,  economic  diplomacy,  and  public\ndiplomacy.\n\nHowever,  two  other  geo-strategically  consequential  dates  for  the  Arctic\nappeared,  which  complicated  the  Arctic  security  environment:  Pompeo’s\nspeech  of  2019  in  Finland  and  the  Russian  “special  military  operation”\nagainst  Ukraine  since  February  2022.  Under  the  circumstances,  three\nscenarios  regarding  an  Arctic  security  dilemma  are  likely  in  the  future,\nnamely:  (1)  an  emerging  China–US–Russia  strategic  triangle;  (2)  a  new\nCold  War  between  China–Russia  and  US-NATO  in  the  Arctic  region;  and\n(3)  a  period  with  China  being  a  solo  player  in  geopolitical  chaos.  These\nthree  scenarios  accordingly  create  three  potential  options  for  China’s\nfuture  engagement  in  Arctic  global  governance:  (1)  China  continues  to\nincrease  investment  in  the  Arctic  and  seeks  leadership  in  Arctic  global\ngovernance;  (2)  China  withdraws  from  the  Arctic  to  avoid  unbearable\ngeopolitical  risks;  and  (3)  China  pursues  a  new  strategic  balance  with\n“creative  involvement”.  Considering  the  increasing  risks  and  uncertainties\nagainst  the  background  of  COVID-19  and  the  Russia–Ukraine  conﬂict,\nit  is  clear  that  China’s  legitimate  rights  and  interests  as  well  as  its  image\nin  the  Arctic  were  damaged  unexpectedly  in  terms  of  the  implementa-\ntion  of  the  PSR.  This  situation  urgently  necessitates  the  end  of  China’s\n“follow-up”  strategy  in  the  Arctic,  accompanied  by  a  rethink  of  the  PSR.\nChina’s  new  changes  in  its  climate  diplomacy  indicate  that  a  shift  of\nChina’s  Arctic  strategy  from  “follow-up”  to  “creative  involvement”  is\ntaking  place.  China’s  rich  experiences  and  a  rising  role  in  addressing  global\nclimate  change  over  the  years,  especially  during  the  United  States’  absence\nfrom  the  Paris  Agreement,  have  provided  an  opportunity  for  China  to\nupdate  its  Arctic  strategy.  It  is  the  idea  of  “creative  involvement”  that  is\nsupposed  to  pull  China  out  of  the  security  dilemma  caused  by  the  deterio-\nration  in  Arctic  geopolitics.  Given  the  United  States  values  its  cooperation\nwith  China  in  climate  change  in  the  Arctic,  China’s  creative  involvement\n\n\f170\n\nX. QINGCHAO\n\nis  to  create  a  win-win  situation.  In  the  face  of  the  new  reality,  it  is  time\nfor  China  to  leverage  its  “creative  involvement”  strategy  for  the  Arctic  to\nreplace  its  previous  deﬁcient  “follow-up”  strategy,  thereby  implementing\nChina’s  global  self-image  as  a  responsible  great  power  on  the  international\nstage.\n\nIn  a  word,  considering  the  complexity  of  cooperation,  competi-\ntion,  and  confrontation  China  faces  in  participating  in  Arctic  affairs  in\nthe  future,  China’s  new  promising  and  aggressive  strategy  of  “creative\ninvolvement”  should  be  taken  seriously.  With  the  emergence  of  creative\ninvolvement  in  the  Arctic  as  part  of  China’s  overall  foreign  policy,  we\nwould  say  that  China’s  Arctic  policy  is  in  transition,  not  on  paper,  but  in\npractice.\n\nAcknowledgements  This  study  was\nfor\nPhilosophy  and  Social  Sciences  as  part  of  the  project  titled  “Strategic  Competi-\ntion  and  Cooperation  in  the  Arctic  among  China,  Russia,  and  the  United  States\nfrom  the  Perspective  of  Sustainable  Development”  [20BGJ045].\n\nfunded  by  China’s  National  Ofﬁce\n\nCompeting  Interests  The  author  has  no  conﬂicts  of  interest  to  declare  that  are\nrelevant  to  the  content  of  this  chapter.\n\nReferences\n\nBjarnason,  Björn.  2020.  Nordic  Foreign  and  Security  Policy  2020/Proposals.\nRetrieved  from:  https://www.government.se/49f308/contentassets/c128b7\n9d0e9143469e7df83648eddb3c/nordic_foreign_security_policy_2020_final.\npdf.  Accessed  September  6,  2022.\n\nBlinken,  J.  Antony.  2022.  The  Administration’s  Approach  to  the  People’s  Republic\nfrom:  https://www.state.gov/the-administrations-app\n\nof  China.  Retrieved\nroach-to-the-peoples-republic-of-china/.  Accessed  September  1,  2022.\n\nBoulègue,  Mathieu.  2019.  “Russia’s  Military  Posture  in  the  Arctic:  Managing\n‘Low  Tension’  Environment”.  Chatham  House  The\nJune  2019.\nInternational  Affairs,  Research  Paper,\nfrom:  https://www.chathamhouse.org/sites/default/ﬁles/2019-\n\nHard  Power\nRoyal\nRetrieved\n06-28-Russia-Military-Arctic_0.pdf.  Accessed  September  14,  2023.\n\nin  a\nInstitute  of\n\nConley,  A.  Heather.  2018.  China’s  Arctic  Dream,  CSIS  Europe  Program.\nRetrieved  from:  https://www.csis.org/analysis/chinas-arctic-dream.  Accessed\nSeptember  13,  2023.\n\nConley,  A.  Heather  and  Melino,  Matthew.  2020.  America’s  Arctic  Moment:\nGreat  Power  Competition  in  the  Arctic  to  2050,  Washington,  D.C.:  Center\n\n\f7 CHINA’S ARCTIC STRATEGY: FROM “FOLLOW-UP” … 171\n\nfor  Strategic  and  International  Studies.  Retrived  from:  https://csis-website-\nprod.s3.amazonaws.com/s3fspublic/publication/Conley_ArcticMoment_lay\nout_WEB%20FINAL.pdf.\n\nErnie,  Regehr.  2018.  “Nuclear  Submarines\n\nin  the  Arctic:  Limiting  Strategic\nAnti-Submarine  Warfare”.  The  Simons  Foundation.  Retrieved  from:  https://\nwww.thesimonsfoundation.ca/highlights/nuclear-submarines-arctic-limiting-\nstrategic-anti-submarine-warfar