15	1	\N	\N	\N	cc7b0340d2c8e559903cd93632b472665f4ab8fa853a0b65d595a42ddb04bcee	/Volumes/jer4TBv3/enliterator/arctic_research/Putin's Arctic Strategy_Rotnem.pdf	\N	text	completed	754862	Problems of Post-Communism\n\nISSN: 1075-8216 (Print) 1557-783X (Online) Journal homepage: www.tandfonline.com/journals/mppc20\n\nPutin’s Arctic Strategy\nCollaboration or Conﬂict after Ukraine?\n\nThomas E. Rotnem\n\nTo cite this article: Thomas E. Rotnem (2018) Putin’s Arctic Strategy, Problems of Post-\nCommunism, 65:1, 1-17, DOI: 10.1080/10758216.2016.1222869\n\nTo link to this article:  https://doi.org/10.1080/10758216.2016.1222869\n\nPublished online: 27 Oct 2016.\n\nSubmit your article to this journal\n\nArticle views: 1342\n\nView related articles\n\nView Crossmark data\n\nCiting articles: 1 View citing articles\n\nFull Terms & Conditions of access and use can be found at\nhttps://www.tandfonline.com/action/journalInformation?journalCode=mppc20\n\n\fProblems of Post-Communism, vol. 65, no. 1, 2018, 1–17\nCopyright © 2018 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC\nISSN: 1075-8216 (print)/1557-783X (online)\nDOI: 10.1080/10758216.2016.1222869\n\nPutin’s Arctic Strategy\nCollaboration or Conﬂict after Ukraine?\n\nThomas E. Rotnem\nDepartment of Political Science and International Affairs, Kennesaw State University, Kennesaw,\nGeorgia\n\nRussia’s Arctic policy was inaugurated not only to harvest the vast economic resources to be\nfound there, but also for the associated political beneﬁts of restoring Russia’s status vis-à-vis\nthe West and appealing to Russian pride, patriotism, and exceptionalism, notions especially\nimportant for Putin’s regime after the disturbances of 2011. While Russia’s activity in the\nregion had been more collaborative than conﬂictual, since the 2013–2014 Ukrainian events a\nmore confrontational approach has emerged—an approach driven by increasing disappoint-\nment and frustration with the West.\n\nPROLOGUE\n\nAs the Arctic Ocean warms, a variety of states—both the\n“Arctic 5”1 and those more distant—have intensiﬁed their\ninterest in the region. The Russian Federation has arguably\nbeen the one most engaged in the Arctic, particularly since\nVladimir Putin’s second term as president. The Kremlin’s\nArctic focus is part of a two-pronged strategy—one eco-\nnomic, the other political—to use the Arctic for long-term\neconomic development as well as for status augmentation\nvis-à-vis the West and a legitimating formula to increase\npopular support for the regime.2\n\nRussia’s activity in the Arctic, sometimes peppered with\ndisturbing rhetorical ﬂourishes\nand sporadic military\nencounters before the events in Ukraine in early 2014,\nnevertheless was mostly characterized by collaboration\nwith other Arctic powers. In the aftermath of President\nViktor Yanukovych’s fall, however, Russia’s actions in the\nFar North began to display a more confrontational approach\ndue to increasing frustration and disappointment with the\nWest, borne out by the Kremlin’s heightened perceptions of\na Western threat as well as the sanctions regime itself. As\n\nAddress correspondence to Thomas E. Rotnem, Department of Political\nScience and International Affairs, Kennesaw State University, Social\nSciences Building (SO 402), Room 5044, Kennesaw, GA 30144, USA.\nE-mail: trotnem@kennesaw.edu.\n\nColor versions of one or more ﬁgures in this article can be found online\n\nat www.tandfonline.com/mppc\n\nsuch, Russia’s evolving approach to the Arctic will be dis-\ncussed, while also assessing the attendant risks associated\nwith Putin’s Arctic strategy in the “post-Ukraine” security\nand current economic environment.\n\nINTRODUCTION\n\nHarkening back to the days of Soviet Arctic glory, the Putin\nregime early on demonstrated Russia’s interest in the Arctic\nby submitting a claim in 2001 before the United Nations\n(UN) Commission on the Limits of the Continental Shelf.\nAfter the commission found that the claim lacked sufﬁcient\nsupporting scientiﬁc evidence, the Russians stepped up their\nscientiﬁc and development interests in the region in the near\nterm, culminating in 2007 with the dramatic planting of the\ntitanium Russian ﬂag on the seabed at the North Pole. This\nsymbolic gesture, which the regime conceded had no legal\nmeaning, nevertheless presumably masked Russia’s real\ninterest in the region—extracting the vast stores of hydro-\ncarbons and precious gems and metals that underlie Russia’s\nadjoining Arctic seas. However, beyond purely economic\ninterests, what other factors may have spurred Russia’s\ngreater\nin the region? As well, why has\nRussia’s Arctic policy become more conﬂictual vis-à-vis\nthe West in recent times?\n\ninvolvement\n\nWith these research questions in mind, data for the study was\ncompiled from an exhaustive review of books, articles, and\njournalistic accounts of Arctic affairs, in general, and Russia’s\n\n\f2\n\nROTNEM\n\nrole in the Arctic, more speciﬁcally. Additionally, numerous\nRussian-language source materials, including books, newspaper\narticles, government documents, and speeches of important\nRussian government ofﬁcials were examined, particularly as\nthey pertained to Arctic matters. In the end, I also found the\nstatus and honor literature in international relations to be a useful\ntheoretical backdrop	{"filename": "Putin's Arctic Strategy_Rotnem.pdf", "file_size": 754862, "document_type": "strategic_document"}	{}	\N	2025-08-11 21:46:06.897804	2025-08-11 22:38:35.113037	extracted	{"terms_count": 11, "extracted_at": "2025-08-11T22:13:23.944Z"}	Problems of Post-Communism\n\nISSN: 1075-8216 (Print) 1557-783X (Online) Journal homepage: www.tandfonline.com/journals/mppc20\n\nPutin’s Arctic Strategy\nCollaboration or Conﬂict after Ukraine?\n\nThomas E. Rotnem\n\nTo cite this article: Thomas E. Rotnem (2018) Putin’s Arctic Strategy, Problems of Post-\nCommunism, 65:1, 1-17, DOI: 10.1080/10758216.2016.1222869\n\nTo link to this article:  https://doi.org/10.1080/10758216.2016.1222869\n\nPublished online: 27 Oct 2016.\n\nSubmit your article to this journal\n\nArticle views: 1342\n\nView related articles\n\nView Crossmark data\n\nCiting articles: 1 View citing articles\n\nFull Terms & Conditions of access and use can be found at\nhttps://www.tandfonline.com/action/journalInformation?journalCode=mppc20\n\n\fProblems of Post-Communism, vol. 65, no. 1, 2018, 1–17\nCopyright © 2018 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC\nISSN: 1075-8216 (print)/1557-783X (online)\nDOI: 10.1080/10758216.2016.1222869\n\nPutin’s Arctic Strategy\nCollaboration or Conﬂict after Ukraine?\n\nThomas E. Rotnem\nDepartment of Political Science and International Affairs, Kennesaw State University, Kennesaw,\nGeorgia\n\nRussia’s Arctic policy was inaugurated not only to harvest the vast economic resources to be\nfound there, but also for the associated political beneﬁts of restoring Russia’s status vis-à-vis\nthe West and appealing to Russian pride, patriotism, and exceptionalism, notions especially\nimportant for Putin’s regime after the disturbances of 2011. While Russia’s activity in the\nregion had been more collaborative than conﬂictual, since the 2013–2014 Ukrainian events a\nmore confrontational approach has emerged—an approach driven by increasing disappoint-\nment and frustration with the West.\n\nPROLOGUE\n\nAs the Arctic Ocean warms, a variety of states—both the\n“Arctic 5”1 and those more distant—have intensiﬁed their\ninterest in the region. The Russian Federation has arguably\nbeen the one most engaged in the Arctic, particularly since\nVladimir Putin’s second term as president. The Kremlin’s\nArctic focus is part of a two-pronged strategy—one eco-\nnomic, the other political—to use the Arctic for long-term\neconomic development as well as for status augmentation\nvis-à-vis the West and a legitimating formula to increase\npopular support for the regime.2\n\nRussia’s activity in the Arctic, sometimes peppered with\ndisturbing rhetorical ﬂourishes\nand sporadic military\nencounters before the events in Ukraine in early 2014,\nnevertheless was mostly characterized by collaboration\nwith other Arctic powers. In the aftermath of President\nViktor Yanukovych’s fall, however, Russia’s actions in the\nFar North began to display a more confrontational approach\ndue to increasing frustration and disappointment with the\nWest, borne out by the Kremlin’s heightened perceptions of\na Western threat as well as the sanctions regime itself. As\n\nAddress correspondence to Thomas E. Rotnem, Department of Political\nScience and International Affairs, Kennesaw State University, Social\nSciences Building (SO 402), Room 5044, Kennesaw, GA 30144, USA.\nE-mail: trotnem@kennesaw.edu.\n\nColor versions of one or more ﬁgures in this article can be found online\n\nat www.tandfonline.com/mppc\n\nsuch, Russia’s evolving approach to the Arctic will be dis-\ncussed, while also assessing the attendant risks associated\nwith Putin’s Arctic strategy in the “post-Ukraine” security\nand current economic environment.\n\nINTRODUCTION\n\nHarkening back to the days of Soviet Arctic glory, the Putin\nregime early on demonstrated Russia’s interest in the Arctic\nby submitting a claim in 2001 before the United Nations\n(UN) Commission on the Limits of the Continental Shelf.\nAfter the commission found that the claim lacked sufﬁcient\nsupporting scientiﬁc evidence, the Russians stepped up their\nscientiﬁc and development interests in the region in the near\nterm, culminating in 2007 with the dramatic planting of the\ntitanium Russian ﬂag on the seabed at the North Pole. This\nsymbolic gesture, which the regime conceded had no legal\nmeaning, nevertheless presumably masked Russia’s real\ninterest in the region—extracting the vast stores of hydro-\ncarbons and precious gems and metals that underlie Russia’s\nadjoining Arctic seas. However, beyond purely economic\ninterests, what other factors may have spurred Russia’s\ngreater\nin the region? As well, why has\nRussia’s Arctic policy become more conﬂictual vis-à-vis\nthe West in recent times?\n\ninvolvement\n\nWith these research questions in mind, data for the study was\ncompiled from an exhaustive review of books, articles, and\njournalistic accounts of Arctic affairs, in general, and Russia’s\n\n\f2\n\nROTNEM\n\nrole in the Arctic, more speciﬁcally. Additionally, numerous\nRussian-language source materials, including books, newspaper\narticles, government documents, and speeches of important\nRussian government ofﬁcials were examined, particularly as\nthey pertained to Arctic matters. In the end, I also found the\nstatus and honor literature in international relations to be a useful\ntheoretical backdrop for the study.\n\nBased upon this careful review, it appears that, beyond\npurely economic interests in the Arctic, the regime’s con-\nsiderable concern for the Far North is also predicated upon\nenhancing its international status vis-à-vis the West, as well\nas signaling to its domestic audience that Russia’s return to\nthe Arctic is an historic and vital goal.\n\nWith the 2011 Moscow demonstrations and his return to\nthe presidency in early 2012, Putin’s regime appears to have\ncontinued championing the Arctic and its untrammeled devel-\nopment not only for economic purposes, but also, in part, for\nthese international and domestic political purposes. Such\ndevelopments were part and parcel of both a continuing\nsearch for status and respect with regard to Western states\nand a multi-threaded legitimating factor that Putin had pur-\nsued since coming to power, but now saw the vital need to\nramp up yet again. In other words, his “Arctic Strategy” was\njust one appendage of a “social competition strategy”\n(Clunan 2014), seeking global recognition and status through\ninternal development; it also serves as one tentacle of the\nemerging legitimating political formula known as “Putinism,”\none that—in part—recalled former Soviet glories and gave\nvoice and promise to those yet to come.\n\nThus, such rhetorical ﬂourishes regarding the Russian\nArctic, accompanied from time to time with more bellicose\nverbiage, was largely enunciated both for status augmenta-\ntion purposes and for domestic consumption, as many have\nargued that the Russian approach toward Arctic issues at\nthis time was largely collaborative, rather than conﬂictual.\nIndeed, Russia has been a long-time, active member of both\nthe Arctic Council and the Barents Euro-Arctic Council,\ntwo intergovernmental organizations that are procedurally\nconsensus-oriented. However, after the Ukraine events of\nlate 2013, culminating in Russia’s illegal annexation of\nCrimea in March 2014, it is my contention that Russian\nactions and pronouncements regarding the Arctic region\nhave become less collaborative and more conﬂictual\nin\nnature, due primarily to the Kremlin’s unrequited status-\nseeking efforts and a necessity to augment domestic support\nfor the regime. In this paper, I seek to discern how these\nturns of events have come together to result in an evolving\nand changed tenor of the Russian government toward its\nArctic redoubt. As well, I will discuss why the regime’s\nfocus on the Arctic since mid-2015 appears to have drifted.\nIt is doubtful that President Putin’s risky,3 two-pronged\nstrategy toward the Arctic will pay the economic—and\nperhaps political—dividends he hopes it will, given the\naltered security environment since Ukraine,\nthe recent\ndownturn in global hydrocarbon markets, and the potential\n\nnegative domestic political repercussions from an Arctic\nboondoggle that promises a lot, but may deliver very little.\nIn the ﬁnal analysis, however, there is still hope that there\nwill be a return to a more collaborative approach to the\nArctic that will win out over the more recent evolution\ntoward confrontation.\n\nTHE KREMLIN’S “ARCTIC GOLDEN GOOSE”\n\nRussia’s two-pronged Arctic strategy is predicated upon a\nwarming Arctic, something that the UN’s Intergovernmental\nPanel on Climate Change (IPCC) claims will continue with\nthe IPCC’s Fifth\na high degree of conﬁdence. Indeed,\nindicated that during the 1979–2012\nAssessment Report\nperiod, the average loss of Arctic Sea ice very likely reached\nbetween 3.5 percent and 4.1 percent per decade.4 As well,\nthe report predicted that Arctic Sea ice may vanish within\nthirty to forty years (Working Group I 2013).\n\nSuch conditions allow easier access to the signiﬁcant\nhydrocarbon deposits mentioned earlier, as well as rich\nstores of gems,5 mineral ores, and rare earth metals located\nin the Arctic region.6 Indeed, a 2008 study by the U.S.\nGeological Survey (USGS) revealed that 30 percent of the\nworld’s undiscovered natural gas reserves and 13 percent of\nthe globe’s oil reserves were located in the Arctic, with\nRussia’s offshore shelf region perhaps holding a majority\nshare of these totals (Bird et al. 2008). Of these ﬁgures,\napproximately 240 billion barrels of oil and equivalents\n(mainly natural gas) have already been found, a ﬁgure that\nconstitutes nearly as much as the total proven reserves of the\nKingdom of Saudi Arabia. Beyond this, it was estimated\nearly on that another 400 billion barrels of oil and oil\nequivalents lay “undiscovered” still in the Arctic region\n(Emmerson 2010).7\n\nA cursory perusal of the USGS study’s data on the likely\nareas for potential recoverable assets demonstrate that two\nof these areas lie just north of Russia, in the Barents Sea\nbasin and the West Siberian basin (i.e., the Kara and Laptev\nthe Arctic Ocean.8 (See Table 1 and\nSea regions) of\nFigure 1.) By USGS and Russian estimates, therefore, the\nArctic basins to the north of Russia constituted a huge\npotential for fossil fuel extraction, perhaps dwarﬁng the 80\nbillion barrels of oil that Russia’s onshore Arctic oilﬁelds\nyielded to 2008 (Sale 2010).\n\nAlthough Russia has been active in oil and natural gas\nexploration in its Arctic regions since at least the 1970s, the\noffshore areas that the 2008 USGS study indicates hold\npromising reserves did not attract much attention for a\nnumber of reasons, owing mainly to the harsh environment\nand icy seas. Thanks to climate change, however, this has\nchanged and Russia’s leadership has turned its attention\nresolutely toward exploration in the offshore Arctic. As\nPresident Dmitrii Medvedev said at a September 2008\nSecurity Council meeting, “Our ﬁrst and main task is to\n\n\fTABLE 1\nArctic Basins with High Probability of Signiﬁcant Fossil Fuel Deposits\n\nNatural\nGas\n(trillion\ncubic ft)\n\nNatural\nGas\nLiquids*\n(billion\nbarrels)\n\nCrude\nOil\n(billion\nbarrels)\n\nTotal\n(oil equivalent in\nbillions of barrels)\n\nPetroleum Basin\n\nYenisey-Khatang\n\n99.96\n\n2.68\n\nbasin\n\nWest Siberian\n\n651.50\n\n20.33\n\nbasin\n\nEast Barents basin\nEast Greenland\nRift basin\nWest Greenland-\nEast Canada\nbasin\n\nAmerasia basin\nArctic Alaska\n\nbasin\n\n317.56\n86.18\n\n51.82\n\n56.89\n221.40\n\n1.42\n8.12\n\n1.15\n\n0.54\n5.90\n\n5.58\n\n3.66\n\n7.41\n8.90\n\n7.27\n\n9.72\n29.96\n\n24.92\n\n132.57\n\n61.76\n31.39\n\n17.06\n\n19.75\n72.77\n\nSource: Bird et al. 2008.\n*Natural gas liquids include fuels and chemicals that are separated out\nfrom either natural gas or crude oil and include ethane, butane, propane,\nisobutene, and natural gasoline. They may also be used as feedstocks to\nmake a variety of chemicals and plastics.\n\nturn the Arctic into Russia’s resource base for the twenty-\nﬁrst century” (Arctic Resources 2008). With taxation of\nfossil fuels9 production or oil and natural gas exports still\naccounting for at least 40 percent of Russia’s budget and\n\nPUTIN’S ARCTIC STRATEGY AFTER UKRAINE\n\n3\n\nwith oil and gas production in many of the country’s aging\nﬁelds declining (Conley and Rohloff 2015), the government\nplaced the Arctic at the center of its near-term economic\npriorities.10\n\nSince 2008, the Medvedev and Putin administrations have\nspent considerable sums developing infrastructure in the Far\nNorth, as well as for exploration,11 drilling, and production at\nstate-owned Gazprom’s Prirazlomnoye offshore oil ﬁeld, its\nonshore Yamal gas deposits at Bovanenkova, and more\nthe joint venture between Gazprom and\nrecently for\nNovatek for the port and LNG (liqueﬁed natural gas) plant\nat Sabetta, which will serve the Yuzhno-Tambeyskoe gas-\nﬁeld, also on the Yamal peninsula. In future, Gazprom Neft,\na subsidiary of Gazprom, will build an oil production facility\non Yamal as well as directly offshore. The state has also\nsupported extensive exploration and potential drilling projects\nin the south Kara Sea by the country’s other state-owned oil\nand gas company, Rosneft. As of mid-2015, Rosneft planned\nto spend a total of $400 billion in Arctic offshore ﬁelds over\nthe next twenty years (Chehade 2015).12\n\nHowever, the Russian leadership also hopes to develop\nthe Arctic for additional purposes: to create a safe, reliable\ntransit route, the “Northern Sea Route” (NSR), for intra-\nArctic and transoceanic shipping, which will serve both as\nan export route for Russia’s LNG, oil, and minerals, while\ngarnering Russia transport revenues from foreign-ﬂagged\ncargo ships that wish to take advantage of the shortest\ntransit route between Europe and Asia. As President Putin\nremarked, “Huge interest around the world is focused on our\n\nFIGURE 1 Arctic Oil and Natural Gas Basin Map.\nSource: U.S. Energy Information Administration (January 2012).\n\n\f4\n\nROTNEM\n\nNSR…. Considering that Russia has the largest icebreaking\nﬂeet in the world, we are going to develop and use this\neconomic advantage of ours” (Putin: Arktika 2013). The\nregime champions the NSR’s advantages over the Suez\nCanal, proclaiming that the route hugging Russia’s northern\nshores is 40 percent shorter, signiﬁcantly cheaper, and\navoids the pirate-infested waters of the Indian Ocean and\ntransit backlogs through the Straits of Malacca.\n\ntransits through the NSR rose signiﬁcantly.\n\nWith Arctic Sea ice receding to about half the extent\nof that in 1979 (Bering Strait 2013), between 2010 and\n2013,\nIn\n2010, only four ships plied its waters, carrying 110,000\ntons of cargo. In 2012, a total of 46 ships traversed the\nNSR, and in 2013, 71 ships with 1.4 million tons of\ncargo sailed the NSR. Plans exist to increase total ton-\nnage transiting the route to seventy million by 2030\n(Litvinova and Makarov 2014). One early study estimated\nthat trans-NSR shipping may grow by thirty times by then\n(Koranyi, 2013).\n\nAs a consequence, the Medvedev and Putin administra-\ntions have passed a variety of strategic economic plans,13\neach in part dedicating some efforts and resources to fully\ndeveloping the NSR,\nincluding updating port facilities,\nbuilding search and rescue stations, improving communica-\ntion and weather-forecasting facilities, and dredging river\ndeltas to improve cargo turnover. In addition, the govern-\nment is investing in bringing a number of next-generation\nicebreakers on line in the shortest possible timeframe, add-\ning to its already formidable ﬂeet of 42 nuclear- or diesel-\npowered icebreakers.14\n\nThe Putin administration demonstrated its view in 2013\nthat the NSR is a geo-strategic asset, as well, making plans\nin September of that year to begin regular naval patrols\nalong the shipping lane. At that time, the Russian minister\nof defense announced the policy shift, after sending the\nﬂagship of the Russian Northern Fleet, the heavy nuclear-\npowered battlecruiser, Pyotr Velikii (“Peter the Great”), at\nthe head of a ﬂotilla of ships plying through much of the\nNSR. Besides helping to stop the ﬂow of illegal drugs and\nimmigrants into the northernmost reaches of Russia in the\nwarmest months of the year, the plan converges nicely with\nRussia’s goal to extend its claims to sovereignty over here-\ntofore sparsely traveled coastal waters to its north. It also\nallows the country to exert incipient sovereign claims to\nseabed resources in areas adjacent to its continental shelf,\nfor example, along the Mendeleev and Lomonosov ridges,\ntwo seabed ridges that, if recognized as part of the Russian\nshelf, would extend Russia’s claims to fossil fuel, additional\nnon-hydrocarbon resources, ﬁshing grounds, and perhaps\ntransport routes well into the Arctic Ocean.\n\nAlthough the exploitation of Russia’s Arctic regions has\nbeen a vital economic interest to the regime for some time,\n“mastering the Arctic” has also been important for both global\nand domestic political purposes, especially after the demon-\nstrations in 2011–2012 and Putin’s return to the presidency.\n\nTHE IMPORTANCE OF RUSSIA’S FAR NORTH: THE\nSEARCH FOR GLOBAL STATUS AND A\nLEGITIMATING IDEOLOGICAL FRAMEWORK\n\nBesides the potential economic beneﬁts accruing from such\nArctic endeavors, the Putin regime also pursued a vigorous\nArctic policy in order to gain status vis-à-vis the West, as\nwell as support from its domestic audience. Before provid-\ning evidence to support these assertions, I will ﬁrst reﬂect\nmore widely upon status augmentation and “Putinism” in\ncontemporary Russia. In my view, the two have become\nwholly linked in Putin’s Arctic strategy.\n\nAccording to psychologists as well as other social scien-\ntists, the need for social status is a crucial motivation for\nhuman behavior (Tajfel 1978; Wright 1994; Lebow 2008).\nAn individual’s status is suggested by the position or rank\nheld “within a publicly acknowledged or collectively under-\nstood social hierarchy” (Ward 2013). And, as Heller (2014)\nargues, claims for respect proffered by individuals are\n“active requests\nstatus,\nwhich is something that groups and states, too, require or\nseek. Indeed, Lebow (2006) concurs, asserting that the quest\nfor status among the latter—that is, states seeking to gain\nhonor or standing—is an endeavor that can be traced back\nmillennia to the Greek notion of “spirit.” What is more, all\nstates seek status, not merely the great powers (Carvalho\nand Neumann 2014).\n\nto acknowledge” one’s\n\nsocial\n\nMuch like Wood (2013), Morgenthau (1973) discussed\nstatus or “prestige” as a political resource, perhaps not as\nimportant as power, but nonetheless signiﬁcant, as “what\nothers think about us is as important as what we actually\nare.” Here, again, is the requirement that status is something\nthat must be conceded by others, for it “has no meaning until\nit is acknowledged” from without (Lebow 2008). Therefore,\nstatus inconsistency—or the situation that arises when a given\nstate believes it has not been accorded by others the level of\nstatus it deems necessary—can lead to hostile or aggressive\nresponses (Ward 2013); some argue competition for status is a\nfundamental cause of warfare (Lebow 2010).\n\nRussia’s “quest for respect” (or status) from the West is\nnothing novel, having been a “constant factor, driving post-\nSoviet Russian foreign policy” since the early 1990s (Heller\n2014). Sakwa concludes that this drive is perhaps centuries\nold (2008). However long status concerns have directed\nRussian, Soviet, or post-Soviet elites, some argue that such\ninterests have become more signiﬁcant than economic or\nsecurity questions in today’s Russia (Forsberg et al. 2014).\nForsberg (2014) avers that what is most important is not the\nobjective status position held by Russia, but the perceptual\ngap between the West and Russia concerning what status is.\nKortunov (2016) also argues that, at base, misperception\nand misunderstanding regarding the cultural norm of respect\nare the cause of current Western–Russian difﬁculties.\n\nTsygankov (2014) maintains that, besides the perceptual\ndistinction between Western and Russian conceptions of\n\n\fhonor, a complicating factor in the relationship is Russia’s\nown “deep emotional connection to the West,” which cre-\nates an intense quasi “sibling rivalry.” Accordingly, when\nRussia’s elites believe the country is being respected by the\nWest, Russia “displays emotions of hope,” whereas frustra-\ntion and anger set in when the Kremlin feels the West has\ndisregarded its honor or identity. This then portends status-\ninconsistent, assertive, and aggressive behavior on the part\nof Russia. After the events in Ukraine, Russia’s elite felt\ndemeaned by the West, hence the subsequent evolution from\ncollaboration toward greater confrontation in the regime’s\nArctic policy.\n\nYet, Tsygankov’s article also draws attention to the\ndomestic side of Russian status concerns, discussing how\nforeign policy elites must at one and the same time address\nforeign and local audiences.\n\nSimilarly, Ward (2013) and Swart (2016) underscore the\nlink between international relations, status, and the domestic\nrealm in different cultural contexts.15 To be sure, Forsberg\net al. (2014), agree, saying that elites value status for instru-\nmental purposes as well as for intrinsic ones, as status bol-\nsters their domestic legitimacy and popularity. For their part,\nthe mass public views the country’s status and standing as\nvitally important, tending to “project many of their emotional\nneeds on their state and seek vicarious fulﬁllment through its\nsuccesses” (Lebow 2006). Insofar as a signiﬁcant section of\nthe population of Russia laments the loss of global respect\nsince 1991, this public penchant serves as ready grist for\nincreasing Putin’s base of support. Putin’s Arctic strategy,\ntherefore, was inaugurated also to buttress the regime.\n\nIndeed, since coming to power, the Putin regime has devel-\noped a multi-tiered, legitimating ideology that some have\nlabeled “Putinism” (Laqueur 2015; Stier 2014). The resulting\nsupportive ideology includes a veritable hodge-podge of con-\ncepts and notions, an ideological potpourri that was reinvigo-\nrated and reinforced after the 2011–2012 demonstrations.\n\nTo be sure, the regime’s initial liberal economic bent has\ngiven way to an entrenched, state capitalist, dirigiste eco-\nnomic philosophy guiding the economy’s development\nideology also contains\n(Shevtsova 2015). The Putinist\nwithin it a sort of co-dependent\nreliance upon the\nOrthodox Church, wherein the state’s mission is conﬂated\nwith the Church’s (Coyer 2015), and Russian Orthodoxy\nbecomes a “useful tool” for the state to use to advance its\ngoal of domestic tranquility (Petro 2015). Likewise, the\nreversion to traditional authoritarian measures with the\ntrappings of democracy (Laqueur 2015) can be justiﬁed as\ninstability or chaos.16\nsaving the public from political\nMoreover, the development of a personality cult around\nPutin serves to “infantilize the public and civic mentality”\nin order to create a reverent populace that holds Putin’s\nregime beyond challenge (Blank 2015).\n\nAn anti-Western narrative, born out of resentment at\nRussia’s lost superpower status and the humiliations of the\n1990s, forms another imaging tier of Putinism (Laqueur\n\nPUTIN’S ARCTIC STRATEGY AFTER UKRAINE\n\n5\n\nthe\n\nthat\n\ndrives\n\nregime’s\n\nbiopolitics\n\n2015). Andrei Piontkovsky extends the analysis further,\narguing that not only the Russian state, but perhaps the\nentire russkii mir, or “Russian world,” is under threat from\nthe West, and as such it becomes Putin’s “sacred duty to\nprotect not only the citizens of Russia, but all ethnic\nthe\nRussians and Russian-speaking people throughout\nworld” (Dekhtiarenko 2015). Defending this russkii mir\nfrom the decadent culture of the West is another powerful\nimage\nagenda\n(Makarychev and Medvedev 2015). Stephen Blank and\nYounkyoo Kim argue that, rather than anti-Westernism,\nanti-Americanism is perhaps the most powerful driver in\nRussian foreign policy today (2015), as the United States\nattempts to (quoting from Aleksandr Dugin) “force upon\nother continents its anti-ethnic, anti-traditional, ‘babylonic\nmodel’” (Barbashin and Thoburn 2014). To be sure,\nPutinism contains within it\nthe United\nStates—or the international organizations it instrumentally\nmanipulates—betrayed Russia and is actively denying\nMoscow its place as a great power (Kotkin 2015; Stoner\nand McFaul 2015; Russia’s National Idea 2015).17 Even in\nRussian ﬁction today, Laruelle ﬁnds constructed an “image\nof Russia surrounded by internal and external enemies,”\nwhere the West and the United States deny the Russians\ntheir great power status “so that they can be more easily\nruled” (Laruelle 2012a).\n\nthe notion that\n\nRussian exceptionalism frames yet another leg upon\nwhich the Putinist ideological scaffolding stands, one that\nproves Russia’s special mission, provides an “alternative\nsocial model” to that found in the West (Kotkin 2015),\nand gloriﬁes the “great Slav nation,” while serving as an\nhistoric, Eurasian conduit between East and West (Gaddy\nand O’Hanlon 2015). According to Mark Urnov, one reason\nthe Kremlin’s propaganda may be so successful in trumpet-\ning the exceptionalist line is that such rhetoric fulﬁlls the\nneed that Russians have for “a positive identity, self-asser-\ntion and national pride” (Ivanov 2015).18 A corollary to\nexceptionalist notions is the regime’s consistent emphasis\nupon instilling national pride and patriotism in the populace;\nindeed, Dauce et al. note no less than three Russian govern-\nment programs that exist to inculcate among the citizenry\n“love for motherland, devotion to fatherland, willingness to\nserve its interests and defend it, up to and including self-\nsacriﬁce” (2015). Petersson,\ntoo, demonstrates one other\nmeans of ramping up patriotic fervor for the regime, namely,\nby successfully hosting the Sochi Winter Olympic Games as\na form of “great power myth-making” exercise, in full view\nof the global community (2014).19 Vitalii Gorokhov simi-\nlarly suggests that hosting sports “mega-events” contributes\nto boosting international recognition and the regime’s pres-\ntige and legitimacy, while also drawing upon past Soviet\nsporting achievements for added succor (2015).\n\nBesides ushering in a comprehensive drive toward a largely\nunchecked opening of the Arctic for economic exploitation\nthe Kremlin’s Arctic policy is also in\nand development,\n\n\f6\n\nROTNEM\n\na\n\nas\n\nstate\n\n“far north”\n\nprogrammatic consonance with these latter ideological notions\nof “Putinism,” as a nod to Russian exceptionalism, as a paean\nto Soviet achievements (or “Sovietism,” as described by Lilia\nShevtsova), or as an appeal to national pride and patriotism,\napparently again for the instrumental purpose of furthering the\nregime’s stability and popularity. For example, Laruelle con-\nvincingly demonstrates that various metanarratives may be\nfound in Russian nationalist discourse today that extol\nRussia’s uniqueness\n(2012b).\nFurthermore, Josephson’s work on “hero projects” of the\nSoviet era (1995) reveals the display value of such accom-\nplishments to the Soviet elite, of which the opening of the\nSoviet Arctic was one major feat (2014); Putin’s appeals to\nmodern-day Arctic exploits appear to echo those from the\nancien regime. As well, according to Laruelle, the regime has\nused its recent efforts in the Arctic as a “nation-branding”\nexercise, the desired effect of which is to both gain Western\nrecognition for Russia as a “great power” and to connect its\ndevelopment with Russian national sensibilities, sentiments\nthat view the Far North “as a place of refuge” (2014a) that\napparently needs to be preserved, renewed, and fortiﬁed from\nforeign threats. Finally, Pavel Baev argues that Putin’s pench-\nant for promoting the Russian Federation as an “Arctic super-\npower” was undertaken to gain popular support (2013).20\n\nPUTIN’S ARCTIC STRATEGY: REGIME STATUS\nAUGMENTATION IN PRACTICE\n\nSpeaking to the Seliger National Youth Forum in August\n2014, President Putin argued for Russia’s exceptional and\nthe region is “a\nunique role in the Arctic, stating that\ncrucially important region for Russia. Our country is the\nworld’s biggest in terms of size. Even after the collapse of\nthe Soviet Union, which was truly vast, Russia remains the\nbiggest country in terms of territory, but more than seventy\npercent of our territory is located in the Far North. This is\nsomething we need to remember” (Razvitie Arktiki 2014.)\nEarlier, in a wide-ranging interview on the government’s\nArctic policy, Putin had also discussed Russia’s exceptional\nconnection to the Far North, while also perhaps detailing\ncertain status claims to the global audience, stating that the\n“Arctic is an inalienable part of Russia. So it has been and\nso it always will be” (Putin: Arktika 2013).\n\nRussia’s modern-day struggle for the Arctic is also\nnoticeably reminiscent of—and often metaphorically\njoined to—the Soviet drive to conquer the region in the\n1930s, as Stalin’s regime exhorted polar explorers and\naviators onward, while seeking to build great cities,\ndevelop mammoth infrastructure projects, and unlock the\nvast mineral deposits in the unimaginably austere frozen\nNorth—at whatever cost—in order to gain the loyalty of\nand “hold the Soviet nation together”\nthe masses\n(McCannon 1998). Putin, when visiting the Franz Josef\nLand archipelago as prime minister in 2010, exclaimed to\n\ntelevision cameras, “Geopolitically, the deepest interests\nof the Russian state are connected with the Arctic…. The\nregion needs to be mastered [osvaivat’], the more actively,\nthe better” (Lukin 2013). Of course by “mastering” the\nArctic, Putin’s Arctic strategy envisages a wholesale\nexploitation of its mineral and hydrocarbon riches, as\nwell as establishing the Northern Sea Route as a poten-\ntially major transit route for shipping between Asia and\nEurope; all this harkens back to the ﬁrst major opening of\nthe Arctic, that taking place during Stalin’s “revolution\nfrom above” in the 1930s.\n\nMoreover, President Putin later made an appeal\nto\nnational pride and patriotism, proclaiming that “For us, the\nArctic opens a new page in our history, which we shall call\nthe epoch of industrial breakthrough, with heady economic\nand infrastructural development” (Putin: Arktika 2013).\nAnd such aspirations appear to be in keeping with Russian\nattitudes, as a poll conducted by Komsomolskaia Pravda in\n2007 demonstrated that almost two-thirds of respondents\nthought that Russia should “make the North attractive for\nRussian businesses and the general population,” while\nanother 18 percent thought the state should master these\nterritories by taking part in “large-scale state projects, as in\nthe Soviet years” (Arktika—goriachaia 2007).\n\nBeyond such speech acts, the regime has signaled its\nconcerted support for a Soviet-style Arctic presence through\nother means. In 2010, for example, Prime Minister Putin\ninitiated a new program to eliminate thousands of tons of\nharmful waste scattered throughout Far Northern territories.\nAppealing to national pride and drawing a further parallel\nwith earlier Soviet history, Putin proposed a “general\ncleanup” (uborka) of the Arctic,\ninviting all citizens to\ntake part in the “polar subbotnik” (Vladimir Putin predlozhil\n2010). The program began in earnest in 2011 and continues\nto today, with a variety of local, state, and federal agencies\n—directed by the Ministry of Natural Resources and\nEcology—taking part in cleanup efforts. In 2016 alone, the\nministry will spend 1.6 million rubles for operations on the\nFranz Josef Land archipelago (Rossiiskie vlasti 2016).\n\nMoreover,\n\nin 2010 the government began to sponsor\nseasonal “Arctic Days” fora in various cities throughout\nthe country.21 Perhaps the most high proﬁle of these events\nhave taken place in Moscow and St. Petersburg, centered\naround photo, ﬁlm, and other cultural and historical exhibi-\ntions detailing past and present life in Russia’s Far North;\nhigh-ranking government elites attend afﬁliated lectures and\nconferences discussing the Arctic’s natural environment,\necological problems affecting the region, and eco-tourism\nopportunities. Additional “Arctic Days” fora have taken\nplace in Murmansk,\nthe Yamal peninsula, and Yakutsk,\namong others, with the latest opening in March–April\n2016 in the Republic of Sakha (Plan meropriiatii, 2016).\n\nAdditionally, beginning in 2012, the Russian Geographic\nSociety, the Northern (Arctic) Federal University, and the\nFederal Service for Hydrometeorology and Environmental\n\n\fMonitoring began offering educational and research oppor-\ntunities for young Russian students aboard the “Professor\nMolchanov” research vessel, dubbed the “Arctic Floating\nUniversity.” Since that time seven such groups have sailed\nthe waters of the Pechora and Barents Seas, visiting outposts\non Novaia Zemlia and the Franz Josef Land archipelago.\nFocusing on the unique beauty, resources, and opportunities\nin Russia’s Far North, the goal of the enterprise is to prepare\nfuture specialists for research in the region, as well as to\nincrease participants’ appreciation for the Arctic’s role in the\ncountry’s future (Konstantin Zaikov 2015).\n\nIn 2012 the nation celebrated the silver anniversary of the\nArktika trip to the North Pole, the ﬁrst time any nuclear\nicebreaker—or indeed any surface vessel—had sailed that\nfar north. The following year, Putin signed a decree creating\na new professional holiday, Polar Explorer’s Day, which\nwould be observed each year on May 21; on that day in\nthe Soviet Union set up the world’s ﬁrst polar\n1937,\nresearch drift station, North Pole-1 (Vladimir Putin Signed\n2013). Additionally, state-supported television aired in\n2012–2013 no less than three separate, multi-part documen-\ntaries on the Arctic; to one extent or another these ﬁlms,\n“Three Captains— The Russian Arctic,” “Battle for the\nArctic,” and “The Arctic—Russia’s Project,” focus largely\non heroic tsarist, Soviet, and Russian triumphs in the Far\nNorth.22 Moreover, a variety of reference volumes and\nhistorical booklets on the Arctic have appeared in recent\nyears, including Polar Captains of the Russian and Soviet\nFleets and The Russian Columbus of the 20th Century:\nBoris Vilkitskii.\n\nHence, just as every Soviet achievement in the frozen\nnorth denoted glory for the nation, so, too, every modern-\nday move toward conquest of new frontiers in the Arctic\nenhances a powerful myth under construction of great\nthe regime is\npower progress and vigor. In this sense,\ndrawing upon a deep popular aspiration for Russia to be\ntaken seriously again, leveraging this to enhance domestic\nsupport and legitimacy. To be sure, a poll conducted by the\nstate-owned VTsIOM polling agency in early 2014—shortly\nafter the Crimean annexation—demonstrated that 42 percent\nof Russians would like to see the Russian Federation\nbecome “a great power, like the USSR was once,” and a\nlike number want to see Russia as one of the ten to ﬁfteen\nmost inﬂuential and developed countries in the world (Most\nRussians 2014).\n\nAccordingly, on the occasion of the opening of a large\nscientiﬁc-ecological expedition to the Far North, Putin\nremarked about the importance of the event for the country,\nclaiming “This is a symbolic event for all of Russia, because in\nthe ﬁrst place, it underscores our growing activity in the Arctic.\nOur strengths and efforts here are increasing and we will work\nin many directions” (Vstrecha s uchastnikami, 2012).\n\nThe exercise in great power myth construction and status\naugmentation is also well-served by propagating concerns\nthat need to be\nthat\n\nthreats from outside forces exist,\n\nPUTIN’S ARCTIC STRATEGY AFTER UKRAINE\n\n7\n\nchecked by Russia’s action. In terms of the Arctic and its\nresource potential, in 2009 the Kremlin promulgated a pre-\nsidential decree “On the Russian Federation’s National\nSecurity Strategy to 2020” in which its authors claimed,\n“we cannot rule out the use of force to resolve problems\nthat arise in the competitive battle for resources” (Ukaz ‘O\nStrategii’ 2009). In 2012, President Putin further drew atten-\ntion to Russia’s need to protect its northern areas by issuing\nan executive order that sought to develop “the Navy ﬁrst\nand foremost in the Arctic areas and in Russia’s Far East\nwith the aim of protecting the Russian Federation’s strategic\ninterests” (Executive Order 2012). More recently, the pre-\nsident has referred directly to the threats to Russia posed by\nthe United States in the Arctic. Addressing the Seliger\nYouth Forum in August 2014, Putin said: “The Arctic\nplays a very important part for us in terms of guaranteeing\nour security, too. It is regrettably the case that the United\nStates’ attack submarines are concentrated in that area …\nand the missiles they carry would reach Moscow within\n15–16 minutes…. In short, we have this concentration of\ninterests in the Arctic” (Razvitie Arktiki, 2014).23\n\nKREMLIN COLLABORATION IN THE ARCTIC\n\nPolicy directives and ofﬁcial statements such as these are\nviewed with increasing suspicion in the West, but it must\nbe noted that Russian activities in the Arctic have long been\nboth conﬂictual and collaborative, even before 2014.\nAccording to several prominent researchers on the region,\nthe Russian approach to the Arctic has never been solely\nantagonistic, but a dichotomous one, with considerable atten-\ntion given to regional collaboration, punctuated periodically\nwith “possessive” objectives (Conley and Rohloff 2015;\nFlikke 2011; Laruelle 2014b; Sergunin and Konyshev\n2014a). To be sure, early on the Kremlin concentrated its\nenergies in creating an “Arctic brand” for itself as a state that\nwas energetically focused on cooperation with littoral and\nnon-littoral states in the region (Laruelle 2014c). However,\nat times “possessive” ambitions have come to the fore.\n\nStill, a signiﬁcant body of evidence exists that demon-\nstrates Russia’s earlier commitment\nto a collaborative\nagenda in the Arctic. Indeed, while seen by some as an\nattempt to seize additional territory beyond the 200-mile\nexclusive economic zone, Russia’s initial claims submission\nto the United Nations Commission on the Limitation of the\nContinental Shelf in 2001 actually was evidence that the\ncountry was committed to following the established inter-\nnational legal process for settling continental shelf disputes.\nFurthermore, Russia has always argued that territorial con-\nﬂicts should be resolved according to international legal\nprinciples, particularly in accordance with the UN’s\nConvention on the Law of the Sea and the 2008 Ilulissat\nDeclaration (Sergunin and Konyshev 2014a).\n\n\f8\n\nROTNEM\n\nAgain, Russia has been an active member of the con-\nsensus-driven Arctic Council (AC) and the Barents Euro-\nArctic Council (BEAC), while various Russian regional\ngovernments have been active in “paradiplomacy” efforts\nwith their counterparts in neighboring states (Joenniemi and\nSergunin 2014a). In the BEAC’s Regional Working Group\non the Environment, for example, Russian participation was\nvitally important in exploring issues of surface and drinking\nwater contamination, biodiversity, and raising public aware-\nness and environmental consciousness in the Finnish,\nNorwegian, and Russian border areas (RWGE Annual\nReport 2013). Additional working groups dealt with issues\nin areas as diverse as education, health, investment and\neconomic cooperation, energy, transport and logistics, and\nthe rights of indigenous peoples.\n\nIn addition, in 2010 Russia and Norway amicably settled\ntheir disagreements over maritime delimitation in the Barents\nSea, despite the fact that the agreement was relatively unpop-\nular in Russia (Baev 2013). Moreover, in 2011 Russia and\nother Arctic states promulgated the Aeronautical and Maritime\nArctic Search and Rescue agreement, the ﬁrst legally binding\nAC-negotiated agreement. This was followed in early 2013 by\nthe Arctic Council agreement on Cooperation on Marine Oil\nPollution Preparedness and Response in the Arctic.\n\nAt each of these seminal junctures, Russian policymakers\nhave reiterated Russia’s desire for greater cooperation in the\nArctic, hoping that favorable results in the northern theater\nreafﬁrm and encourage prospects in other arenas\nwill\n(Staalesen 2010). Even as recently as October 2013, Putin\nsaid in answer to a question concerning changes that the\nwarming Arctic has for Russia’s security and economic\ndevelopments in the region, “Of course, the Northern Fleet\nwill continue to carry out its military duties. However, we\nvery much count on all that has happened in recent times\nwill take us away from a Cold War, away from open con-\nfrontation with the West” (Putin: Arktika 2013).\n\nWhy have Russia’s actions in, and rhetoric regarding the\nArctic become more confrontational in the 2014–2015 per-\niod? Of course, the events in late 2011 in Moscow, along\nwith Putin’s cynical return to the presidency in 2012, had\nsome role to play in an evolving Russian narrative on the\nFar North. However, much of what happened—in terms of\nstepped-up military activities and argumentative policy\nstatements—apparently has more to do with the events in\nUkraine,\nthe sanctions regime, and subsequent develop-\nments. In addition, why have Russian policymakers appar-\nently put less emphasis upon the Arctic since the second\nhalf of 2015? These questions will be considered in the\nfollowing sections.\n\nCONFLICT IN THE ARCTIC AFTER UKRAINE\n\nThe events in Ukraine in late 2013 and early 2014 appar-\nently triggered a further reassessment of Russian policy\n\ntoward the West, NATO, and the United States, a policy\nthat had been growing ever more distant from Putin’s ﬁrst-\nterm approach of “Western engagement” and focusing more\non “pragmatic nationalist” goals of multipolar competition,\nnational prestige, and recognition of Russia’s great power\ninterests (Donaldson et al. 2014). From the Kremlin’s per-\nspective, the ouster of Yanukovych on the night of February\n21, 2014—on the heels of an agreement signed between\nYanukovych and opposition forces, with the backing of\nWestern and Russian governments—was an opportunistic,\ntreacherous blow to Russia’s national interests, apparently\nsecured in order to put in place a pro-Western, Russophobic\ngovernment in Kiev. To the Kremlin, this demonstrated the\nWest’s continuing desire to expand its inﬂuence eastward.\nAccording to this narrative, Russia’s leadership had made\nit increasingly clear in recent years that no additional mem-\nbers of the Commonwealth of Independent States should\nbecome candidates for NATO membership. And now, even\nafter the Kremlin demonstrated the lengths to which it\nwould go to forestall “near abroad” countries’ entry into\nNATO, with the defeat of Georgia in the August 2008 war,\nthe West was again disrespecting Russia’s status as well as\nits geopolitical interests in the very heart of Russia’s birth-\nplace, Kievan Rus. Also motivating a reassessment was a\nthe West was promoting another “color\nbelief\nrevolution,”24 one whose demonstration effect might engulf\nelite circles in Russia who had only recently avoided a\nWestern-inspired regime change in 2011 (Stoner and\nMcFaul 2015).\n\nthat\n\nSuch developments demanded a rejoinder, and not only\nalong Russia’s western border, the area most proximate to\nthe transformative events taking place in Ukraine. To be\nsure, Russia’s leaders responded as they have in the Arctic\nfor at least two additional reasons. For one, many Russian\nexperts believed that at least since 2008, NATO has been\nmarkedly intensifying its own activities in the Arctic,25 and\nthey were convinced that the Western alliance would con-\ntinue penetrating the region (Sergunin and Konyshev\n2014a), an area that—at least until climate change began\n—had been Russia’s last refuge.26 Some believe the West\nviews the Arctic as a “key to the restoration of a former\ngeopolitical balance,” and for that reason the United States\nand the NATO powers are ﬁxated upon the region (Polunin\n2015). Second, much of Russia’s sea-based nuclear deter-\nrent is located in the Arctic realm, and hence, a northern\nresponse to the West should have been anticipated.\n\nWith the “unacceptable” ouster of Yanukovych,\nthe\nKremlin’s perception of the Western threat was ampliﬁed,\ncompelling the Russian leadership to take a much stronger\napproach to the West not only in Ukraine, but elsewhere,\ntoo. The introduction of the sanctions regime after the\nannexation of Crimea and the downing of MH-17 was\nanother added blow to status-conscious Russia, an action\nthat only further cemented the Kremlin’s more aggressive\nthe sanctions\nstance in the Arctic. From the beginning,\n\n\ftargeted President Putin’s network of political and economic\nsupporters, the hope being that severe economic pain would\ncause the elite class to pressure Putin to back down or\nremove Putin altogether (Gaddy 2015). As well—and most\nimportant for our purposes here—the sanctions adversely\naffected Russia’s Arctic enterprise, the promised future of\nRussia’s hydrocarbon-driven economy, by banning Western\ncompanies from providing investment funds, equipment,\nand technology for Arctic oil and gas exploitation.\n\nThe Kremlin responded to the Western sanctions regime in\nthree ways. For one, they imposed counter-sanctions against\nthe United States, the European Union, Norway, Canada, and\nAustralia. Second, Russia began a full-court press to ﬁnd\nalternative investment partners for its Arctic development\nincluding speciﬁc successful overtures to both\nprojects,\nChina and India. Last, responding to the West’s damaging\nsanctions “provocation,” which seriously constricted existing\nArctic development plans, the Kremlin ramped up its military\npresence in the northern reaches of Russia.27\n\nStill, the more deﬁnitive motivation for Russia’s intensi-\nﬁed activity in the North was the greater perception of threat\nfrom the West in the wake of the Ukraine conﬂict, as well as\nthe mistrust and fear that resulted from the West’s unwill-\ningness to honor Russia’s status (Tsygankov 2014). The\npotential inclusion of Ukraine in NATO was a threat not\nonly along Russia’s southwestern border, but along its entire\nperimeter. To be sure, Russia has long been concerned with\nthe potential for NATO—or a sub-group of countries within\nthe military alliance—to expand activities in the Arctic. For\nexample, the Kremlin reacted strongly to plans spearheaded\nby the United Kingdom in 2011 to create a nine-member\n“mini-NATO” in northern Europe made up of the ﬁve\nNordic states and the three Baltic countries\n(Russia\nOpposes 2011). As such, Russian scholars would rather\npoint to recent Western actions in the Arctic as the cause\nof the stepped-up Russian rearmament and modernization\nprogram there.28 As one scholar at the Institute of U.S. and\nCanada Studies remarked,\n\nAs far as the security of Russia’s Arctic shelf is concerned,\nthe region has been largely unprotected in military terms:\nthere were no tracking systems, radars, ground troops or\nnaval forces. In the meantime, pretty close to it is the U.S.\nbastion in Alaska, with its intelligence means, missile\ndefense systems and naval forces. Apart from that,\nthe\nScandinavian countries have created their own military\nbloc inside NATO to protect their interests in the near-\nArctic zone. Anti-Russian exercises have been held regu-\nlarly there. Therefore, the measures being taken to enhance\nthe security of Russia’s Arctic shelf are Russia’s proportion-\nate response to Western challenges (Zamyatina, 2014).\n\nThus, because of Yanukovych’s ouster,\n\nthe scale of\nRussia’s activities in the region dramatically increased.\nIndeed, within days of completing the annexation of\n\nPUTIN’S ARCTIC STRATEGY AFTER UKRAINE\n\n9\n\nCrimea in March 2014, the Russian Security Council met\nto abrogate various Russian–Ukrainian military agreements\nover the Black Sea Fleet and Crimea; at the same meeting,\nthe council also decided to strengthen “Russia’s position in\nthe Arctic region,” unofﬁcially linking the security situation\nin Crimea with that on Russia’s northern reaches (Meeting\n2014). At a meeting of the same body in April, Putin spoke\nof additional military infrastructure improvements being\nmade in the region due to increasing interest by Western\nstates in the Arctic (Putin na zasedanii 2014).29\n\nSuch improvements across the length and breadth of the\nArctic concerned NATO greatly, as Russia began the reno-\nvation of thirteen Soviet-era airﬁelds, the construction of ten\nradar posts, ten search-and-rescue bases, and the creation of\na ﬁring range for pilot training. In addition, two motorized\ninfantry brigades, each with six thousand soldiers, were\nthe ﬁrst would be stationed\nplanned for Arctic locales;\nnear Murmansk, while the second would be based on the\nYamal peninsula, near sensitive oil and gas facilities\n(Conley and Rohloff 2015; Majumdar 2014). At the same\ntime, Russian authorities claimed these infrastructural and\nforce improvements were warranted, given the country’s\nextensive plans to develop vast hydrocarbon deposits in\nthe region as well as the Northern Sea Route; this is espe-\ncially so, given the degeneration in infrastructure and rede-\nployments that took place following the collapse of the\nSoviet Union.30\n\nPresident Putin responded in late 2014 to additional\nWestern concerns over the “Vostok-2014 exercises,” the lar-\ngest Russian military maneuvers since the end of the Soviet\nera. Discussing the September exercises in the Far East and\nArctic, Putin mentioned at a meeting of the Defense Ministry\nBoard that they were a response to U.S. plans to build a missile\ndefense system and NATO’s stepped-up activities in Eastern\nEurope. At the same meeting, Putin discussed the creation of a\nnew Joint Strategic Command of the North, based on the\nNorthern Fleet, historically Russia’s largest ﬂeet (Expanded\nMeeting 2014). Within a month, Chief of Staff General Valerii\nGerasimov declared that in response to Western military activ-\nities, special focus would be given to strengthening Russia’s\nmilitary forces in 2015 not only in the Crimea and Kaliningrad\noblast, but also in the Arctic (Rossiia v 2015, 2015).\n\nAdditional examples of the Russian leadership reacting to\nNATO activities in the region include the March 16, 2015,\nﬁve-day snap military exercises, which included 80,000\ntroops, 65 warships, 220 aircraft, and ﬁfteen submarines, in\naddition to putting the Northern Fleet on full combat readi-\nness (Grove 2015). The maneuvers were undertaken ostensi-\nbly in response to the “Joint Viking” military exercise\nlaunched by Norway the previous week, reportedly the lar-\ngest military exercise on NATO’s northern ﬂank since 1967.\nRussia also responded to NATO’s “Arctic Challenge\nExercise” in late May 2015. Here, six NATO member states,\nalong with Sweden, Finland, and Switzerland, launched the\nexercises in the north of Norway, with the involvement of\n\n\f10\n\nROTNEM\n\napproximately 4,000 troops and 115 ﬁghter jets. Russia\nresponded the same day “Arctic Challenge” began, putting\nthree times as many soldiers and 250 aircraft into the ﬁeld in\nthe northern Komi Republic and Russia’s Central Military\nDistrict (Conley and Rohloff 2015; Russia Begins 2015).\n\nIn terms of shifts in military doctrine, over the summer of\n2015 Russia’s Security Council was reportedly revising its\n2014 military doctrine, which now mentions NATO as a\nprimary threat to Russia’s security interests. The impetus for\nthe more recent revision appears to have been the Obama\nadministration’s 2015 national security document and a\nfollow-up Pentagon national military strategy document, in\nwhich Russia’s aggression, belligerence, and coercion are\nlisted as particular problems in securing the peace in Central\nand Eastern Europe (National Security Strategy 2015).\nAccording to Sergei Rogov, director of the Institute of\nU.S. and Canada Studies, these new U.S. policies provide\n“doctrinal support for the United States’ new Cold War\nagainst Russia,” and Russia’s leaders will be taking this\ninto consideration as they revise Russia’s military doctrine,\nleading to a “sharp aggravation of Russian–U.S. relations\n[that] will certainly be reﬂected in it” (Zamyatina 2015). As\nsuch, the Marine Doctrine published in late July emphasized\nas major foci strengthening Russia’s sea power in both the\nArctic and the Atlantic Oceans; according to Deputy Prime\nMinister Rogozin, “We emphasize [these areas] because\nNATO has been developing actively of late and coming\ncloser to our borders, and Russia is of course responding\nto these developments” (Russian Federation 2015).\n\nTensions continued to mount throughout the summer as\nthe most recent exercises by NATO forces in August and\nSeptember 2015 again provoked a strong Russian rebuke.\nResponding to both NATO’s ﬁrst deployment of its newly\ncreated Very High Readiness Joint Task Force and its\n“Immediate Response 2015” exercises, Ivan Soltanovskii,\nthe director of the Russian Foreign Ministry’s European\nCooperation Department, stated, “We have a negative atti-\ntude to the mounting military activity of NATO in the areas\nbordering our country and are observing a growth in the\nnumber and the scope of exercises which are being held\nunder the NATO aegis…. The enhancement of this military\npresence and infrastructure near our borders has an obvious\nprovocative nature” (Diplomat: Rise 2015).\n\nIn addition to these developments, the tarnished relation-\nship between Russia and the West in the Arctic in both the\nsecurity and economic realms poisons perceptions in other\narenas, with each rhetorical ﬂourish or action taken by one\nside eliciting a yet more ruinous response from the other.\nFor example, some Russian experts now believe that the\nUnited States will use its chairmanship of the Arctic Council\nin 2015–2017 in order to create conﬂict in the body, conﬂict\nthat will be injurious to Russia’s status, more generally, and\nits interests in the AC, more speciﬁcally;31 others believe\nRussia’s renewed claims before the UN Commission on the\nLimitation of the Continental Shelf (CLCS) will not get a\n\nfair hearing,32 due to the current political situation over\nUkraine (Alexandrova 2015; Bovt 2015).33\n\nBesides ramping up military pressures in the Far North,\nthe Russian regime also signaled its displeasure with the\nWest by appointing a fervent nationalist to head the newly\nIn this capacity, Dmitrii\nformed Arctic Commission.\nRogozin, a longtime representative of the national patriotic\nmovement in Russia,34 could be counted on by the regime\nto make incendiary, provocative comments sure to make\nheadlines both at home and abroad; as such, Rogozin—not\nknown to be an exceptionally effective manager or major\ndecision-maker on Arctic affairs35—was nonetheless conse-\nquential in drawing attention to the regime’s more assertive\napproach to Arctic matters. In that regard, then, Rogozin’s\nnationalist bravado served to support Putinist appeals to\nnational pride and patriotism in the Far North, but also to\nsignal to the West Russia’s irritation with the West’s unwill-\ningness to recognize and honor its status there.\n\nRogozin had been increasing his involvement in Arctic\nissues since 2012; for example, at a Naval Collegium meet-\ning that year, Rogozin argued vociferously for the regime to\nset achievable goals for securing Russia’s national interests\nin the Arctic, arguing “If we don’t do that, we will lose the\nbattle for resources, which means we’ll also lose in a big\nbattle for the right to have sovereignty and independence.”36\nA few months later, the deputy prime minister expanded on\nthis theme, expressing his concerns that developing the\nArctic shelf will certainly lead to conﬂicts of\ninterest\nbetween Arctic and non-Arctic states that\nits\nresources. Ominously, Rogozin added that “It is possible\nthis conﬂict will exceed the diplomatic limits…. It is also\nquite possible that Russian oil and gas production facilities\nmay become targets of secret acts of sabotage by rival\ncountries.”37 To be sure, Rogozin perceives the West as\nboth coveting the economic assets of Russia’s Arctic and\nat the same time wanting to check Russia’s developmental\nadvances there, seeing that as something that would only\nstrengthen the Russian state.38\n\nsought\n\nHowever, with his elevation to head the newly created\nState Commission for Arctic Development\nin February\n2015, Rogozin now became ofﬁcially the principal interlo-\ncutor on all political, economic, and military questions con-\ncerning Russia’s plans in the Arctic.39 Within a week of the\nArctic Commission’s initial meeting, the deputy prime min-\nister caustically tied together the events in Ukraine in 2014\nwith the government’s growing activities in the Arctic, say-\nreuniﬁcation of\ning, “Last year, we had the historical\nSevastopol and the Crimea. This year, we present a new\nview and new powerful stress on the development of the\nArctic. Basically, it is all about the same thing” (Staalesen\n2015b). Similarly corrosive comments were made by\nthe opening of a new Arctic drift station,\nRogozin at\nNorth Pole—2015, at which the Commission chair men-\ntioned a number of problems in the Arctic that even\nSoviet planners had not been able to resolve, stating, “But\n\n\fnow the accent should be put on the fact that we should go\nto the Arctic now and forever and make it our own.”\n\nDuring the same visit, Rogozin’s trip to the North Pole by\nway of Spitsbergen caused a diplomatic spat of sorts with the\nNorwegian government.40 Since Rogozin had been placed on\na list of sanctioned Russian government ofﬁcials, Norway\nclaimed that his brief visit to the island on his way northward\nwas prohibited, despite the fact that the 1920 Treaty provides\nvisa-free access to Russians. The Norwegian government\nsummoned the Russian ambassador for consultations, arguing\nthe visa-free regime was being reevaluated. Having returned\nto Russia, Rogozin responded that the Norwegian govern-\nment was envious that “we went swimming at the north\nPole,” and compared Norwegian diplomats to “ostriches,\nhiding their heads in the snow.” He later tweeted that “The\nArctic is a Russian Mecca” (Dmitrii Rogozin probralsia\n2015; Rogozin sravnil Norvegiiu 2015).41 Thus, having the\nnationalist hardliner serve as the public face of Russia’s\nArctic strategy allows the regime to not only focus national\npride and patriotism on such efforts, but also to underscore\nthe regime’s dismay, in attempts to have its status acknowl-\nedged and respected by the West.\n\nCONCLUSIONS\n\nSuch statements and actions from the newly appointed\nprincipal front man of Russia’s Arctic strategy will undoubt-\nedly prove consequential for how Russia conducts itself in\nthe region and how its relationship with Western countries\nthe sanctions\nover Arctic matters develops. Of course,\nis on severely restricting Russia’s\nregime, focused as it\nfreedom of maneuver in the Arctic, also works against a\nmore cooperative relationship with the West. Perhaps most\nimportant, however, is the Kremlin’s increased perception of\nthreat emanating from the West—and the United States\nmost especially—in the wake of the Ukrainian events. As\nthis analysis demonstrates, in the eyes of those governing\nlocalized only on the western\nis not\nthis threat\nRussia,\napproaches to Russia, but\nin the\nis surely also present\nextreme North—Russia’s formerly sole impregnable border,\nbut now, due to climate change, becoming ever more vul-\nis reﬂective of the\nnerable. Each of these factors,\nregime’s renewed focus on national pride and patriotism\nand its disappointment and disenchantment with Western\npolicies that disregard Russia’s status and interests.\n\ntoo,\n\nAt the same time, there appears to be some ongoing pro-\ngress in fashioning a more constructive relationship between\nRussia and its Arctic neighbors. For example, in the last two\nyears Russia has acceded to two sets of rules governing polar\nshipping. The most recent of these, the Polar Code issued by\nthe International Maritime Organization in May 2015, restricts\nmethods by which ships traversing the Arctic region can dis-\npose of wastes. Another example of successful cooperation in\nthe region is the July 2015 signing of the ban on unregulated\n\nPUTIN’S ARCTIC STRATEGY AFTER UKRAINE\n\n11\n\nﬁshing in the central Arctic’s “donut hole”—an area the size of\nthe Mediterranean Sea—by the ﬁve Arctic littoral states,\nRussia included. Last, Russia’s August 2015 revised submis-\nsion of claims to an additional 1.2 million square miles of the\nArctic to the UN Commission on the Limitation of the\nContinental Shelf demonstrates the country’s commitment to\npursuing such claims in accordance with the only process\nrecognized as legitimate under international law; as such,\nsubmitting such claims to the body can be considered neither\nas “neo-imperial,” nor as an attempt at “nationalization,” as\nsome critics have contended (McCain 2015). Thus, while the\nlast several years have been characterized by greater conﬂict in\nthe region, opportunities for cooperation have not been\nspurned.\n\nStill, since the latter half of 2015,\n\nit appears the\nKremlin’s approach to the Far North has moderated some-\nwhat, with Arctic matters becoming more peripheral than\nbefore, and cooperative overtures—rather\nthan caustic\nremarks—more apparent\nthan in previous years. Indeed,\nRussia’s new National Security Strategy mentions the\nArctic only twice, less prominently than before, and argues\nfor “equal and mutually beneﬁcial international coopera-\ntion” in the region.42\n\nThe regime may have calculated that the beneﬁts accru-\ning from a more confrontational approach pale in compar-\nison to the costs, particularly in the current energy\nlevels,\nenvironment. With hydrocarbon prices at current\nthe vast majority of Arctic production remains unproﬁtable.\nBoth private and public Russian oil and gas companies are\nhaving to radically reduce their capital investment plans.\nEven if the price of oil were to increase signiﬁcantly,\nWestern sanctions leave Russian companies without\nthe\nneeded technologies and know-how to develop projects in\nthis inhospitable, challenging environment (Ampilov 2014).\nAs well, the sanctions regime robs the energy sector of\nWestern investment, while the Russian government is only\nable to fund the most strategically important projects due to\nsevere budget shortfalls.43 In addition, international interest\nin the Northern Sea Route has plummeted since 2014, with\ngross tonnage transits falling by almost 80 percent over the\nprior year. Since then, further declines have been noted,\nwith safety concerns, ecological risks, and costs being\ncited as causes.44\n\nConsequently, given all of these risks, it is not assured\nthat Russia’s Arctic strategy will be an economic windfall\nfor Kremlin coffers.45 The political beneﬁts accruing to the\nregime from “mastering” the Arctic regions may also be in\ndoubt, as the perceived accomplishments depend in no small\nmeasure upon the return on investment. If the Arctic strat-\negy comes to naught, it may actually serve to undercut the\ncurrent regime’s legitimacy further, rather than serve as an\nemblem of Russian exceptionalism and achievement as well\nas a wellspring of national pride.\n\nIn addition, Russia’s attempt to augment its status via a\nmulti-faceted Arctic initiative may bring some political\n\n\f12\n\nROTNEM\n\nbeneﬁt vis-à-vis international rivals elsewhere, but relying\non isolationist rhetoric and military posturing in the region\nin Russia’s never having the opportunity to\nmay result\neffectively develop its Arctic holdings (Laruelle 2014a).\nThus, certain economic, political, and international con-\nstraints may outweigh the importance of the Arctic as a\ntool for either status augmentation or domestic legitimacy.\n\nNOTES\n1. There are ﬁve littoral states to the Arctic Ocean: the United States,\nCanada, Denmark, Norway, and the Russian Federation. The “Arctic\n5,” along with Finland, Sweden, and Iceland, countries that lie within\nthe Arctic Circle, but do not have a littoral presence on the Arctic\nOcean, constitute a broader category of countries known as Arctic\nstates. A variety of other, non-Arctic countries have demonstrated\nincreasing interest\nincluding South Korea, Japan,\nChina, and India. It is interesting to note that China considers itself\na “near Arctic state.”\n\nin the region,\n\n2. The latter effort has become even more important since the demon-\nlarge cities in\n\nstrations in Moscow, St. Petersburg, and other\n2011–2012.\n\n3. Exploration and extraction of oil and gas deposits in Russia’s Arctic\nregions were inherently risky endeavors in the past due to the poor\nmapping of potential deposit sites (Long et al. 2008), the signiﬁcant\nuncertainties concerning actual investment levels in Arctic production,\ncertain technological problems and operational\nrisks (Emmerson\n2012), and the complexity and cost of resource extraction (Keil 2013).\nIn summer 2012, Arctic Sea ice was at a record low level (Holland\n2014).\n\n4.\n\n5. Lukin notes that 100 percent of Russia’s indigenous diamonds (as\nwell as 40 percent of its gold reserves) are located in the Russian\nArctic (2013, 9).\n\n7.\n\n6. Marlene Laruelle notes that\n\nthe overwhelming majority of the\nworld’s reserves of platinum, nickel, and cobalt, and over 60 percent\nof copper reserves, are to be found in the Arctic, especially in Russia\nand Canada, while Russia’s Arctic contains the world’s second\nlargest rare earth deposits, after China’s (2014a, 152, 154).\nIt is important to note that many of the earliest assessments of the\nArctic’s vast “undiscovered” hydrocarbon deposits turned out to be\nerroneous; both Gazprom and Rosneft have been forced recently to\nscale back their earlier investment plans accordingly (Kennedy 2015).\n8. Additional areas of high likelihood of fossil fuel resource potential\nare located immediately north of the Alaskan/Canadian coastline\n(Beaufort Sea), and to the west and east of Greenland.\nIn 2014 Russia produced 23 trillion cubic feet of gas and 10.7\nmillion barrels of oil per day (Clemente 2015).\nIn 2011 the Arctic contributed 15 percent to Russia’s GDP and\naccounted for 25 percent of its exports (Lukin 2013). In 2012 it\ncontributed to 11 percent of Russia’s GDP, with 22 percent of its\nexport earnings (Josephson 2014, 350).\nPricewaterhouseCoopers maintains that the Russian Federation is\nresponsible for discovering 43 of the 61 largest natural gas and oil\nﬁelds in the Arctic Circle to date (Clemente 2015).\n\n9.\n\n10.\n\n11.\n\n12. Of course, such future capital expenditure plans by Rosneft, as well as\nthose of other major oil and gas producers like Gazprom, Novatek, and\nLukoil, are highly dependent upon the future price of oil and gas, the\ncourse of Western sanctions, and emerging energy industry dynamics.\nthe Strategy for\nDeveloping the Arctic Zone to 2020, and the Strategy for\nDeveloping Port Infrastructure to 2030.\n\n13. These include the Transport Strategy to 2030,\n\n14. By comparison, the United States has only two icebreakers, with\n\nonly one actually operational at any one time.\n\n15. Discussing the importance of honor to domestic political support,\nSwart avers that honor was “central to the authority of the prince and\nthe polity” (2016).\n\n16. Laruelle demonstrates that among other values espoused by a number\nof independent Russian patriotic youth clubs, the need for order and\nstability was one value that is also espoused by Kremlin ideologists\n(Laruelle 2015a).\n\n17. Recent polls indicate anti-American and anti-Western sentiments are\non the rise among Russians; in May 2015 a Levada Center poll\nrevealed that 60 percent of the population believe the United States\nis a serious threat to Russia, and in August 2015 a VTsIOM poll\ndemonstrated that 70 percent of Russians harbored negative senti-\nments about America (Coyer 2015; Majority of Russians Say 2015).\n18. A recent poll conducted by the Levada Center demonstrates that 40\npercent of those polled agree that Russians are a “special people.” In\n2000, only a third agreed with the statement (Kirby 2015).\n\n19. Russia’s winning bid for the 2018 FIFA World Cup is a further\n\n20.\n\nemployment of this approach.\nIn his book The Conquest of the Russian Arctic, Paul Josephson\nconcurs that gaining superpower status and legitimacy were impor-\ntant motivating factors for elites’ refocusing their attention on the\nArctic (2014, 356).\n\n21. Among others, sponsors routinely include the Ministry of Natural\nthe Russian\n\nResources and Ecology,\nAcademy of Sciences, and the Russian Geographic Society.\n\nthe Federation Council,\n\n22. Each of these can be viewed on YouTube.\n23.\n\nIt is perhaps possible that the regime’s efforts at status augmentation\nand great power myth-making—in the Arctic and elsewhere—are\nhaving an effect. Indeed, according to a recent Levada Center poll,\nroughly two-thirds of Russians see their country today as a great\npower, a higher percentage than at any time since 1999 (Pozitsii\nRossii 2015).\n\n25.\n\n24. Claims made by Victoria Nuland, the assistant secretary of state for\nEuropean and Eurasian affairs, in a December 2013 speech concern-\ning U.S. ﬁnancial support for democratic change in Ukraine, only\nheightened such fears.\nSpeaking at a Marine Board meeting in late 2012, Dmitrii Rogozin,\ndeputy prime minister in charge of the defense industry and former\nenvoy to NATO in 2008–2011, stated that at nearly every alliance\nmeeting, the subject of the Arctic and its changing environment\nwould be discussed. Plans were discussed by NATO members to\nsend ships to the vicinity of Russia’s Northern Sea Route, allegedly\nto assist with civil navigation. Instead, Rogozin assumed that,\n“Clearly, an attempt to undermine international regulations that pro-\nvide for cooperation and security in the Arctic Zone is behind [that\nrhetoric]” (Battle for, 2012).\n\n26. Before climate change made the northern border more vulnerable,\nRussia did not have to fear foreign encroachment in the High North\nas much as it had to on its western, southern, and eastern borders,\nwhere Moscow faced the triple threat of NATO advancement, Islamic\nterrorism, and Chinese migration.\n\n27. According to a recent report in The Wall Street Journal, U.S. sanc-\ntions on OAO Novatek, Russia’s largest independent producer of\nnatural gas, have seriously undermined the enormous $27 billion\nYamal gas project in the Arctic (Marson and Williams 2015).\nIn all, Russia plans to spend twenty trillion rubles on new weapons\nprocurement from 2011 to 2020 (Isachenkov 2015).\n\n28.\n\n29. However, at the same meeting Putin was also careful to point out that\n“we are interested in stable development of the region, based on coop-\neration and respect for international rights” (Putin na zasedanii 2014).\n30. Even some Western defense analysts would agree that Russia’s\nrenewed emphasis on the Arctic is less a new offensive than a\nresponse to NATO and a desire to protect economic interests in the\nregion (Sergunin and Konyshev 2015). At least one U.S. military\nofﬁcial has also apparently downplayed Russia’s military activities in\n\n\f31.\n\n32.\n\nthe region; commenting a few days prior to the beginning of NATO’s\n“Arctic Challenge Exercise” in May 2015, U.S. Coast Guard\nAdmiral Robert J. Papp Jr. said, concerning Russian activities in\nthe Arctic, “Everything we have seen them doing so far is lawful,\nconsidered, and deliberative. So we’ll just continue monitoring it and\nnot overreact to it” (New Security 2015).\nStatements by Senator John McCain arguing that the United States\nshould use its chairmanship of the AC as an opportunity to “make\nrecognition of Mr. Putin’s hegemonic ambitions a top priority” do\nnot help moderate such beliefs (McCain 2015).\nFor proof, skeptics may point to statements made by Representative\nEd Royce, the chairman of the House Committee on Foreign Affairs,\nwho remarked concerning Russia’s permissible, renewed application\nbefore the UN’s CLCS for claims extending beyond its exclusive\neconomic zone, “Russia has been aggressively pushing its claims to\nthe Arctic, especially the resource-rich continental shelf…. The U.S.\nand others bordering the Arctic must maintain a united front against\nMoscow’s aggressive ambitions toward this vital region” (Lossan\n2015).\n\n33. As the deputy director of the Marine Geology Division at Russian\nAcademy of Sciences’ Oceanology Institute, Professor Leopold\nLobkovsky, stated, “Russia’s bid puts winds into the sails of\nDenmark and Canada’s bids…. If this model is passed, the ridge\ncan be shared among the three countries…. Unfortunately, this is not\nhappening, they do not want to interact with us” (Russian Arctic\nShelf 2015).\n\n34. Rogozin was appointed deputy prime minister in charge of the\ndefense industry in December 2011, and has been a hawkish sup-\nporter of the defense industry since. Before his elevation, Rogozin\nhad been an ardent supporter of ethnic Russians living in former\nSoviet republics; indeed, after the collapse of the Soviet Union,\nthe Congress of Russian\nRogozin was an early supporter of\nCommunities, a nationalist political grouping that desired to leverage\nstate support for the defense of Russian minorities now living abroad,\nin addition to revising Russian borders and integrating Russian-\nmajority areas of neighboring states into a greater union.\n\nElected to the State Duma in 1997, he also occupied several high-\nproﬁle positions in foreign affairs committees, effectively using such\nposts to caustically defend Russia’s interests. Rogozin headed ﬁrst\nthe Duma’s committee in charge of relations with the Parliamentary\nAssembly to the Council of Europe,\nlater chairing a committee\ncharged with responsibility for the Kaliningrad region and EU\nexpansion.\nIn 2003, Rogozin forged the “Rodina” or “Motherland” party, a\nnationalist-patriotic alternative to the CPSU, and captured ten percent\nof the vote in the 2004 parliamentary elections; the party was banned\nfrom taking part in the 2005 Moscow city elections, due to its rabid\nanti-immigrant campaign message, and was later disbanded by the\nKremlin in 2006, as some considered the movement a political threat\n(Laruelle 2015b). However, President Putin later rewarded Rogozin\nwith an appointment as Russia’s ambassador to NATO in 2007,\nperhaps valuing him for his ability to champion—not always very\ndiplomatically—the country’s security interests. Indeed, Rogozin’s\ncharge at this time included defending Russia’s position during the\n2008 war in the Caucasus, objecting to missile defense plans in\nEastern Europe, and opposing NATO expansion eastward.\n\n35. The author wishes to thank several reviewers for these observations.\n“Battle for Arctic Key for Russia’s Sovereignty—Rogozin,” Russia\n36.\nToday, December 4, 2012.\n“Rogozin: Active Development of Arctic Shelf to Cause Conﬂict of\nInterest, Sabotage,” Interfax, June 28, 2013.\nIn late 2013, speaking about NATO’s interest in the Arctic at a Naval\nCollegium, Rogozin stated, “Naturally, military activity every time is\n\n37.\n\n38.\n\nPUTIN’S ARCTIC STRATEGY AFTER UKRAINE\n\n13\n\nreally just a cloak for economic ambitions. … In 2009–2010 in\nBrussels, Washington, Ottawa, and other capitals, there was an active\ndiscussion of plans to build up military activities in the Arctic\nregion” (“Rogozin ob osvoenii Arktiki” 2013). The following year,\nRogozin argued that the sale of Alaska was a “betrayal of Russian\npower status,” and that Russia maintained the “right to reclaim our\nlost colonies” (Peterrsen 2015).\n\n39. The commission’s purpose is to evaluate the resource base of the\nregion, develop an investment program for the exploitation of these\nresources, and develop policies for the Arctic in the socio-economic,\npolitical, and military realms (Rogozin vozglavil 2015). According to\nLukin, the origins of the commission grew out of a meeting of the\nExpert Council on the Arctic and Antarctic in 2012, chaired by A.N.\nChilingarov (Lukin 2013, 138). The government began intensive\ndiscussions regarding the commission’s creation as early as July\n2014 (Dmitrii Rogozin, 2015).\n\n41.\n\n40. According to the 1920 Treaty of Spitsbergen, Norway has sovereign\ncontrol over the island and the other isles making up the Svalbard\narchipelago. Although the treaty assigns sovereignty to Norway,\ncontracting parties to the agreement enjoy equal rights—subject to\nlocal law—to engage in maritime, mining, and industrial practices on\nor about the islands making up the archipelago.\nIn summer 2015 Rogozin made headlines again, declaring at the\nannual Tekhnoprom International Forum—which he both founded\nand chairs—that the next year’s forum would concentrate on the\nproblems of mastering the Arctic region, saying that “We plan to\nmake our people rejoice, and our adversaries upset, with the con-\nstruction of a few new exemplars of military technology—ﬁrst of all\nthe creation of new aviation complexes, new rocket forces, and a\npowerful ﬂeet” (Bolotin 2015).\n“O Strategii Natsional’noi Bezopasnosti Rossiiskoi Federatsii,”\nDecember 31, 2015.\n\n42.\n\n44.\n\n43. Although the government’s “pivot to Asia” was designed in part to\ngenerate greater Chinese interest—and more recently the Russian\nlegislature has made it easier for such foreign investors to own or\ncontrol more of the production of new hydrocarbon discoveries—to\ndate, Chinese interest has not met expectations.\nIt is important to note, though, that while international interest has\nfallen, domestic usage has increased year on year, with cargo turnover\nincreasing by 37 percent between 2014 and 2015 (Pettersen 2016).\nthere is increasing evidence that senior ofﬁcials in the\nIndeed,\nRussian government are re-evaluating the worth of extensive new\nhydrocarbon projects in the country’s frontiers, believing Russia’s\ndependency on oil and gas prices to be a major threat to its national\nsecurity; Prime Minister Dmitrii Medvedev, former Chief of Staff\nSergei Ivanov, and Security Council Secretary Nikolai Patrushev\nhave recently all separately voiced such concerns.\n\n45.\n\nORCID\n\nThomas E. Rotnem http://orcid.org/0000-0002-0049-0279\n\nREFERENCES\n\nAlexandrova, Liudmila. 2015. “Russia Would Like to See Border of Its\nArctic Shelf Expanded.” TASS, August 5, at http://tass.ru/en/opinions/\n812810, accessed August 7, 2015.\n\nAmpilov, Yurii. 2014. “Osvoenie shel’fa Arktiki\n\ni Dal’nego Vostoka:\nProblemy i perspektivi” (The Development of the Arctic Shelf and the\nFar East: Problems andPerspectives). Offshore (Russia) 4, no. 6: 8–15.\n\n\f14\n\nROTNEM\n\n“Arctic Resources Central to Russia’s Energy Security—Medvedev.” 2008.\nSputnik News, September 17, at http://sputniknews.com/russia/\n20080917/116894260.html, accessed December 14, 2014.\n\n“Arktika—goriachaia tochka XXI veka” (The Arctic: The 21st Century\nHotspot). 2007. Komsomol’skaia pravda, April 23, at www.kp.ru/daily/\n23892/66464, accessed May 14, 2015.\n\nBaev, Pavel. 2013. “Russia’s Arctic Ambitions and Anxieties.” Current\n\nHistory 112: 265–70.\n\nBarbashin, Anton, and Hannah Thoburn. 2004. “Putin’s Brain: Alexander\nDugin and the Philosophy behind Putin’s Invasion of Crimea.” Foreign\nAffairs, March 13, at https://www.foreignaffairs.com/articles/russia-fsu/\n2014-03-31/putins-brain, accessed May 27, 2015.\n\n“Battle for Arctic Key for Russia’s Sovereignty—Rogozin” 2012. RT,\nDecember 4, at www.rt.com/politics/arctic-sovereignty-r