c, 46–47, 98, 113\npolitical, 96, 188\nsocial importance of, 48, 179, 181\n\nwhale biology, 192–95\nwhalers, 179–97\nwhaling, 10, 20, 28, 97–99, 176–97,\n\n207\n\nWildavsky, Aaron, 13, 18, 37, 177–78,\n\n187–99, 200\n\nWynne, Brian, 14–15, 178\n\nY\nyouth, 100\n\n32, 37–38, 58–83\n\nresilience, 103–16\n\nV\nVirkkala, Ann-Maria, 77\nvitalism, 2, 5–6\nvoice, x, 26, 28, 31, 165\neclipsing of, 2, 5, 43\n\n  well-being, 38, 43, 104–12, 114–16\nYukon, 109, 134–35, 138\nYup’ik, Yupiit, 17, 38, 51, 105, 109\n\nThis open access edition has been made available under a CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 license thanks to the support of Knowledge Unlatched and the European Research Council (ERC) under the European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme (Grant agreement No. 856543). https://doi.org/10.3167/9781800735934. Not for resale.\n\n	skipped	{"skipped_at": "2025-08-11T22:38:35.113Z", "skip_reason": "All 16 terms were duplicates of already-processed terms"}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	e770bd514cb552df0e651b8043a2c891f6a5318d30c9667538f5652c438ce3cd	\N	markitdown	gpt-4.1	tier_1	171346	684975	{"routing_info":{"status":"tier_1","model":"gpt-4.1","estimated_tokens":171346,"limit":380000,"reason":"Content fits in Tier 1 model (gpt-4.1)","cost_tier":"standard"},"original_metadata":{"service":"Ingest::TokenAwareMarkitdownService","file_path":"/Volumes/jer4TBv3/enliterator/arctic_research/Risky Futures- Climate, Geopolitics, and Local Realities_Ulturgasheva (Uncertain Circumpolar North Series).pdf","file_size_bytes":6643487,"content_length_chars":684975,"estimated_tokens":171346,"timestamp":"2025-08-11T22:07:51Z","supported_formats":[".pdf",".docx",".pptx",".xlsx",".md",".txt",".html",".rtf",".odt",".jpg",".jpeg",".png",".bmp",".gif",".svg"]},"detection_result":{"media_type":"document","markitdown_supported":true,"mime_type":"application/pdf","file_extension":".pdf"},"processed_at":"2025-08-11T22:07:51Z"}
17	1	\N	\N	\N	0af212af247e8380938cba2a18837c3b2b98ead00d6c624ff04eb73f13f69627	/Volumes/jer4TBv3/enliterator/arctic_research/Russia's Arctic icebreaking capability-an assessment_Aliyev.pdf	\N	text	completed	2125910	Defense & Security Analysis\n\nISSN: (Print) (Online) Journal homepage: www.tandfonline.com/journals/cdan20\n\nRussia’s Arctic icebreaking capability: an assessment\n\nNurlan Aliyev\n\nTo cite this article: Nurlan Aliyev (2024) Russia’s Arctic icebreaking capability: an assessment,\nDefense & Security Analysis, 40:3, 405-429, DOI: 10.1080/14751798.2024.2365542\n\nTo link to this article:  https://doi.org/10.1080/14751798.2024.2365542\n\nPublished online: 23 Jul 2024.\n\nSubmit your article to this journal\n\nArticle views: 271\n\nView related articles\n\nView Crossmark data\n\nFull Terms & Conditions of access and use can be found at\nhttps://www.tandfonline.com/action/journalInformation?journalCode=cdan20\n\n\fDEFENSE & SECURITY ANALYSIS\n2024, VOL. 40, NO. 3, 405–429\nhttps://doi.org/10.1080/14751798.2024.2365542\n\nRussia’s Arctic icebreaking capability: an assessment\nNurlan Aliyev\n\nFaculty of Social Science, University of Economics and Human Sciences in Warsaw, Warsaw, Poland\n\nKEYWORDS\nIcebreakers; Arctic; Russia’s\nArctic strategy; dual -use\ncapabilities; navy; civilian\nicebreakers\n\nABSTRACT\nThis paper explores the Russian icebreaker ﬂeet capabilities,\nproblems, and perspectives. It also analyses Russian civilian and\nmilitary icebreakers, and their dual-use capabilities. The paper\nresponds to a question grounded in Russia’s Arctic policy: How\ncritical is icebreaking capability to Russia’s Arctic strategy? Based\non the ﬁndings, the paper concludes that the inﬂuences of\nclimate change and Russia’s foreign and external security policy\naﬀect the development of its icebreaker ﬂeet in the Arctic. In this\nrespect, temperatures of the ice melting in the Arctic and delays\nin building new icebreakers, because of access to the technology\ncaused by the sanctions, are stressed. On the other hand, Russia’s\nconfrontation with the West following the invasion of Ukraine\nand the sanctions over it, increases the allocation of government\nﬁnance to support local shipbuilding companies. Moreover, the\npaper argues that Russia’s economic diﬃculties and shipbuilding\ndelays should enhance the dual-use capabilities of the Russian\nicebreakers in the coming years.\n\nSince Soviet times the Russian icebreaker ﬂeet has been described, both in and outside of\nRussia, as one of the main factors of Russia’s strength in the Arctic. Interest in Russia’s\nicebreaker ﬂeet has grown since the 2010s again – especially in the last few years – as\nMoscow’s economic and security involvement in the region has increased. Moreover,\nRussia’s 2022 invasion of Ukraine would further aﬀect the Russian economic projects\nin the Arctic, including the Northern Sea Route and its icebreaker ﬂeet. But amidst\nthe deterioration of the Russian economy, the dual-use capabilities of the icebreakers\nmay increase. Concerns regarding Russia’s power projection into the Arctic and\nTrans-Atlantic, worries about Russia’s purported intentions to control the Northern\nSea Route, makes it necessary to develop a better understanding of the Russian icebreaker\nﬂeet’s capabilities and intention.\n\nIn this respect, this paper aims to explore the Russian icebreaker ﬂeet capabilities,\nproblems, and perspectives on its development. The paper explores the use of icebreakers\nby the Russian military and also, how civilian icebreakers and infrastructure are exploited\nby the military. The paper identiﬁes research questions grounded in Russia’s Arctic\npolicy: How critical is its icebreaking capability to Russia’s Arctic strategy? Based on\nthe paper’s ﬁndings, it is clear that the inﬂuences of climate change and Russia’s\nforeign and external security policy aﬀect the development of its icebreaker ﬂeet in the\n\nCONTACT Nurlan Aliyev\n© 2024 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group\n\naliyevnurlan13@gmail.com\n\n@anurlan\n\n\f406\n\nN. ALIYEV\n\nArctic. On the other hand, Russia’s confrontation with the West, with its invasion of\nUkraine and the sanctions over it, will force an increase in government ﬁnance to dom-\nestic shipbuilding companies. Furthermore, Russia’s economic diﬃculties and shipbuild-\ning delays may enhance the dual-use capabilities of old and new Russian icebreakers in\nthe coming years.\n\nIn methodological terms, the paper employs qualitative methods. The research uses\ncontent analysis regarding oﬃcial documents, political and military elite speeches, inter-\nviews, and reports to explain Russia’s oﬃcial positions and policies regarding both the\nmilitary and civilian icebreaker ﬂeets, and their use in the Arctic. Development of the ice-\nbreakers, their problems, eﬀects of the invasion of Ukraine on the construction of ice-\nbreakers, and their dual-use capabilities are analysed in this paper. Primary and\nsecondary sources are used to analyse developments regarding the Russian icebreakers\nin the Arctic. Interviews and reports from Russian and Western media are also used.\nThe paper uses oﬃcial information, reports, and interviews.\n\nHistory\n\nNo nation is interested in icebreakers as much as Russia. (Vice Admiral Stepa	{"filename": "Russia's Arctic icebreaking capability-an assessment_Aliyev.pdf", "file_size": 2125910, "document_type": "research_paper"}	{}	\N	2025-08-11 21:46:06.911773	2025-08-11 22:38:35.115055	extracted	{"terms_count": 15, "extracted_at": "2025-08-11T22:13:56.771Z"}	Defense & Security Analysis\n\nISSN: (Print) (Online) Journal homepage: www.tandfonline.com/journals/cdan20\n\nRussia’s Arctic icebreaking capability: an assessment\n\nNurlan Aliyev\n\nTo cite this article: Nurlan Aliyev (2024) Russia’s Arctic icebreaking capability: an assessment,\nDefense & Security Analysis, 40:3, 405-429, DOI: 10.1080/14751798.2024.2365542\n\nTo link to this article:  https://doi.org/10.1080/14751798.2024.2365542\n\nPublished online: 23 Jul 2024.\n\nSubmit your article to this journal\n\nArticle views: 271\n\nView related articles\n\nView Crossmark data\n\nFull Terms & Conditions of access and use can be found at\nhttps://www.tandfonline.com/action/journalInformation?journalCode=cdan20\n\n\fDEFENSE & SECURITY ANALYSIS\n2024, VOL. 40, NO. 3, 405–429\nhttps://doi.org/10.1080/14751798.2024.2365542\n\nRussia’s Arctic icebreaking capability: an assessment\nNurlan Aliyev\n\nFaculty of Social Science, University of Economics and Human Sciences in Warsaw, Warsaw, Poland\n\nKEYWORDS\nIcebreakers; Arctic; Russia’s\nArctic strategy; dual -use\ncapabilities; navy; civilian\nicebreakers\n\nABSTRACT\nThis paper explores the Russian icebreaker ﬂeet capabilities,\nproblems, and perspectives. It also analyses Russian civilian and\nmilitary icebreakers, and their dual-use capabilities. The paper\nresponds to a question grounded in Russia’s Arctic policy: How\ncritical is icebreaking capability to Russia’s Arctic strategy? Based\non the ﬁndings, the paper concludes that the inﬂuences of\nclimate change and Russia’s foreign and external security policy\naﬀect the development of its icebreaker ﬂeet in the Arctic. In this\nrespect, temperatures of the ice melting in the Arctic and delays\nin building new icebreakers, because of access to the technology\ncaused by the sanctions, are stressed. On the other hand, Russia’s\nconfrontation with the West following the invasion of Ukraine\nand the sanctions over it, increases the allocation of government\nﬁnance to support local shipbuilding companies. Moreover, the\npaper argues that Russia’s economic diﬃculties and shipbuilding\ndelays should enhance the dual-use capabilities of the Russian\nicebreakers in the coming years.\n\nSince Soviet times the Russian icebreaker ﬂeet has been described, both in and outside of\nRussia, as one of the main factors of Russia’s strength in the Arctic. Interest in Russia’s\nicebreaker ﬂeet has grown since the 2010s again – especially in the last few years – as\nMoscow’s economic and security involvement in the region has increased. Moreover,\nRussia’s 2022 invasion of Ukraine would further aﬀect the Russian economic projects\nin the Arctic, including the Northern Sea Route and its icebreaker ﬂeet. But amidst\nthe deterioration of the Russian economy, the dual-use capabilities of the icebreakers\nmay increase. Concerns regarding Russia’s power projection into the Arctic and\nTrans-Atlantic, worries about Russia’s purported intentions to control the Northern\nSea Route, makes it necessary to develop a better understanding of the Russian icebreaker\nﬂeet’s capabilities and intention.\n\nIn this respect, this paper aims to explore the Russian icebreaker ﬂeet capabilities,\nproblems, and perspectives on its development. The paper explores the use of icebreakers\nby the Russian military and also, how civilian icebreakers and infrastructure are exploited\nby the military. The paper identiﬁes research questions grounded in Russia’s Arctic\npolicy: How critical is its icebreaking capability to Russia’s Arctic strategy? Based on\nthe paper’s ﬁndings, it is clear that the inﬂuences of climate change and Russia’s\nforeign and external security policy aﬀect the development of its icebreaker ﬂeet in the\n\nCONTACT Nurlan Aliyev\n© 2024 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group\n\naliyevnurlan13@gmail.com\n\n@anurlan\n\n\f406\n\nN. ALIYEV\n\nArctic. On the other hand, Russia’s confrontation with the West, with its invasion of\nUkraine and the sanctions over it, will force an increase in government ﬁnance to dom-\nestic shipbuilding companies. Furthermore, Russia’s economic diﬃculties and shipbuild-\ning delays may enhance the dual-use capabilities of old and new Russian icebreakers in\nthe coming years.\n\nIn methodological terms, the paper employs qualitative methods. The research uses\ncontent analysis regarding oﬃcial documents, political and military elite speeches, inter-\nviews, and reports to explain Russia’s oﬃcial positions and policies regarding both the\nmilitary and civilian icebreaker ﬂeets, and their use in the Arctic. Development of the ice-\nbreakers, their problems, eﬀects of the invasion of Ukraine on the construction of ice-\nbreakers, and their dual-use capabilities are analysed in this paper. Primary and\nsecondary sources are used to analyse developments regarding the Russian icebreakers\nin the Arctic. Interviews and reports from Russian and Western media are also used.\nThe paper uses oﬃcial information, reports, and interviews.\n\nHistory\n\nNo nation is interested in icebreakers as much as Russia. (Vice Admiral Stepan Osipovich\nMakarov)1\nSince the end of the nineteenth century when the ﬁrst Russian icebreaker “Yermak”\nwas built, icebreakers have been an essential part of Russia’s Arctic strategy.2 In 1916,\nthe ﬁrst non-freezing port in the Arctic was founded: Romanov-on-Murman (the\npresent name is Murmansk). By 1915, the ﬁrst Russian icebreaker shipbuilding pro-\ngramme was created.3 The ﬁrst Russian icebreakers, including Yermak, were built in\nEnglish shipyards. In 1932, the USSR established the Main Directorate for the Northern\nSea Route, which took over the order of new icebreakers.4 From 1938–1941, in Leningrad\nand Nikolaev, the ﬁrst four Arctic icebreakers (coal-ﬁred steam icebreakers) in the USSR\nwere built with the lead ship “I. Stalin” (the other three were the “V. Molotov,” “L. Kaga-\nnovich” and “A. Mikoyan”). The planned creation of domestic diesel-electric icebreakers\nin the late 1930s was postponed.5 They were supplied later from the USA under the Lend-\nLease programme. Three icebreakers, “Southwind,” “Northwind,” and “Westwind” were\ntransferred to the USSR in 1944 and early 1945.6 Under the names “Admiral Makarov,”\n“Captain Belousov,” and “North Pole” they operated under the ﬂag of the USSR for\nseveral years and were returned to the United States in 1955. Thus, American vessels\nwere the ﬁrst diesel-electric icebreakers in the Soviet Union. The ﬁrst Soviet diesel-elec-\ntric icebreaker “Moskva” was built in Finland in 1960.7 Soviet-Finnish co-operation in\nshipbuilding began in 1932. The serial construction of icebreakers for the Soviet\nUnion began in 1952, when a Helsinki shipyard received orders for two sea icebreakers\nof 10,500 hp each. The peak of icebreaker construction for the USSR occurred from 1950\nto the 1980s.8 Since the mid-1970s, 21 icebreakers of projects 1101, 1105, 1108 and 1191\nwere built in Finland for the USSR, and 20 of them are still in service.9\n\nAfter WW2, the USSR was actively engaged in the development of atomic energy.\nFirstly, for military purposes, to create nuclear weapons, but simultaneously the propo-\nsals on peaceful use of nuclear energy were received with the utmost interest by the\nSoviets. This resulted in the ﬁrst nuclear-powered icebreaker in the world, the Lenin,\n\n\fDEFENSE & SECURITY ANALYSIS\n\n407\n\nbeing launched in 1959.10 It was not only an indicator of achievement of the Soviets’\nscience and industrial capabilities; it was also promoted by the Communist leadership\nas a symbol of achievement of Communism. Such approaches by the elite regarding\nnuclear-powered icebreakers can still be found in contemporary Russia. In the 1990s,\nthe icebreaker ﬂeet was strongly aﬀected by the economic crisis and a generally dimin-\nished focus on the Arctic by Moscow. The maximum number of the Russian icebreakers\nserviced in the Arctic, both nuclear and diesel-electric, reached 18 in 1989, but in 2002\nthere were 12 operational icebreakers (7 nuclear and 5 diesel-electric icebreakers).11\nHowever, since the mid of 2000s the situation has been changing.\n\nContemporary Russian icebreakers\n\nThe Russian state and non-state-owned icebreaker ﬂeets consist of more than 40 vessels,\nincluding nuclear, diesel-electric, and diesel-powered icebreakers. The Atomﬂot\n(Rosatom)12 lead nuclear-powered icebreaker ﬂeet13 currently includes: two nuclear-\npowered icebreakers, with two nuclear-powered icebreakers with a capacity of 81.5 thou-\nsand hp. (“Arktika” and “Sibir”), two nuclear-powered icebreakers with two-reactor\nnuclear power plants with a capacity of 75 thousand hp. (“Yamal,” “50 Years of\nVictory” [ 50 Let Pobedy], project 10520/10521) and two icebreakers with a one-\nreactor installation, with a capacity of about 50 thousand hp. (“Taimyr,” “Vaygach”).\nThey are complemented by the nuclear-powered container ship “Sevmorput” (reactor\ncapacity – 40 thousand hp). In addition. Rosatomﬂot operates three service vessels and\na container ship “Rossita.” It is also in charge of the port ﬂeet vessels designed to\nserve the water area of the port of Sabetta: ice-class tugs “Pur” and “Tambey”; icebreaking\ntugs Yuribey and Nadym; and the “Ob” port icebreaker.14\n\nThe majority of these icebreakers were built during the time of the Soviet Union and\nthere are now issues regarding their operational longevity. Three icebreakers of the Arctic\nclass, the Siberia, the Russia, and the Soviet Union were decommissioned in 2010s. The\nservice period of the reactor plants on the icebreakers currently in operation has been\nextended and the end of operation of nuclear-powered icebreakers will be: 50 Years of\nVictory – 2039; The Yamal – 2028; The Taimyr 2027; The Vaigach 2026.15 The building\nof nuclear-powered icebreakers for Project 22220 is on-going. Their main task is to\nensure year-round navigation in the Western region of the Arctic. It is planned that\nthe icebreakers of Project 22220 will replace those of projects Arctic and Taimyr. The\nvessels’ dual-draft concept allows for operating both in the Arctic and in the mouths\nof the polar rivers, particularly in the shallower areas of the Yenisei estuary and the\nOb Bay area. However, in the eastern part of the Arctic they can be used only in the\nsummer-autumn period because of more extreme weather and ice conditions in the\nwinter-spring period. The icebreakers can overcome ice obstacles up to 3 metres thick.\nOn 21 October 2020, a state ﬂag raising ceremony was held on the lead universal\nnuclear-powered icebreaker “Arktika.”16 Later it returned to the dockyard several\ntimes for substantial reﬁts. Another icebreaker of project 22220, “Sibir” was handed\nover to Rosatom in December 202117 (power – 60 MW (on shafts)); maximum icebreak-\ning capacity – 2.9 metres; estimated service life – 40 years.18\n\nCurrently three more vessels of the Project 22220 line – Ural, Yakutia (Ob"yedinen-\nnaya sudostroitel’naya korporatsiya, 2022) and Chukotka – are being built at the Baltic\n\n\f408\n\nN. ALIYEV\n\nshipyard facilities.19 In the coming years, the ships of this project will become the basis\nfor the civilian icebreaker ﬂeet of Russia. The icebreakers of Project 22220 are planned to\nhave beams of 34 m, which is 4 metres wider than their “Arktika” class predecessors. It is\nessential for assisting large cargo ships. Moreover, this new class of icebreaker is able to\ncombine the function of a deep-draft icebreaker – necessary for operating in the Central\nArctic – and shallow-draft icebreaker for working in the mouths of the Siberian rivers.\nThis dual-draft icebreaker takes aboard 9000 tons of ballast water and changes its\ndraft from 10.5 to 8.5 metres by discharging ballast water. The power of this class of\nships is up to 60,000 kW.20 According to Deputy General Director of Glavsevmorput\nVladimir Arutyunyan, the Ural will exclusively serve Rosneft’s Vostok Oil project.21\n\nAnother Russian prospective vessel is the “superpower” nuclear-powered icebreaker\nRossiya (Project 10510 – Lider). The vessel was planned to be operational in 2027. Its\nicebreaking capability (maximum) will be 4 metres; displacement: draught as per CWL\nis about 69,700 tons.22 The Rossiya will have two RITM-400 reactors, will be 1.8 times\nmore powerful than the RITM-200 reactors created for the icebreakers of Project\n22220. The icebreaker will be capable of laying a channel about 50 metres wide. Accord-\ning to Rosatom, it will ensure year-round pilotage of large-tonnage vessels throughout\nthe entire water area of the Northern Sea Route.23 The advantage of using nuclear-\npowered icebreakers is their power and icebreaking capabilities. Unlike diesel ice-\nbreakers, they can operate for extended periods without refuelling. On several occasions,\nnuclear-powered icebreakers have remained at sea for nearly 400 days. The nuclear ﬂeet\nproﬁtability is expected to improve with the perspective increase in cargo volume along\nthe northern sea route (NSR).24\n\nMost of the Russian diesel-electric icebreakers25 were built during the Soviet Union\nand their numbers began to decrease due to the end of their operational periods. Cur-\nrently, the Federal state unitary enterprise “Rosmorport”26 operates 33 icebreakers and\nicebreaking tugs, including: 23 linear icebreakers, including 7 linear shallow-draught ice-\nbreakers; and 10 auxiliary and port icebreakers and icebreaking tugs.27\n\nBetween 2008 and 2016, ﬁve diesel-electric icebreakers of Project 21900 with a\ncapacity of about 18 MW were accepted for service. In 2015–2016 the construction of\nthree 16 MW diesel-powered icebreakers was completed, 8 icebreakers of various\ncapacities from 6.8 to 60 MW are currently under construction.28 In 2020, the “Viktor\nChernomyrdin” was accepted by Rosmorport for service. It is a diesel-electric linear ice-\nbreaker with a capacity of 25 MW, and according to Rosmorport, it is “the largest and\nmost powerful non-nuclear icebreaker in the world.”29 It is designed to ensure uninter-\nrupted pilotage of ships in the Gulf of Finland, and can operate in the Arctic and Ant-\narctic. The main operator of diesel-electric icebreakers is Rosmorport, but several\nvessels are owned by the Lena United River Shipping Company,30 the carrier on the\nsection of the Northern Sea Route from Khatanga Bay to Pevek, and Norilsk Nickel\nwhich has own Arctic ﬂeet since 2006.31\n\nCorporate icebreakers32\n\nIn recent years, state and private companies involved in the exploration of natural\nresources in the Arctic have actively developed their own icebreaker capabilities, or\ninvested in Rosatom’s icebreaker building projects. In 2018 and 2019, Andrey Vilkitsky\n\n\fTable 1. Contemporary Russian icebreakers – nuclear powered icebreakers (The Table includes Russian operational nuclear-powered icebreakers).\n\nIcebreaker\n“Arktika”\n\n“Siberia”\n\n“Ural”\n\n“Yamal”\n\n“50 Let Pobedy”\n\n“Taimyr”\n\n“Vaygach”\n\n“Sevmorput,” the nuclear-\npowered container ship\n\nSource: Atomﬂot – rosatom.ru.\n\nYear of\ncommissioning\n\n2020\n\n2021\n\n2022\n\n1992\n\n2007\n\n1989\n\n1989\n\n1988\n\nBuilding shipyard\n\nBaltic Shipyard, Saint\nPetersburg, Russia\nBaltic Shipyard, Saint\nPetersburg, Russia\nBaltic Shipyard, Saint\nPetersburg, Russia\n\nRussia\n\nRussia\n\nThe Helsinki Shipyard,\n\nFinland\n\nThe Helsinki Shipyard,\n\nFinland\n\nZaliv Shipyard, Kerch,\n\nUkrainian SSR\n\nSpecs\n\nOperator\n\nOperationality Ownership\n\nProject 22220, two nuclear-powered reactors, with a capacity\nof 81.5 thousand hp., icebreaking capacity – up to 3 metres\nProject 22220, two nuclear-powered reactors, with a capacity\nof 81.5 thousand hp, icebreaking capacity – up to 3 metres.\nProject 22220, two nuclear-powered reactors, with a capacity\nof 81.5 thousand hp., icebreaking capacity – up to 3 metres.\nProject 10520, two nuclear-powered reactors, with a capacity\nof 75 thousand hp., icebreaking capacity – 2.25 metres.\nProject 10521, two nuclear-powered reactors, with a capacity\nof 75 thousand hp., icebreaking capacity – 2.7 metres.\nProject 10580,one nuclear-powered reactors, with a capacity\nof about 50 thousands hp. icebreaking capacity – 1.77\nmetres.\n\n10580, one nuclear-powered reactor, with a capacity of about\n\n50 thousands hp., icebreaking capacity – 1.77 metre.\n\nProject 10081, reactor capacity – 40 thousand hp., icebreaking\n\ncapacity – up to 1 metre.\n\nAtomﬂot\n\n(Rosatom)\n\nAtomﬂot\n\n(Rosatom)\n\nAtomﬂot\n\n(Rosatom)\n\nAtomﬂot\n\n(Rosatom)\n\nAtomﬂot\n\n(Rosatom)\n\nAtomﬂot\n\n(Rosatom)\n\nAtomﬂot\n\n(Rosatom)\n\nAtomﬂot\n\n(Rosatom)\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nD\nE\nF\nE\nN\nS\nE\n&\nS\nE\nC\nU\nR\n\nI\n\nT\nY\nA\nN\nA\nL\nY\nS\n\nI\n\nS\n\n4\n0\n9\n\n\f410\n\nN. ALIYEV\n\nand Alexander Sannikov, diesel-electric icebreakers from Project IBSV01 (type Aker\nARC 130A) started service escorting oil shipment\nthe\nGazprom Neft.33 These new generation icebreakers operate in the area of the Arctic term-\ninal of the Novoportovskoye ﬁeld in the Gulf of Ob in the Kara Sea and ensure year-\nround supplies of Novy Port oil to the world market.34 They are designed to perform\nmulti-operational tasks: icebreaker escort of tankers, mooring and loading operations,\ntowing ships, ﬁreﬁghting, participation in oil spill response operations, and assistance\nin rescue operations.35\n\nfrom Arctic projects of\n\nThe Yamal LNG project is served by 15 gas carriers, only one of which is owned by the\nRussian company Sovcomﬂot. All 15 Arc7-classed LNG tankers are equipped dual fuel\ndiesel-electric propulsion systems with three “Azipod” units supplied by the Swiss-\nbased engineering company ABB.36 The capacity of these gas carriers is 172.6 thousand\ncubic metres. They can operate at temperatures of up to minus 50 degrees and are capable\nof passing through ice of 2.1 m thickness. The accelerated launch of the Yamal LNG and\nthe Arctic LNG is necessary to accelerate the expansion of the gas-carrier ﬂeet. In this\nregard, Novatek placed an order for the construction of 15 gas carriers with delivery\ndates in 2022, 2024 and 2025.37 In 2021 Nornickel38 stated that it may take part in the\nconstruction of three icebreakers. In particular, the company plans to ﬁnance fully a\nliqueﬁed natural gas (LNG) icebreaker, which it will need in 2024–2025. It will likely\nbe operated by Atomﬂot. Nornickel, together with Rosatom, will also work out the\nissue of building a diesel and port icebreaker. Novatek also planned to invest in gas-\npowered icebreakers. According to the industry’s representatives, out of four LNG-\npowered icebreakers, Nornickel can potentially become an investor in one, two more\nby Novatek, and the ﬁnancing of the fourth will be realised by coal or oil companies.39\nUnder the agreement between Nornickel and Rosatom, the completion of another ice-\nbreaker\nto replace the Taimyr-type nuclear-powered icebreakers was planned.\nRosatom and Nornickel also worked on the construction of port icebreakers.40\n\nFar Eastern Shipbuilding and Ship Repair Center JSC, Rosatom and Norilsk Nickel,\nwithin the framework of the 6th Eastern Economic Forum, signed an agreement of\nintent on co-operation in the design and construction of a dual-fuel, diesel-LNG ice-\nbreaker in September 2021. “The agreement is aimed at the parties’ participation in\nsolving state problems: the implementation of investment projects in the Russian\nArctic, the development of infrastructure, an increase in the cargo turnover of the North-\nern Sea Route, the creation of their own production of LNG icebreakers,” said the state-\nment published by the owner of the shipbuilding company.41 The icebreaker is designed\nto provide pilotage for ships operating on Nornickel’s projects in the Taimyr Peninsula,\nas well as in the western part of the Northern Sea Route. It was planned that the ice-\nbreaker’s power-plant will operate on both diesel and gas-engine fuel, which will\nprovide the ship with high environment-friendly engine. It was expected that the\nimplementation of the agreement would contribute to the formation of competencies\nfor the enterprises of the Far Eastern Shipbuilding and Ship Repair Center in the\ndesign and construction of LNG icebreakers, expanding the portfolio of shipbuilding\norders and optimal utilisation of the production capacities of the Zvezda shipbuilding\ncomplex.42 Moreover, on the same day, 3 September 2021, Rosatom, Nornickel and\nFar Eastern Shipbuilding and Ship Repair Center concluded an agreement on the joint\ndesign and construction of a dual-fuel, diesel-LNG icebreaker.43 Nornickel planned in\n\n\fDEFENSE & SECURITY ANALYSIS\n\n411\n\n2021 to allocate about 90 billion rubles for the construction and modernisation of port\nfacilities in the Arctic, the construction of icebreakers, including those working on\nLNG by 2030. The company also planned to invest 40 billion rubles in the modernisation\nof the Dudinsky seaport.44\n\nSome Russian companies operate or rent foreign ship companies’ small-class ice-\nbreakers for their needs. In 2009 the multifunctional icebreaker Varandey, built at the\nKeppel Singmarine shipyard (Singapore), was accepted by LUKOIL to operate in the\narea of the Varandey oil export terminal in the Barents Sea.45 In 2015 Keppel Singmarine\ntransferred three ice-class vessels to the Malaysian company Bumi Armada. These ships\nwere intended to operate on the oﬀshore developments of the Russian LUKOIL in the\nnorthern part of the Caspian Sea.46\n\nMilitary icebreakers47 and dual-use capabilities\n\nThe ﬁrst Russian combat icebreakers appeared in the 1960s. The ﬁrst of Project 97 was\nthe Dobrynya Nikitich icebreaker, built at the Admiralty Plant in Leningrad. On 31\nDecember 1960, it entered the auxiliary ﬂeet of the USSR Navy.48 Later another 30 ice-\nbreakers of this project and its modiﬁcations were built.49 From the beginning of con-\nstruction, dual-use perspectives of the Russian military icebreakers were considered.\nProject 97 icebreakers were exploited not only by the navy and border guards, but also\nby the Ministry of the Sea Transport of USSR.\n\nIn the late 1960s, a new version of the icebreaker appeared, which received project\nnumber 97P. The lead icebreaker Ivan Susanin was launched in 1973.50 It diﬀered\nfrom the previous 97th version as it lacked a nose rotor, an increased length, and a\nlanding and take-oﬀ pad for a Ka-25PS type helicopter. The hull itself was divided by\ntransverse bulkheads into seven compartments; unsinkability was ensured when one\nor two adjacent compartments were ﬂooded. In addition, they had weapons: gun\nmounts AK-726 and AK-630, a rotary cannon built for self-defence. In the period\nfrom 1973 to 1981, eight ships of this project were delivered to the Navy and the\nborder service of the USSR.51 At present, there are practically no vessels of Project 97\nleft. In the 1990s, most of the icebreakers of the Project 97 were dismantled. The remain-\ning units serve in the Rosmorport and units of the Russian Navy, as well as border troops.\nCurrently the main task of military icebreakers is to prohibit unauthorised passage of\nforeign warships in the waters of the Northern Sea Route. In addition, they provide ships,\nas well as deployed outposts and garrisons for the ﬂeet in the territory of the Arctic\nOcean, fuel, and other necessary products. The Russian Ministry of Defence has six mili-\ntary bases in the High North: on the Kotelny Islands (Novosibirsk Islands), Alexandra\nLand (part of the Franz Josef Land archipelago), Sredniy (Severnaya Zemlya), as well\nas in the village of Rogachevo (Novaya Zemlya), on Cape Schmidt, and Wrangel\nIsland (part of the Chukotka Autonomous Okrug). In addition to them, there are\nmore than a dozen military airﬁelds, positions for anti-aircraft missile systems, and mili-\ntary ports.52 The structure is built in such a way that the bases not only protect the ter-\nritory from a possible attack through the North Pole, but also become gates to “entry and\nexit.” The military infrastructure works year-round, but it is tied to the seasonal delivery\nof material and technical equipment. Therefore, icebreakers are important to the oper-\nation of military bases and infrastructure in the Arctic.\n\n\f412\n\nN. ALIYEV\n\nIn 2017 the Ilya Muromets, a multi-purpose patrol icebreaker of Project 21180, was\ndelivered to the ice-breaker group of the Russian Northern Fleet. The Ilya Muromets\nis a diesel-electric icebreaker. It is a single-deck, multifunctional vessel designed for ice-\nbreaker support for the basing and deployment of ﬂeet forces in ice conditions; indepen-\ndent pilotage of ships and vessels; towing of ships and other ﬂoating structures in icy\nconditions, and through clean water; extinguishing ﬁres at emergency facilities; contain-\nment of spills and collection of oil products; helicopter reception (without basing and\nrefuelling); transportation of containers on the open part of the upper deck, including\nrefrigerated containers with appropriate power supply; as well as other deck and hold\ncargo.53\n\nCurrently, two Project 23550 icebreakers – Ivan Papanin and Nikolai Zubov – are\nbeing built in the JSC “Admiralty Shipyards” in St. Petersburg.54 They are capable of per-\nforming the tasks of a multi-purpose warships, an icebreaker and a tug. At the same time,\nthey carry a wide range of systems, including artillery, as well as helicopters and assault\nboats. The ships can also carry container weapon systems, so that they can become full-\nﬂedged combat icebreakers. They can independently deliver artillery and missile strikes\nagainst sea, coastal, and even air targets. The Ivan Papanin has the AK-176MA automatic\nartillery mount on board, an icebreaker with a deck gun. The automatics of the gun and\nthe means of supplying ammunition make it possible to ﬁre at a rate of up to 125 rounds\nper minute at a distance of up to 16 km.55\n\nIt was planned that the Project 21180M icebreaker Evpatiy Kolovrat, which is being built\nat the Almaz Shipbuilding Company in St. Petersburg, will be in the Navy’s service in 2022.56\nAccording to the Izvestia, it is being built for the Kamchatka grouping of the Paciﬁc Fleet.57\nIn December of 2022 the icebreaker started the factory sea trials in the Baltic Sea.58\n\nThe Russian Border Guard Service has ordered two, albeit slightly modiﬁed, combat\nicebreakers similar to the Ivan Papanin. The ﬁrst such icebreaker, a frontier patrol ship of\nthe 1st rank “Purga” of ice class of Project 23550 “Ermak” was laid down in 2020 in\nVyborg.59 It will combine the capabilities of an icebreaker, a patrol cruiser and a tug.\nIn 2020, the deadlines for the delivery of these ships to the Navy was 2024, but it is\nnot clear whether these deadlines will be met.60\n\nIn 2019 the chief designer of the X-35 missile at the Tactical Missile Armament Cor-\nporation stated that the Russian Navy has adopted a new concept of “open stern” which\napplies to Project 23550.61 According to it, highly specialised combat units for solving\nspeciﬁc tasks are not created, but rather act as platforms. They have navigation equip-\nment to carry out shipping tasks. And in the aft there is a free compartment where\nseveral easily removable combat modules can be placed. With this approach, it is possible\nto create carrier ships without tying them to the solution of a speciﬁc combat mission,\nand to install a combat module depending on the situation, even right at sea, while on\nduty. According to this concept, allegedly the aft of the shipcan contain either two-\ntube launchers of Kalibr cruise missiles, or four-tube Uranus complexes with an X-35\nmissile, or two Onyxes.62 At present, in addition to missiles, containers can also\nicebreakers of\ncontain various deep-sea robotic and diving complexes. Therefore,\nProject 23550, including Ivan Papanin, can be useful not only in surface operations,\nbut in underwater ones as well.\n\nHowever, when considering more serious support and provision of military bases,\nthere are problems regarding the capabilities of the military icebreakers. The thickness\n\n\fDEFENSE & SECURITY ANALYSIS\n\n413\n\nof the ice can – in some cases – exceed three metres, which makes it impossible to support\nmilitary facilities in the event of a military conﬂict.63\n\nIt is obvious that the military and economic activities of the region are interconnected.\nFor instance, icebreakers of the Atomﬂot (the Yamal, Taimyr, Vaygach, 50 Years of Victory\nand Arktika) are involved in escorting ships along the Northern Sea Route, and, if necess-\nary, providing rescuers for the military.64 Moreover, non-military organisations are\ninvolved in cruises and even military exercises along the Northern Sea Route conducted\nby the Northern Fleet.65 At various stages of such activities hydrographic vessels of the\nNorthern Fleet, border patrol ships and icebreakers of Atomﬂot interact.66 The scenarios\nof conducted exercises also include issues such as ensuring the safety of economic activity\nand resolving crisis situations or defending important industrial facilities and ports in the\nArctic.67 Carrying out such large-scale military exercises in the region is impossible without\nthe involvement of the Atomﬂot, Rosmorport and other large private companies in the\nregion. Therefore, the Ministry of Defence, the Rosatom, and other state bodies in the\nregion need to coordinate the activities of the Navy. Public and private companies\nsupport military activities, such as providing their vessels and infrastructure for the\nneeds of the Navy. Although the Russian government and the President’s administration\nconduct several meetings on the Arctic problems and intend to improve interactions\nbetween state enterprises, private companies, and the military, problems amongst them\nappear periodically. This is mainly because, generally, in Russia, security issues outweigh\neconomic activities and competition for control and coordination over the NSR and\nports in the Russian Arctic Zone. The last issue is especially an acute problem between\nAtomfot and Rosmorport and, to some extent, private companies. However, currently,\nAtomﬂot has overcome its competitors on this issue.\n\nIn some cases, co-operation between military and other institutions and the emphasis\non dual-use capabilities is reﬂected in oﬃcial documents. For instance, the 2017 version\nof the governmental programme on the Russian Arctic Zone socioeconomic develop-\nment addressed some military security-related issues by referring to the necessity of\ncreating a dual-use transport infrastructure,\nincluding the ports of Dikson, Tiksi,\nPevek, and Providence, as well as needing to maintain the technical readiness of the\nNorthern Fleet to operate in the NSR water area. The Strategy 2020 also called for the\ncreation of an eﬀective Coast Guard system of the Federal Security Service.68 It should\nbe noted that one of the main tasks of the Russian nuclear-powered icebreaker ﬂeet is\n“ensuring the safety of navigation in the Arctic, including the activities of the Navy\nand emergency preparedness in the Arctic region.”69\n\nAmong other things the Russian nuclear-powered icebreakers provide for state needs,\nincluding the guaranteed performance of works and services to ensure the safety of navi-\ngation in the Arctic region, including emergency and rescue readiness, as well as the\nactivities of the Navy.70 Even the issue of building a new icebreaker ﬂeet is emphasised\nby Atomﬂot leadership and experts as “one of the most important for ensuring Arctic\nshipping, the normal functioning of the Northern Sea Route and the implementation\nof control in the Arctic in the interests of ensuring the country’s national security and\nprotecting the natural environment.”71 Moreover, the Russian government developed\nrules for the passage of foreign warships along the NSR in 2019.72 Foreign military\nvessels should notify Russia of their plans 45 days in advance and take Russian pilots\naboard. In the event of a worsening ice situation, according to the rules, only Russian\n\n\f414\n\nN. ALIYEV\n\nicebreakers will be authorised to provide service to foreign vessels. On 1 September 2023,\nchanges to the new rules for navigation in the waters of the NSR came into force. In par-\nticular, a registry model is being introduced for issuing permits for navigation of ships\nalong it. A conﬁrmation of the issuance of a permit will be an entry made in the corre-\nsponding register maintained by the NSR General Administration, Glavsevmorput.73\nThe primary maxim of those rules is an intention to cement Russia’s control and\nstrengthen it over the NSR.\n\nRussia’s invasion of Ukraine in 2022 and the icebreakers\n\nSince the beginning of Russia’s invasion of Ukraine, the Northern Fleet’s ground forces\nhave been used in the war.74 Russia has few ground forces left on the Kola Peninsula in\nthe northwest near Finland, because they were moved towards Ukraine despite Russia\nkeeping its strategic forces in the North.75 It suggests that the Kremlin sees the situation\nin the High North as stable. However, the land forces on Kola are reduced to a ﬁfth of\ntheir original numbers before the invasion of Ukraine though its strategic power\nremains in the region.76 The invasion also aﬀects Russia’s relations with other Arctic\nstates. Recently Russia terminated its memoranda with the Nordic Council of Ministers,\nwhich includes Denmark, Iceland, Norway, Finland, and Sweden, to establish infor-\nmation centres in St. Petersburg and Kaliningrad.77\n\nOther problems and challenges endure as well. For instance, the Russian vice president of\nthe Russian-Finnish “Helsinki Shipyard Oy” was arrested in Moscow for treason in July\n2022.78 The company was engaged in the construction of icebreakers and other vessels,\nmainly for Russia. The Finnish company Wärtsilä79 announced it had completed its exit\nfrom the Russian market. The company supplied ship engines and parts to Russian shipyards,\nmainly for icebreakers.80 Its departure aﬀects current building programmes, such as that of the\nYakutia and Chukotka. Instead of importing rudders and propellers, Russian-made equip-\nment is now planned to be used, including Transmashholding’s diesel engines.81\n\nHowever, Russia plans to build more icebreakers and support ships, both nuclear and\nnon-nuclear, with envisaged completions in 2027, 2030, and 2035. The plan for the devel-\nopment of the NSR by 2035 target, to build 2 more nuclear-powered icebreakers project\n22220 by 2030, and a nuclear-technological support ship by 2027.82 The plan also\nincludes construction of an additional 4 icebreakers, but does not clarify their project\nnumbers. However, ﬁnance for 2 more previously planned “Lider”s has not yet been\nfound. In April 2023, the Kommersant reported that the cost of building a lead\nnuclear-powered icebreaker “Russia” of project 10510 “Lider,” which previously\namounted to 128 billion rubles, may increase by 40–60%, and the construction period\nmay shift. It is assumed that the icebreaker will be built at the Zvezda shipyard in 2027.83\nSanctions have already started to limit building progress of icebreaker projects. For\nexample, Norilsk Nickel and Rosneft decided not to pursue plans to build LNG-\npowered vessels because of sanctions. Norilsk Nickel and Rosatom decided to build a\ndiesel-electric icebreaker, abandoning the use of a liqueﬁed natural gas (LNG) fuel\nsystem.84 They plan to install only Russian equipment on the icebreaker, including\nTransmashholding85 diesel engines and conventional shaft lines instead of imported\nrudder propellers. The abandonment of the LNG fuel system is a result of Western sanc-\ntions. One of the reasons the company was keen to use LNG-powered vessels is that it is\n\n\fTable 2. Contemporary Russian icebreakers – diesel-electric powered icebreakers (The Table includes Russian main operational vessels which have icebreaker\ncapacities).\n\nIcebreaker\n“Tor”\n\n“Ivan\n\nKrusenstern”\n\n“Dudinka”\n\n“Semyon\n\nDezhnev”\n\n“Ivan Sus