capacity\n\n13,000 hp, icebreaking capacity – 0.9 metre\n\nProject 21900, power capacity 16 MW,\n\nicebreaking capacity – 1 metre\n\npower capacity 16.8 MW, icebreaking capacity\n\n– 1.7 metre\n\nProject 21900, power capacity 16 MW,\n\nicebreaking capacity – 1 metre\n\nmultifunctional rescue vessel of project Р-\n\n70202, power capacity 9180 kw, icebreaking\ncapacity – 1.2 metres\n\n2015\n\nVyborg Shipyard PJSC (United Shipbuilding\n\nCorporation)\n\nProject 21900M, power capacity 18 MW,\nicebreaking capacity – up to 1.5 metres\n\n“Murmansk”\n\n2016\n\nVyborg Shipyard PJSC in cooperation with\n\nArctech Helsinki Shipyard\n\n“Novorossiysk”\n\n2016\n\nVyborg Shipyard PJSC\n\n“Ilya Muromets”\n\n2017\n\nAdmiralty Shipyard\n\nProject 21900M, power capacity 18 MW,\nicebreaking capacity – up to 1.5 metres\n\nProject 21900M, power capacity 18 MW,\nicebreaking capacity – up to 1.5 metres\n\nProject 21180, power capacity 10.4 MW,\nicebreaking capacity – up to 0.9 metre\n\n4\n1\n6\n\nN\n\n.\n\nA\nL\nI\n\nY\nE\nV\n\nOperational\nstatus\n\nOwnership\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nOperator\nFSUE “Rosmorport” (Federal\nAgency of Marine and\nRiver Transport)\n\nFSUE “Rosmorport” (Federal\nAgency of Marine and\nRiver Transport)\n\nFSUE “Rosmorport” (Federal\nAgency of Marine and\nRiver Transport)\n\nFSUE “Rosmorport” (Federal\nAgency of Marine and\nRiver Transport)\n\nFSUE “Rosmorport” (Federal\nAgency of Marine and\nRiver Transport)\n\nFSUE “Rosmorport” (Federal\nAgency of Marine and\nRiver Transport)\n\nLUKOIL TRANS\n\nOperational\n\nPrivate\n\nFSUE “Rosmorport” (Federal\nAgency of Marine and\nRiver Transport)\n\nFSUE “Rosmorport” (Federal\nAgency of Marine and\nRiver Transport)\n\n(LUKOIL)\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nFSUE “Rosmorport” (Federal\nAgency of Marine and\nRiver Transport)\n\nFSUE “Rosmorport” (Federal\nAgency of Marine and\nRiver Transport)\n\nFSUE “Rosmorport” (Federal\nAgency of Marine and\nRiver Transport)\nThe Northen Fleet\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nOperational\n\nRussian Navy\n\n\f“Alexander\nSannikov”\n\n“Andrey\n\nVilkitskiy”\n\n“Ob”\n\n2018\n\n2018\n\n2019\n\nArctech Helsinki Shipyard, Vyborg Shipyard\n\nPJSC\n\nArctech Helsinki Shipyard, Vyborg Shipyard\n\nPJSC\n\nVyborg Shipyard PJSC (United Shipbuilding\n\nCorporation)\n\n“Yuribey”\n\n“Nadym”\n\n2018\n\nCraneship LLC\n\n2018\n\nCraneship LLC\n\n“Viktor\n\nChernomyrdin”\n\n2020\n\nAdmiralty Shipyard\n\nProject Aker ARC 130A, IBSV01, power capacity\n22 MW, icebreaking capacity – up to 2 metres\nProject Aker ARC 130A, IBSV01, power capacity\n22 MW, icebreaking capacity – up to 2 metres\n\nProject the Aker ARC124 project (ice class\nIcebreaker7), a port icebreaker, power\ncapacity 12 MW, icebreaking capacity – 1.5\nmetres\n\nIce class Arc 6, an icebreaking tug, power\ncapacity 7 MW, icebreaking capacity – 1\nmetre\n\nIce class Arc 6, an icebreaking tug, power\n\ncapacity 6.4 MW, icebreaking capacity – 1\nmetre\n\nproject 22600, power capacity 25 MW,\nicebreaking capacity – 2 metres\n\nGazprom Neft\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nGazprom Neft\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nAtomﬂot (Rosatom)\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nAtomﬂot (Rosatom)\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nAtomﬂot (Rosatom)\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nFSUE “Rosmorport” (Federal\nAgency of Marine and\nRiver Transport)\n\nOperational\n\nState\n\nSources: Russian Maritime Register of Shipping – rs-class.org, FSUE “Rosmorport” – rosmorport.com, Federal Agency of Marine and River Transport (Rosmorrechﬂot) – morﬂot.gov.ru, United\nShipbuilding Corporation – aoosk.ru, Admiralty Shipyards – admship.ru, Baltic Shipyard – bz.ru, Vyborg Shipyard – vyborgshipyard.ru, Helsinki Shipyard – helsinkishipyard.ﬁ, Ministry of\nDefence of the Russian Federation – mil.ru, Portnews.ru, Moyport.info, Seanews.ru, Paluba.media, Aker Aarctic – akerarctic.ﬁ, Neftegaz.ru, LUKOIL – lukoil.com, NORNICKEL – nornickel.\ncom, Ledokolfest.ru, Da-ﬁsh.ru.\n\nD\nE\nF\nE\nN\nS\nE\n&\nS\nE\nC\nU\nR\n\nI\n\nT\nY\nA\nN\nA\nL\nY\nS\n\nI\n\nS\n\n4\n1\n7\n\n\f418\n\nN. ALIYEV\n\nresponsible for “the worst Russian environmental disaster” in the north when 21,000\ntonnes of its diesel leaked into rivers and subsoil in 2020, and after that the company\nannounced that it will be investing heavily in higher production and improved environ-\nmental performance in 2022–2025.86 Against this backdrop, however, Rosatom obtained\nﬁnancing in 2022 for the development of the eastern NSR.87\n\nTasks and strategies\n\nThe tasks of the Russian nuclear-powered icebreaker ﬂeet cover economic and strategic\nissues such as: ensuring year-round pilotage of ships along the Northern Sea Route in the\nwestern part of the Arctic; laying channels in drifting and fast ice to ensure the passage of\nships to ports in freezing water areas; icebreaking support for the development of the\nArctic oil and gas ﬁelds of the Yamal Peninsula and the shelf of the Barents and Kara\nSeas; provision of escort of vessels with the necessary cargoes for enterprises and settle-\nments in the Arctic (function of the “Northern Delivery”); provision of scientiﬁc research\nwork for solving national economic and security problems in the Arctic basin, including\nthe polar regions of the Arctic; and ensuring safety of navigation in the Arctic, including\nthe activities of the Navy and emergency preparedness in the region.88\n\nRussia’s new naval doctrine states amongst other issues the need for control over\nforeign military activity on the Northern Sea Route.89 The development of the NSR is\ndeclared as one of Russia’s main maritime national interests and a strategic goal. The\ndoctrine put “the eﬀorts of a number of states to weaken the control of the Russian Fed-\neration over the Northern Sea Route, the build-up of foreign naval presence in the Arctic”\nas amongst the main challenges and threats to “national security and sustainable devel-\nopment of the Russian Federation associated with the World Ocean.” The doctrine\ndeclares the strategic course of the US and allies on dominance in the oceans as a\nmain challenge and a threat to Russia’s national security in the open seas.90\n\nThe previous 2015 Maritime doctrine also provided insight into how Moscow might\nengage in a maritime conﬂict with the West; including mobilising its civilian ﬁshing ﬂeet,\noperation of oceanographic vessels, and other ostensibly non-military vessels and instal-\nlations to support a potential conﬂict. Michael B. Petersen stressed that the mobilisation\nof civilian resources for use in conﬂict has a long history in Russia, and this doctrine pro-\nvides a clear articulation of its practice in the maritime domain. “In any case, the doctrine\nenvisions an active role for Moscow’s improving military and its civilian resources,” he\nconcludes.91 The new doctrine also listed measures to mobilise Russian civilian vessels,\ninstallations, and ports to support the Navy during a potential conﬂict.92 In some\ncases, oﬃcial documents reﬂect co-operation amongst the military and other institutions\n(scientiﬁc) and companies (oil & gas) on dual-use capabilities. For instance, the 2017 gov-\nernmental programme on northern socioeconomic development addressed some mili-\ntary security-related issues by referring to the necessity of creating a dual-use\ntransport infrastructure, including in the ports of Dikson, Tiksi, Pevek, and Providence.93\n\nProblems and perspectives\n\nAs reﬂected in Russian oﬃcial documents, the government targets to develop the ice-\nbreaker ﬂeet and build new vessels despite the sanctions and challenges resulting from\n\n\fDEFENSE & SECURITY ANALYSIS\n\n419\n\nthe invasion of Ukraine. The design and construction of heavy icebreakers for use in the\nRussian Arctic represents a unique technological development where Russia considers\nitself the world leader, although Finnish ship designers have been at times inﬂuential\npartners.94 Finnish companies are not solely involved in Russia’s icebreaker building pro-\njects, as in recent years South Korean and Turkish shipbuilders also participated in such\nprojects. The government contract for the construction of a rescue icebreaker for the\nNorthern Sea Route was won by a Turkish company. In 2021 Kommersant reported\nthat Kuzey Star Shipyard would become a subcontractor for the construction of two\ndual-fuel icebreakers (powered by diesel and LNG with a capacity of 12–14 MW) for Ros-\nmorport at a total cost of 18.5 billion rubles.95 The icebreakers will operate year-round in\nthe Baltic, White and Barents Seas, as well as in the Paciﬁc Basin, and in the seas of the\nArctic Ocean and river estuaries during summer-autumn period.\n\nIn 2021 it was reported that Rosatom plans to order an additional six icebreakers to ensure\nthe export of Russian raw materials and international transit of goods along the Northern Sea\nRoute by 2030.96 According to the ﬁrst deputy head of the Rosatom, Kirill Komarov, two of\nthese are nuclear-powered icebreakers of the Project 22220 series with a capacity of 60 MW,\nand four are powered by liqueﬁed natural gas (LNG), each with a capacity of 40 MW. The\ncost of the nuclear-powered icebreaker was estimated at 60 billion rubles. The construction\nof new icebreakers is synchronised with the growth of cargo traﬃc and should be provided\nwith appropriate agreements with shippers. These vessels will be required to replace nuclear-\npowered icebreakers carrying the cargo of Gazprom Neft, Norilsk Nickel and NOVATEK\nprojects in the Gulf of Ob after 2026–2029.97\n\nVyacheslav Ruksha, Deputy Director General of the State Atomic Energy Corporation\nRosatom and Director of the Northern Sea Route Directorate, noted that Russia should\nhave 12–14 icebreakers in operation to ensure year-round operation of the Northern Sea\nRoute by 2030. “At the turn of 2030, we should have at least 7 nuclear-powered ice-\nbreakers but we need to have 12–14 icebreakers.”98 Ruksha’s statement is based on the\nMay decree of President Vladimir Putin as it sets a goal to increase cargo turnover\nalong the NSR to 80 million tons by 2024. This did seem to be a diﬃcult goal before\nthe start of the invasion, but it will be especially diﬃcult to achieve amidst the war\nand Western sanctions. Moreover, in the Strategy for Developing the Russian Arctic\nZone and Ensuring National Security until 2035, building additional icebreakers (one\nfrom Project 22220 and two from Lider – Project 10510) for providing annual transpor-\ntation through the NSR is noted.99 The Strategy sets a goal to increase the cargo turnover\nalong the NSR to 90 million tons by 2030 and 130 million tons by 2035.100 According to\nRuksha, the cargo traﬃc is expected to reach 110 million tons by 2030, taking into\naccount transit. According to Komarov, by 2030 it is planned to transport about 30\nmillion tons of transit cargo along the Northern Sea Route. In 2020, cargo traﬃc along\nthis route amounted to 33 million tons, mainly of oil, LNG, and metals from Nornickel.\nThe implementation of the plan will largely depend on Novatek’s LNG projects, which\nplanned to increase cargo traﬃc to 35.5 million tons by 2024, as well as on Rosneft’s\nVostok Oil project, which, according to the oil company, planned add 30 million tons\nof freight traﬃc in 2024, and the Northern Star coal project by Roman Trotsenko (the\nplan for 2024 is about 4 million tons).101\n\nIn December 2021 Rosatom reported that the volume of cargo transportation through\nthe NSR exceeded 33.5 million tons. Transit along the NSR exceeded 2 million tons. In\n\n\f420\n\nN. ALIYEV\n\n2020 it was about 1.3 million. The bulk of the cargo traﬃc was created by oil and gas\ninvestment projects: oil and oil-related products of 7.7 million tons, LNG and gas con-\ndensate of 19.6 million tons, coal of 221.5 thousand tons, and ore concentrate of 47.7\nthousand tons. Other cargoes amounted to more than 4 million tons.102 Since 2016\nthere has been a trend of increasing maritime freight traﬃc (2016 – 7.3 million tons;\n2017 – 9.93 million tons; 2018 – 20.2 million tons; 2019 – 31.5 million tons).103\nDespite war and sanctions, transit along the NSR increased in 2022 as well. Cargo\ntraﬃc on the Northern Sea Route in 2022 reached 34.117 million tons. During 2022,\ncargo traﬃc increased primarily due to LNG transportation. In 2022 LNG transportation\nby the NSR increased to 1.2 million tons, container transportation grew by 226 thousand\ntons, and dry bulk by 109 thousand tons. The main shippers were the Russian companies:\nNovatek, Norilsk Nickel, and Gazpromneft.104 The NSR transits in 2022 mainly were\nRussian outbound cargo, because after the start of the invasion of Ukraine, international\nshipping through the route decreased to zero. The transit of foreign cargo along the\nNorthern Sea Route (NSR) for 2022 decreased by 10 times and amounted to 200 thou-\nsand tons, according to the Directorate of the Northern Sea Route.105 However, since\n2022 the NSR has been becoming critical for the Russian economy.\n\nAn earlier head of Rosatom stated that by 2030, a group of 13 icebreakers will be oper-\national on the NSR.106 In particular, nine icebreakers will operate in the western sector of\nthe Arctic, and another four in the eastern sector. According to Rosatom’s estimates, this\nwill be enough to handle up to 150 million tons of cargo per year. “According to prelimi-\nnary estimates, it is possible to double the transportations along the NSR on the horizon\nuntil 2030, and after 2034, reach the amount of freight traﬃc along the ‘Big’ NSR up to\n250 million tons,” said CEO Alexei Likhachev.107 Moreover, Rosatom and NOVATEK\nplanned to launch year-round navigation in eastern part of NSR in early 2024.108\n\nHowever, these are only plans and their occasional roles as advertisements, for both\ninternal and external audiences, can be strikingly visible. Therefore, there are, at times,\ndiﬀerent prognoses within the same state institutions. The exact amount of sea traﬃc,\nand the number of new icebreakers will depend on several factors. There are demands\nfor new icebreakers and their construction is planned by the state but, also with large\nRussian companies involved in the Arctic energy and other projects. However,\nWestern sanctions have caused severe adjustments to the construction schedules of\nships and vessels. The conditions of the Russian economy and developments in the\nworld economy at large will inﬂuence projects in the Russian Arctic Zone, including\nthe building of planned icebreakers. Moreover, Russia’s invasion of Ukraine in 2022\nhas already started challenging Russia’s plans. Due to the sanctions, Russian shipbuilding\nfaced diﬃculties in equipping the ﬂeet under construction with engines, propulsion, and\nnavigation systems. Since the breakup of the Soviet Union the construction of many ships\nhave been mostly carried out in cooperation with foreign companies. Therefore, until\nrecently, the main suppliers of engines and diesel generators were companies from the\nUSA, Japan, Germany, Finland, and Sweden. Although the Russian authorities consider\nthe creation of a separate state programme “Development of engine building” with\nfunding from the federal budget, realisation of it will require additional ﬁnancial\nresources and time.109 It is possible that Russia can import from “friendly or unfriendly”\ncountries through using of several legal or illegal import schemes, but most probably,\ntheir quantity are not same as before 2022.\n\n\fDEFENSE & SECURITY ANALYSIS\n\n421\n\nEﬀects of weather conditions\n\nIt is obvious that perspectives of the Russian icebreaker ﬂeet mainly depend on the inﬂu-\nences of climate change in the Arctic.\n\nThere will be no seasonal disappearance of ice cover in the Arctic Ocean by 2050, First\nDeputy Director General of the Atomﬂot stated: “Even in the warmest year, the Arctic\nseas will be ice-free only from August to October.”110 While Rosatom is well-known\nfor actively advocating for the increased role of the nuclear-powered icebreaker ﬂeet,\nthe weather conditions alone in the Arctic waters during the winter season 2021 empha-\nsised the necessity of icebreakers for sea navigation through the NSR. However, studies\nsupport other predictions that summers in the Arctic Ocean are likely to lose their ice\ncover entirely by summer 2035.111\n\nThere are few documents available to vessels for help in navigating the NSR in various\nice conditions, and information regarding permissible navigation areas and ice naviga-\ntion conditions. The main oﬃcial document is “Navigation rules in the water area of\nthe Northern Sea Route.”112 Data for identiﬁcation of easy, moderate, or diﬃcult ice con-\nditions were accumulated during winter navigation of vessels by the Norilsk Nickel in the\nsouth-western part of the Kara Sea and in the Yenisei Gulf.113\n\nRussian scholar Dumanskaya stressed that two opposite processes regarding the\nRussian icebreaker ﬂeet are observed: increasing power of built and designed icebreakers\non the one hand, and decreasing ice thickness in all Russian seas due to sustainable\nwarming on the other. According to Dumanskaya, truly challenging conditions for ice\nnavigation are occurring in the following situations: lack of icebreakers for assistance\ndue to increased ship traﬃc on the route; the beam of icebreakers is insuﬃcient to\nassist very large ships (e.g. a super tanker with beam of 50 m); the main icebreaker in\nthe region is underpowered for moderate ice conditions at the sea (e.g. IB “Magadan”\nin the Sea of Okhotsk); icebreaking capability is decreased due to exhausted lifetime of\nvessels; convoy of vessels meets with a hummocked and ridged ice zone; the presence\nof ice class vessels not in compliance with moderate ice conditions in the sea; and a tech-\nnical accident. She emphasised that the concept of easy, moderate, and diﬃcult ice con-\nditions corresponding with winter severity is still used by navigators to excuse problems\nduring their winter navigation, even though ice conditions are usually not the main chal-\nlenge. Obviously, when one loses the way in ice for any reason, the situation is only more\ncomplicated and makes navigating almost any ice condition diﬃcult.114\n\nAs a result, Rosatom proposed to change the rules for navigation along the Northern\nSea Route (NSR) in connection with the “November crisis” in 2021. It happened in a\n“shoulder season,” when ice conditions are especially unpredictable. Ice appeared\nmuch earlier than expected, and 20 vessels got stuck in sea ice by early November\n2021. During the “November crisis” Rosatom and the ship owning companies identiﬁed\nimportant gaps in crisis response procedures. Special representative of Rosatom for the\ndevelopment of the Arctic, deputy chairman of the State Commission for the Arctic Vla-\ndimir Panov, said the role of long-term planning in an environment of growing cargo\ntraﬃc and coordination is becoming a priority given the two operating icebreaker\nﬂeets (nuclear and diesel). It is important to increase responsibility for all participants:\nshippers, shipping companies, operators of nuclear and diesel icebreaker ﬂeets, and\nanyone authorised sailing along the NSR.115\n\n\f422\n\nN. ALIYEV\n\nDespite fact that Rosatom is the main player in the NSR and currently it is oﬃcially an\noperator of the Northern Sea Route, parts of the NSR infrastructure are not controlled by\nRosatom. The ports on the NSR are operated by Rosmorport, and diesel-electric ice-\nbreakers as well. Rosatom tried to take over several icebreakers from Rosmorport.\nThis is one of the reasons why Rosatom, and its company Atomﬂot, actively advocated\nfor strengthening co-ordination of navigation in the NSR in recent years. Although\nemphasising the need for new icebreakers and demanding the main coordination body\nmeet Rosatom’s organisational interests, its management-related problems are still acute.\n\nProblems of management\n\nA federal state body, the Northern Sea Route Administration, was established in Moscow by\nthe Russian Government in 2013. The institution ensures the “safe navigation and protection\nof the marine environment from pollution in the water area of the Northern sea route.”116 Its\nmain functions are: obtaining and issuing permission for navigation by ships through the\nNSR; issuing certiﬁcates of ice conventional pilotage; researching weather, ice, navigational\nand other conditions along the route; co-ordinating the installation of navigational aids, the\nharmonisation of regions to carry out hydrographic surveys operations; assistance in the\norganisation of search and rescue operations in the water area of the NSR; assistance in elim-\ninating the consequences of pollution from vessels of harmful substances (i.e. sewage or\ngarbage); rendering information services in relation to the water area of the NSR (i.e. about\nthe organisation of navigation, requirements of safe navigation and other related issues);\nmaking recommendations about the development of routes of navigation and using icebreak-\ning ﬂeet in the water area of the NSR; timely data-retrieval from the Russian hydrometeoro-\nlogical service regarding the hydrometeorology forecast and ice analysis.117 The organisation,\ndespite having the same name as the NSR Administration within the USSR Ministry of the\nMerchant Marine (Minmorﬂot), in reality received a limited mandate and is subordinated\nto the Agency of the Sea and River Fleet (Rosmorrechﬂot), under the Ministry of Transport.\nThe NSR’s administration does not have the power to instruct other state bodies, and it does\nnot seem to have any role in policy formation, or icebreaker-acquisition matters.118 According\nto Arild Moe and Lawson Brigham, the picture that emerges is one of two separate structures:\n\nOn the one hand, we see the formal structure with the Ministry of Transport on top and\nsubordinate bodies to implement policy, including use of private companies or other\nstate bodies for technical support. But we also see another structure where one of the tech-\nnical bodies – Atomﬂot – makes the key decisions and controls the most vital information. It\nis the tail that wags the dog.119\n\nAccording to the Resolution of the Government of the Russian Federation of 18 Septem-\nber 2020, N 1487 “On Approval of the Rules for Navigation in the Water Area of the\nNorthern Sea Route,” organisation of navigation of ships in the water area of the North-\nern Sea Route is carried out by Rosatom.120 In order to organise the navigation of ships,\nby the decision of Rosatom, a headquarters for the sea would be created, to provide\n\nicebreaker escort of ships and escort of ships along the routes of navigation of ships in the\nwaters of the NSR, as well as develop routes for navigation of ships and arranges ships of the\nicebreaker ﬂeet in the water area of the NSR, taking into account the hydrometeorological,\nice and navigation conditions in the speciﬁed water area.\n\n\fDEFENSE & SECURITY ANALYSIS\n\n423\n\nThe Resolution stated that there is a permitting procedure for the navigation of ships\nalong the NSR and permits are issued by the Federal Agency for Sea and River Transport,\nor an organisation subordinate to it in agreement with the headquarters.121\n\nThe document stressed that icebreaker escort of vessels, the navigation of which in the\nwaters of the NSR, “according to the admission criteria, must be carried out under ice-\nbreaker escort, is performed by icebreakers entitled to sail under the State ﬂag of the\nRussian Federation.” According to the resolution, icebreaker assistance is carried out\nin accordance with the rules for icebreaker assistance of ships in the waters of the NSR,\n\napproved by the Ministry of Transport of the Russian Federation in agreement with the State\nCorporation Rosatom, and includes the navigation of a vessel (convoy of ships) with the\nhelp of an icebreaker (icebreakers), as well as the activities of the icebreaker ﬂeet, ensuring\nthe speciﬁed navigation, including the formation of a convoy of ships and the placement of\nships in the convoy to follow icebreakers, preliminary laying of a channel in ice, towing of\nships in ice conditions, ice reconnaissance by an icebreaker, ensuring safe anchorage (drift)\nof ships in ice while waiting for an improvement in ice conditions.122\n\nAlthough Rosatom succeeded to obtain the rights over the infrastructure operator of the\nNSR, there was still a diarchy represented by the Northern Sea Route Administration/the\nMinistry of Transport and the headquarters of Marine Operations of Atomﬂot/Rosatom\nuntil August 2022. On 1 August 2022, the Federal State Budgetary Institution “Main\nDirectorate of the Northern Sea Route” began work as part of the Rosatom.123 It included\nthe headquarters of marine operations. It means since then the management of naviga-\ntion along the NSR has been controlled by one body.\n\nIn addition to icebreakers and navigation-related problems, perspectives of the NSR and\ngenerally the Russian Arctic region require the development of coastal infrastructure, in par-\nticular the eastern Arctic. Building new icebreakers and developing port infrastructure will\nallow the use of ships more than 40 m wide with the displacement of more than 100 thousand\ntons for cargo transportation.124 However, even before the start of the Russia’s invasion 2022\nof Ukraine, in 2021, port infrastructure investors have faced challenges associated with the\ncrisis caused by the coronavirus and unpredictable changes of logistic chains.125\n\nConclusion\n\nThe transport crisis in the NSR in November and December 2021 exposed several long-\nstanding problems, in particular problems with transportation management, the NSR\nand the state institutions of the Russian Arctic, and most importantly, with co-ordination\nof plans and activities of all the Russian Arctic players. Moscow tried to solve its main\nproblems of co-ordination by transferring the main responsibilities of the NSR manage-\nment to Rosatom in 2022. Russia’s invasion of Ukraine has aﬀected all of Russia’s Arctic\nprojects, energy extraction, the development of the NSR, and shipbuilding. Because the\nsanctions followed the invasion limit Russia’s state and private companies’ accesses to the\nWestern investments and technology, which are important for the realisation of Russian\nArctic projects. Even China and other potential investors also have concerns trying to\ninvolve in Russia’s projects over the sanctions. However, Moscow intends to meet the\nneeds of the Russian projects in the Arctic with the involvement of non-Western\nactors, such as Asian and Middle Eastern states.\n\n\f424\n\nN. ALIYEV\n\nBased on these developments, this paper argues that the inﬂuences of climate change\nand Russia’s foreign and external security policy have aﬀected the development of its ice-\nbreaker ﬂeet in the Arctic. On the other hand, Moscow’s confrontation with the West\namidst the invasion of Ukraine and the sanctions over it increased the allocation of\nthe government’s ﬁnance to local shipbuilding companies. Moreover, Russia was\nalready heads and shoulders ahead of the rest in icebreaker capability, and the fears of\nsanctions/Western curtailment of investment in the industry, have not fully materialised\nyet. In such circumstances, however, Russia’s economic diﬃculties and shipbuilding\ndelays should enhance the dual-use capabilities of old and new Russian icebreakers in\nthe coming years. Developments of the Russian icebreaker ﬂeet will mainly depend on\nthe weather of the Arctic, developments of the NSR, general economic and ﬁnancial\ntrends in the world, and Moscow’s international behaviour has brought about Western\neconomic sanctions in recent years.\n\nNotes\n\n1. Stepan O. Makarov, Svetlana V. Dolgova and Nikita A. Kuznetsov, Ledokol “Yermak”\n\n(Moscow: Paulsen, 2010), http://militera.lib.ru/bio/jzl_makarov/08.html.\n\n2. Ibid.\n3. Dmitry Gorchakov,\n\n‘Ledokol’nyy ﬂot Rossii – ot ugol’nogo “Yermaka” do atomnogo\n\n“Lenina”’, Habr, September 9, 2021, https://habr.com/ru/post/576550/.\n\n4. Ivan Kukushkin, ‘“Iosif Stalin”/ “Sibir”’, lineynyy ledokol’, Polyarnaya Pochta, May 21, 2008,\n\nhttp://www.polarpost.ru/forum/viewtopic.php?f=4&t=560.\n\n5. Dmitry Gorchakov, ‘Ledokol’nyy ﬂot Rossii’.\n6. Donald L. Canney, ‘Icebreakers and the U.S. Coast Guard’, US Department of Defence,\n1999, 6, https://media.defence.gov/2019/Jul/25/2002162249/-1/-1/0/ICEBREAKERS_D.%\n20CANNEY_1999.PDF.\n\n7. Joint Committee Print, Soviet Economic Prospects for the Seventies a Compendium of Papers\nSubmitted to the Joint Economic Committee Congress of the United States (Washington: U.S.\nGovernment Printing Oﬃce, 1973), 733.\n\n8. Vladislav Bukin, ‘Sudostroyeniye v Finlyandii. Chast’ 1: Verf’ «Arctech Helsinki Shipyard» –\n90 let sotrudnichestva s SSSR i Rossiyey’, KORABEL.RU, May 24, 2015, https://www.korabel.\nru/news/comments/sudostroenie_v_ﬁnlyandii_chast_1_verf_arctech_helsinki_shipyard_-_\n90_let_sotrudnichestva_s_sssr_i_rossiey.html.\n\n9. D.F. Skripnuk, I.O. Liyushchenko, S.V. Kulik and M.M. Stepanova, ‘Analysis of the Current\nState of the Northern Sea Route and the Potential Development of the Icebreaker Fleet’\n(paper presented at 5th international conference ‘Arctic: History and Modernity’, Saint-\nPetersburg, Russia, 18–19 March 2020), 3, doi:10.1088/1755-1315/539/1/012129, https://\niopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1755-1315/539/1/012129/meta.\n\n10. Atomﬂot, ‘Sud’ba byt’ pervym’, 2022, http://www.rosatomﬂot.ru/atomnyy-ledokol-lenin/\n\nistoriya-1/.\n\n11. Skripnuk et al., ‘Analysis of the Current State of the Northern Sea Route’, 3.\n12. The Federal State Unitary Enterprise Atomﬂot is designed to ensure the operation and tech-\nnological maintenance of nuclear-powered icebreakers and vessels of the auxiliary ﬂeet. It is\na part of the Rosatom.\n\n13. More information on operational nuclear-powered icebreakers can be found in Table 1.\n14. ROSATOM,\nﬂeet/.\n\n‘Razvitiye Severnogo morskogo puti’, 2023, https://rosatom.ru/production/\n\n15. ROSATOM, ‘Predstaviteli Rosatoma i yego predpriyatiy prinyali uchastiye v XI Mezhdunar-\nodnom forume “Arktika: nastoyashcheye i budushcheye”’, December 3, 2021, https://\n\n\fDEFENSE & SECURITY ANALYSIS\n\n425\n\nrosatom.ru/journalist/news/predstaviteli-rosatoma-i-ego-predpriyatiy-prinyali-uchastie-v-\nxi-mezhdunarodnom-forume-arktika-nasto/.\n\n16. Atomﬂot,\n\n‘Universal’nyye atomnyye ledokoly proyekta 22220’, 2022, http://www.\n\nrosatomﬂot.ru/ﬂot/universalnyy-atomnyy-ledokol-proekta-22220/.\n\n17. RIA Novosti,\n\n‘Novyy atomnyy ledokol “Sibir’” peredali v ekspluatatsiyu “Rosatomu”’,\n\nDecember 24, 2021, https://ria.ru/20211224/ledokol-1765368763.html.\n\n18. Atomﬂot, ‘Universal’nyy atomnyy ledokol “Sibir’” voshel v sostav ﬂota FGUP “Atomﬂot”’,\nhttps://www.rosatom.ru/journalist/news/universalnyy-atomnyy-\n\n2021,\n\n25,\n\nDecember\nledokol-sibir-voshel-v-sostav-ﬂota-fgup-atomﬂot/.\n\n19. ROSATOM, ‘Keel of Fifth Project 22220 Icebreaker Laid in St. Petersburg’, December 18,\n2020, https://rosatom.ru/en/press-centre/news/keel-of-ﬁfth-project-22220-icebreaker-laid-\nin-st-petersburg/.\n\n20. I.O. Dumanskaya, ‘Dependence of the Modern Icebreaker Fleet from Ice Conditions on the\n\nRussian Seas’, Russian Arctic, no. 5 (2019): 11–22, 14.\n\n21. Atle Staalesen, ‘New Super-Powerful Icebreaker Might Work Exclusively for Putin’s Oil\nTycoon Igor Sechin’, The Barents Observer, November 22, 2022, https://thebarentsob\nserver.com/en/industry-and-energy/2022/11/new-super-powerful-icebreaker-might-work-\nexclusively-rosnefts-arctic.\n\n22. ROSATOM, ‘The Keel has been Laid Down for the Nuclear Icebreaker Rossija in the Far\nEast’, July 7, 2022, https://rosatom.ru/en/press-centre/news/the-keel-has-been-laid-down-\nfor-the-nuclear-icebreaker-rossija-in-the-far-east/.\n\n23. ROSATOM, ‘V Rosatome razrabotana elementnaya baza yadernogo topliva dlya atomnogo\nledokola proyekta 10510 “Lider”’, January 20, 2020, https://rosatom.ru/journalist/news/v-\nrosatome-razrabotana-elementnaya-baza-yadernogo-topliva-dlya-atomnogo-ledokola-proe\nkta-10510-lider/.\n\n24. Oleg Bukharin, ‘Russia’s Nuclear Icebreaker Fleet’, Science & Global Security 14, no. 1\n\n(2006): 25–31, 26.\n\n25. More information on operational the Russian diesel-electric icebreakers can be found in Table 2.\n26. Rosmorport was founded by the Ministry of Transport of the Russian Federation and the\nMinistry of Property Relations of the Russian Federation, and its main mission is to\nprovide safe navigation in the water area and on the ways to seaports.\n\n27. Rosmorport, ‘Ledokol’nyye uslugi’, 2023, https://www.rosmorport.ru/services/icebreakerse\n\nrvices/.\n\n28. Skripnuk et al., ‘Analysis of the Current State of the Northern Sea Route’, 3.\n29. Rosmorport, ‘Novosti predpriyatiya’, November 3, 2020, https://www.rosmorport.ru/news/\n\ncompany/36057/.\n\n30. LORP – Lenskoye ob’yedinennoye rechnoye parokhodstvo, ‘O predpriyatii’, 2023, http://\n\nlorp.ru/company/obshchaya-informatsiya.\n\n31. Anton Murav’yev, ‘“NORNIKEL”: svoy ﬂot v Arktike’, Adventure 1, no. 4 (2017): 14, https://\n\nwww.interros.ru/upload/iblock/f3a/f3abbdd4eb8fab02bd7b94787790a619.pdf.\n32. More information on operational corporate icebreakers can be found in Table 2.\n33. Ye Aliﬁrova,\n\n‘Slazhennyy tandem. Ledokoly Andrey Vil’kitskiy i Aleksandr Sannikov\nnachali kruglogodichnoye soprovozhdeniye otgruzki nefti s arkticheskikh proyektov\nGazprom nefti’, Neftegaz.ru, January 24, 2019, https://neftegaz.ru/news/transport-and-\nstorage/195282-slazhennyy-tandem-ledokoly-andrey-vilkitskiy-i-aleksandr-sannikov-nach\nali-kruglogodichnoe-soprovozhd/.\n\n34. NANS – Natsional’naya Assotsiatsiya neftegazovogo servisa,\n\n‘Vysokotekhnologichnyy\nledokol «Aleksandr Sannikov» voshel v sostav rossiyskogo arkticheskogo ﬂota’, June\n29, 2018, https://nangs.org/news/technologies/vysokotekhnologichnyj-ledokol-aleksandr-\nsannikov-voshel-v-sostav-rossijskogo-arkticheskogo-ﬂota.\n\n35. Gazprom, ‘Gazpromneft’ – usilila sobstvennyy arkticheskiy ﬂot vtorym ledokolom Andrey\nVil’kitskiy’, December 20, 2018, https://www.gazprom-neft.ru/press-center/news/gazprom-\nneft-usilila-sobstvennyy-arkticheskiy-ﬂot-vtorym-ledokolom-andrey-vilkitskiy/.\n\n\f426\n\nN. ALIYEV\n\n36. Mike Schuler, ‘Yamal LNG’s 15-Vessel Fleet of Icebreaking LNG Tankers Now Operational’,\nDecember 11, 2019, https://gcaptain.com/yamal-lngs-15-vessel-ﬂeet-of-icebreaking-lng-\ntankers-now-operational/.\n\n37. Skripnuk et al., ‘Analysis of the Current State of the Northern Sea Route’, 3.\n38. Nornickel is the leader in Russia’s metals and mining industry, as well as the largest palla-\ndium and high-grade nickel producer and one of the largest producers of platinum and\ncopper. The company also produces cobalt, rhodium, silver, gold, iridium, ruthenium, sel-\nenium, tellurium, and sulfur. For more information see: Nornickel, ‘Company Proﬁle’,\nhttps://nornickel.com/company/proﬁle/.\n\n39. Anastasiya Vedeneyeva and Polina Smertina, ‘“Nornikel’” vlozhitsya v ledokoly’, Kommer-\n\nsant, July 23, 2021, https://www.kommersant.ru/doc/4917011.\n\n40. Ibid.\n41. Rosneft, ‘DTSSS, “Rosatom” i “Noril’skiy nikel’” dogovorilis’ o sotrudnichestve v ramkakh\nstroitel’stva dvukhtoplivnogo ledokola’, September 3, 2021, https://www.rosneft.ru/press/\nreleases/item/207665/.\n\n42. Both of those companies are owned by Rosneft. Vedomosti, ‘DTSSS, “Rosatom” i “Noril’skiy\nnikel’” dogovorilis’ o sotrudnichestve v ramkakh stroitel’stva dvukhtoplivnogo ledokola’, Septem-\nber 3, 2021, https://www.vedomosti.ru/press_releases/2021/09/03/dtsss-rosatom-i-norilskii-\nnikel-dogovorilis-o-sotrudnichestve-v-ramkah-stroitelstva-dvuhtoplivnogo-ledokola.\n\n43. ROSATOM, ‘Rosatom, PAO “Nornikel’” i AO “DTSSS” podpisali soglasheniye o sovmest-\nnom proyektirovanii i stroitel’stve SPG-ledokola’, September 3, 2021, https://rosatom.ru/\njournalist/news/rosatom-pao-nornikel-i-ao-dtsss-podpisali-soglashenie-o-sovmestnom-pr\noektirovanii-i-stroitelstve-spg/.\n\n44. TASS,\n\n‘“Nornikel’” vlozhit 90 mlrd rubley do 2030 goda v realizatsiyu transportnykh\n\nproyektov’, December 20, 2021, https://tass.ru/ekonomika/13251083.\n\n45. LUKOIL, Press Release, 2009, https://lukoil.ru/PressCenter/Pressreleases/Pressrelease?rid=\n\n52581.\n\n46. NANS – Natsional’naya Assotsiatsiya neftegazovogo servisa,\n\n‘Singapur postroil dlya\n“LUKOYL” a tri mini-ledokola’, September 11, 2015, https://nangs.org/news/industry/\nsingapur-postroil-dlya-lukojla-tri-mini-ledokola-2002.\n\n47. More information on operational icebreakers of the Russian Navy can be found in Table 2.\n48. Oleg Kuleshov, ‘Boyevyye ledokoly Rossii: proshloye, nastoyashcheye i budushcheye’, Kor-\nabel.ru, December 9, 2019, https://www.korabel.ru/news/comments/voennye_ledokoly_\nrossii_proshloe_nastoyaschee_i_buduschee.htmlhttps://www.korabel.ru/news/comments/\nvoennye_ledokoly_rossii_proshloe_nastoyaschee_i_buduschee.html.\n\n49. Ibid.\n50. Ibid.\n51. Ibid.\n52. Dimitry Litovkin, ‘Arkticheskiy “Bastion”: Rossiya zakryla Sevmorput’, TASS, December 2,\n\n2019, https://tass.ru/opinions/7244521.\n\n53. Admiralteyskiye verﬁ, ‘Dizel’-elektricheskiy ledokol proyekta 21180 “Il’ya Muromets”’, http://\nadmship.ru/production/surface/dizel-elektricheskiy-ledokol-proekta-21180-ilya-muromets-/.\n54. TASS, ‘Ispytaniya korablya ledovogo klassa “Ivan Papanin” nachnutsya v kontse 2022 goda’,\n\nJune 23, 2021, https://tass.ru/armiya-i-opk/11723061.\n\n55. Litovkin, ‘Arkticheskiy “Bastion”’.\n56. PortNews, ‘Ledokol “Yevpatiy Kolovrat” vyveden iz ellinga’, November 22, 2020, https://\n\nportnews.ru/news/305101/.\n\n57. Dmitry Boltenkov and Anton Lavrov, ‘Oborona po-ﬂotski: k 2028 godu Sever poluch