18	1	\N	\N	\N	c7fc00e96a03f3968620581459d4a4f0367beff3e1e00442b98373d542f7646e	/Volumes/jer4TBv3/enliterator/arctic_research/SITREP-NORAD- Deepened China-Russia military cooperation increases threats to US Homeland.pdf	\N	text	completed	223471	NORAD: Deepened China-Russia military cooperation\nincreases threats to U.S. homeland\nSherman, Jason . Sherman, Jason.\n\nProQuest document link\n\nFULL TEXT\n\nFebruary 18, 2025 at 2:40 PM\n\n The likelihood of direct military conflict between the U.S. and its top adversaries is growing, fueled by deepening\n\nstrategic cooperation between China, Russia, North Korea and Iran, according to the head of the North American\n\nAerospace Defense Command (NORAD) and U.S. Northern Command (NORTHCOM).\n\nAir Force Gen. Gregory Guillot warned that these adversaries are expanding their military capabilities and\n\nstrengthening ties in ways that directly threaten the U.S. homeland in Feb. 13 written testimony prepared for the\n\nSenate Armed Services Committee. He described a broad coordinated effort among America's adversaries that could\n\nescalate a regional conflict into a global one.\n\n"The likelihood of direct conflict between the United States and one of its four principal adversaries is increasing,"\n\nGuillot testified. "Strategic cooperation among these adversaries has grown substantially since the beginning of the\n\nUkraine war, increasing the risk that war with one adversary could quickly expand into war with an enemy coalition."\n\nThe testimony underscores how U.S. defense officials are recalibrating their threat assessments to account for not\n\njust China's growing military power but also the potential for coordinated operations with Russia and North Korea.\n\nThe shift marks a significant evolution in strategic concerns as adversaries that have historically acted independently\n\nare now leveraging each other's military assets and expertise to challenge the U.S. and its allies.\n\nOne of the major developments highlighted by Guillot is the closer military collaboration between China and Russia.\n\nIn the summer of 2024, he said China deployed bomber aircraft to a Russian Arctic airbase for the first time and\n\nconducted a joint bomber patrol over the Bering Sea alongside Russian heavy bombers.\n\n"Such 'access transfer' accelerates and extends China's ability to threaten North America in the air domain and raises\n\nthe specter of coordinated military operations in the event of a strategic conflict," Guillot testified.\n\nThe move represents a shift in China's military posture, he said. While Beijing has historically focused its military\n\nmodernization on regional conflicts, its expanded presence in the Arctic and North Pacific suggests a broader\n\nstrategic intent. If China gains routine access to Russian airbases and naval ports in the Arctic, it could significantly\n\nshorten the time required to launch strikes against North American targets, according to the four-star general.\n\nMeanwhile, North Korea has also demonstrated its willingness to support Russia's war in Ukraine, with Pyongyang\n\nreportedly supplying military personnel and munitions to Moscow. Guillot cautioned this growing partnership raises\n\nconcerns about potential technology transfers that could accelerate North Korea's weapons development --\n\nparticularly its strategic missile and cyber capabilities.\n\nWhile the Pentagon has long tracked China's nuclear modernization, Guillot's testimony pointed to an increasing\n\nthreat from conventional Chinese weapon systems that can strike U.S. territory. Over the past year, China has:\n\n* Deployed warships to the Bering Sea for the fourth consecutive year, operating within cruise missile range of critical\n\ninfrastructure in Alaska.\n\n* Expanded its fleet of Shang III-class nuclear-powered guided-missile submarines (SSGNs) which in the coming years\n\ncould provide Beijing a clandestine land-attack capability against the U.S. West Coast.\n\n* Integrated air-refuellable H-6N bombers into joint Sino-Russian bomber patrols extending China's ability to conduct\n\nlong-range strikes without needing forward bases.\n\n\f* Developed conventionally armed intercontinental ballistic missiles (ICBMs) which could allow China to strike U.S.\n\nterritory without crossing the nuclear threshold.\n\nGuillot emphasized China's advancements in these areas "increase the likelihood that an armed conflict would\n\ninclude direct strikes on the homeland." The ability to hold U.S. infrastructure at risk with conventional weapons\n\nprovides Beijing with an escalation option that falls between cyberattacks and nuclear strikes, complicating U.S.\n\nstrategic decision-making.\n\nChina's development of new nuclear delivery systems also poses fresh challenges for U.S. early warning and missile\n\ndefense systems. According to Guillot, China is refining its ability to field nuclear-armed ICBMs equipped with\n\nhypersonic glide vehicles (HGVs) or capable of following a fractional orbital bombardment system (FOBS) trajectory.\n\nThese systems which can approach North America from the south rather than the traditional northern vector are\n\ndesigned to evade U.S. early warning radars.\n\n"Such systems, if fielded, would erode strategic stability by degrading our ability to provide granular and actionable\n\nwar	{"filename": "SITREP-NORAD- Deepened China-Russia military cooperation increases threats to US Homeland.pdf", "file_size": 223471, "document_type": "security_analysis"}	{}	\N	2025-08-11 21:46:06.921408	2025-08-11 22:38:35.116009	extracted	{"terms_count": 15, "extracted_at": "2025-08-11T22:14:13.674Z"}	NORAD: Deepened China-Russia military cooperation\nincreases threats to U.S. homeland\nSherman, Jason . Sherman, Jason.\n\nProQuest document link\n\nFULL TEXT\n\nFebruary 18, 2025 at 2:40 PM\n\n The likelihood of direct military conflict between the U.S. and its top adversaries is growing, fueled by deepening\n\nstrategic cooperation between China, Russia, North Korea and Iran, according to the head of the North American\n\nAerospace Defense Command (NORAD) and U.S. Northern Command (NORTHCOM).\n\nAir Force Gen. Gregory Guillot warned that these adversaries are expanding their military capabilities and\n\nstrengthening ties in ways that directly threaten the U.S. homeland in Feb. 13 written testimony prepared for the\n\nSenate Armed Services Committee. He described a broad coordinated effort among America's adversaries that could\n\nescalate a regional conflict into a global one.\n\n"The likelihood of direct conflict between the United States and one of its four principal adversaries is increasing,"\n\nGuillot testified. "Strategic cooperation among these adversaries has grown substantially since the beginning of the\n\nUkraine war, increasing the risk that war with one adversary could quickly expand into war with an enemy coalition."\n\nThe testimony underscores how U.S. defense officials are recalibrating their threat assessments to account for not\n\njust China's growing military power but also the potential for coordinated operations with Russia and North Korea.\n\nThe shift marks a significant evolution in strategic concerns as adversaries that have historically acted independently\n\nare now leveraging each other's military assets and expertise to challenge the U.S. and its allies.\n\nOne of the major developments highlighted by Guillot is the closer military collaboration between China and Russia.\n\nIn the summer of 2024, he said China deployed bomber aircraft to a Russian Arctic airbase for the first time and\n\nconducted a joint bomber patrol over the Bering Sea alongside Russian heavy bombers.\n\n"Such 'access transfer' accelerates and extends China's ability to threaten North America in the air domain and raises\n\nthe specter of coordinated military operations in the event of a strategic conflict," Guillot testified.\n\nThe move represents a shift in China's military posture, he said. While Beijing has historically focused its military\n\nmodernization on regional conflicts, its expanded presence in the Arctic and North Pacific suggests a broader\n\nstrategic intent. If China gains routine access to Russian airbases and naval ports in the Arctic, it could significantly\n\nshorten the time required to launch strikes against North American targets, according to the four-star general.\n\nMeanwhile, North Korea has also demonstrated its willingness to support Russia's war in Ukraine, with Pyongyang\n\nreportedly supplying military personnel and munitions to Moscow. Guillot cautioned this growing partnership raises\n\nconcerns about potential technology transfers that could accelerate North Korea's weapons development --\n\nparticularly its strategic missile and cyber capabilities.\n\nWhile the Pentagon has long tracked China's nuclear modernization, Guillot's testimony pointed to an increasing\n\nthreat from conventional Chinese weapon systems that can strike U.S. territory. Over the past year, China has:\n\n* Deployed warships to the Bering Sea for the fourth consecutive year, operating within cruise missile range of critical\n\ninfrastructure in Alaska.\n\n* Expanded its fleet of Shang III-class nuclear-powered guided-missile submarines (SSGNs) which in the coming years\n\ncould provide Beijing a clandestine land-attack capability against the U.S. West Coast.\n\n* Integrated air-refuellable H-6N bombers into joint Sino-Russian bomber patrols extending China's ability to conduct\n\nlong-range strikes without needing forward bases.\n\n\f* Developed conventionally armed intercontinental ballistic missiles (ICBMs) which could allow China to strike U.S.\n\nterritory without crossing the nuclear threshold.\n\nGuillot emphasized China's advancements in these areas "increase the likelihood that an armed conflict would\n\ninclude direct strikes on the homeland." The ability to hold U.S. infrastructure at risk with conventional weapons\n\nprovides Beijing with an escalation option that falls between cyberattacks and nuclear strikes, complicating U.S.\n\nstrategic decision-making.\n\nChina's development of new nuclear delivery systems also poses fresh challenges for U.S. early warning and missile\n\ndefense systems. According to Guillot, China is refining its ability to field nuclear-armed ICBMs equipped with\n\nhypersonic glide vehicles (HGVs) or capable of following a fractional orbital bombardment system (FOBS) trajectory.\n\nThese systems which can approach North America from the south rather than the traditional northern vector are\n\ndesigned to evade U.S. early warning radars.\n\n"Such systems, if fielded, would erode strategic stability by degrading our ability to provide granular and actionable\n\nwarning of an inbound attack," Guillot warned.\n\nDETAILS\n\nSubject:\n\nLocation:\n\nTestimony; Modernization; Missiles; Cooperation\n\nNorth Korea; China; Beijing China; Russia; Bering Sea; United States--US; Arctic region;\n\nUkraine; North America\n\nPublication title:\n\nInsideDefense.com's SitRep; Arlington\n\nPublication year:\n\n2025\n\nPublication date:\n\nFeb 19, 2025\n\nPublisher:\n\nInside Washington Publishers\n\nPlace of publication:\n\nArlington\n\nCountry of publication:\n\nUnited States, Arlington\n\nPublication subject:\n\nMilitary\n\nSource type:\n\nTrade Journal\n\nLanguage of publication:\n\nEnglish\n\nDocument type:\n\nNews\n\nProQuest document ID:\n\n3168296968\n\nDocument URL:\n\nhttps://nps.idm.oclc.org/login?url=https://www.proquest.com/trade-journals/norad-\n\ndeepened-china-russia-military-cooperation/docview/3168296968/se-\n\n2?accountid=12702\n\nCopyright:\n\nCopyright Inside Washington Publishers Feb 19, 2025\n\n\fFull text availability:\n\nThis publication may be subject to restrictions within certain markets, including\n\ncorporations, non-profits, government institutions, and public libraries. In those cases\n\nrecords will be visible to users, but not full text.\n\nLast updated:\n\n2025-02-19\n\nDatabase:\n\nAdvanced Technologies &Computer Science Collection,ProQuest Central\n\nLINKS\nLinking Service\n\nDatabase copyright (cid:211) 2025 ProQuest LLC. All rights reserved.\n\nTerms and Conditions   Contact ProQuest\n\n\n	skipped	{"skipped_at": "2025-08-11T22:38:35.115Z", "skip_reason": "All 15 terms were duplicates of already-processed terms"}	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	6c1cd1343c8815414f22b646017834bdd9a4a15b04f4323760bbf08a0e256b50	\N	markitdown	gpt-4.1	tier_1	1601	6379	{"routing_info":{"status":"tier_1","model":"gpt-4.1","estimated_tokens":1601,"limit":380000,"reason":"Content fits in Tier 1 model (gpt-4.1)","cost_tier":"standard"},"original_metadata":{"service":"Ingest::TokenAwareMarkitdownService","file_path":"/Volumes/jer4TBv3/enliterator/arctic_research/SITREP-NORAD- Deepened China-Russia military cooperation increases threats to US Homeland.pdf","file_size_bytes":223471,"content_length_chars":6379,"estimated_tokens":1601,"timestamp":"2025-08-11T22:07:54Z","supported_formats":[".pdf",".docx",".pptx",".xlsx",".md",".txt",".html",".rtf",".odt",".jpg",".jpeg",".png",".bmp",".gif",".svg"]},"detection_result":{"media_type":"document","markitdown_supported":true,"mime_type":"application/pdf","file_extension":".pdf"},"processed_at":"2025-08-11T22:07:54Z"}
19	1	\N	\N	\N	3a19a7c473813730c1f4f2f2e4a5e2c8fa95af6584b21592bde81cc29329e89b	/Volumes/jer4TBv3/enliterator/arctic_research/Stories of Change and Sustainability in the Arctic Regions_Sorly.pdf	\N	text	completed	17909325	\fStories of Change and Sustainability\nin the Arctic Regions\n\nThis  book  presents  stories  of  sustainability  from  communities  in  circumpolar\nregions as they grapple with environmental, economic and societal changes and\nchallenges.\n\nPolar regions are changing rapidly. These changes will dramatically effect eco-\nsystems, economy, people, communities and their interdependencies. Given this,\nthe stories being told about lives and livelihood development are changing also.\nThis book is the first of its kind to curate stories about opportunity and responsi-\nbility, tensions and contradictions, un/ethical action, resilience, adaptability and\nsustainability, all within the shifting geopolitics of the north. The book looks at\nchange and sustainability through multidisciplinary and empirically based work,\ndrawing  on  case  studies  from  Norway,  Sweden,  Alaska,  Canada,  Finland  and\nNorthwest Russia, with a notable focus on indigenous peoples. Chapters touch\non  topics  as  wide  ranging  as  reindeer  herding,  mental  health,  climate  change,\nland-use  conflicts  and  sustainable  business.  The  volume  asks  whose  voices  are\nbeing heard, who benefits, how particular changes affect people’s sense of com-\nmunity and longstanding and cherished values plus livelihood practices and what\nare the environmental, economic and social impacts of contemporary and future\noriented changes with regard to issues of sustainability?\n\nThis volume will be of great interest to students and scholars of sustainability\nstudies,  sustainable  development,  environmental  sociology,  indigenous  studies\nand environmental anthropology.\n\nRita  Sørly  is  an  Associate  Professor  in  Social  Work  at  UiT  The  Arctic  Uni-\nversity of Norway and a Senior Researcher at the Norwegian Research Centre\n(NORCE), Norway.\n\nTony Ghaye is a Professor at Loughborough University in London, UK.\n\nBård Kårtveit is a Senior Researcher in the Department for Regional Develop-\nment at the Norwegian Research Centre (NORCE), Norway.\n\n\fRoutledge Studies in Sustainability\n\nSustainability Governance and Hierarchy\nPhilippe Hamman\n\nEnergy, Environmental and Economic Sustainability in East Asia\nPolicies and Institutional Reforms\nEdited by Soocheol Lee, Hector Pollitt and Kiyoshi Fujikawa\n\nGreen Skills Research in South Africa\nModels, Cases and Methods\nEureta Rosenberg, Presha Ramsarup and Heila Lotz-Sisitka\n\nSustainability and the Automobile Industry in Asia\nPolicy and Governance\nEdited by Aki Suwa and Masahiko Iguchi\n\nSmart Green World?\nMaking Digitalization Work for Sustainability\nSteffen Lange and Tilman Santarius\n\nContesting Hydropower in the Brazilian Amazon\nEd Atkins\n\nCircular Cities\nA Revolution in Urban Sustainability\nJo Williams\n\nStories of Change and Sustainability in the Arctic Regions\nThe Interdependence of Local and Global\nEdited by Rita Sørly, Tony Ghaye and Bård Kårtveit\n\nFor more information on this series, please visit: www.routledge.com/Routledge-\nStudies-in-Sustainability/book-series/RSSTY\n\n\fStories of Change and\nSustainability in the Arctic\nRegions\nThe Interdependence of Local and Global\n\nEdited by Rita Sørly, Tony Ghaye and\nBård Kårtveit\n\n\fFirst published 2022\nby Routledge\n2 Park Square, Milton Park, Abingdon, Oxon OX14 4RN\n\nand by Routledge\n605 Third Avenue, New York, NY 10158\n\nRoutledge is an imprint of the Taylor & Francis Group, an informa business\n\n© 2022 selection and editorial matter, Rita Sørly, Tony Ghaye and Bård\nKårtveit; individual chapters, the contributors\n\nThe right of Rita Sørly, Tony Ghaye and Bård Kårtveit to be identified\nas the authors of the editorial material, and of the authors for their\nindividual chapters, has been asserted in accordance with sections 77 and\n78 of the Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988.\n\nAll rights reserved. No part of this book may be reprinted or reproduced\nor utilized in any form or by any electronic, mechanical, or other means,\nnow known or hereafter invented, including photocopying and recording,\nor in any information storage or retrieval system, without permission in\nwriting from the publishers.\n\nTrademark notice: Product or corporate names may be trademarks or\nregistered trademarks, and are used only for identification and explanation\nwithout intent to infringe.\n\nBritish Library Cataloguing-in-Publication Data\nA catalogue record for this book is available from the British Library\n\nLibrary of Congress Cataloging-in-Publication Data\nNames: Sørly, Rita, editor. | Ghaye, Tony, editor. | Kårtveit, Bård Helge,\neditor.\nTitle: Stories of change and sustainability in the Arctic regions : the\ninterdependence of local and global / edited by Rita Sørly, Tony Ghaye\nand Bård Kårtveit.\nDescription: Abingdon, Oxon ; New York, NY : Routledge, 2022. |\nSeries: Routledge studies in sustainability | Includes bibliographical\nreferences and index.\nIdentifiers: LCCN 2021024298 (print) | LCCN 2021024299 (ebook) |\nISBN 9780367632847 (hardback) | ISBN 9780367632854 (paperback) |\nISBN 9781003118633 (ebook)\nSu	{"filename": "Stories of Change and Sustainability in the Arctic Regions_Sorly.pdf", "file_size": 17909325, "document_type": "climate_research"}	{}	\N	2025-08-11 21:46:06.941083	2025-08-11 22:38:35.11688	extracted	{"terms_count": 15, "extracted_at": "2025-08-11T22:14:38.107Z"}	\fStories of Change and Sustainability\nin the Arctic Regions\n\nThis  book  presents  stories  of  sustainability  from  communities  in  circumpolar\nregions as they grapple with environmental, economic and societal changes and\nchallenges.\n\nPolar regions are changing rapidly. These changes will dramatically effect eco-\nsystems, economy, people, communities and their interdependencies. Given this,\nthe stories being told about lives and livelihood development are changing also.\nThis book is the first of its kind to curate stories about opportunity and responsi-\nbility, tensions and contradictions, un/ethical action, resilience, adaptability and\nsustainability, all within the shifting geopolitics of the north. The book looks at\nchange and sustainability through multidisciplinary and empirically based work,\ndrawing  on  case  studies  from  Norway,  Sweden,  Alaska,  Canada,  Finland  and\nNorthwest Russia, with a notable focus on indigenous peoples. Chapters touch\non  topics  as  wide  ranging  as  reindeer  herding,  mental  health,  climate  change,\nland-use  conflicts  and  sustainable  business.  The  volume  asks  whose  voices  are\nbeing heard, who benefits, how particular changes affect people’s sense of com-\nmunity and longstanding and cherished values plus livelihood practices and what\nare the environmental, economic and social impacts of contemporary and future\noriented changes with regard to issues of sustainability?\n\nThis volume will be of great interest to students and scholars of sustainability\nstudies,  sustainable  development,  environmental  sociology,  indigenous  studies\nand environmental anthropology.\n\nRita  Sørly  is  an  Associate  Professor  in  Social  Work  at  UiT  The  Arctic  Uni-\nversity of Norway and a Senior Researcher at the Norwegian Research Centre\n(NORCE), Norway.\n\nTony Ghaye is a Professor at Loughborough University in London, UK.\n\nBård Kårtveit is a Senior Researcher in the Department for Regional Develop-\nment at the Norwegian Research Centre (NORCE), Norway.\n\n\fRoutledge Studies in Sustainability\n\nSustainability Governance and Hierarchy\nPhilippe Hamman\n\nEnergy, Environmental and Economic Sustainability in East Asia\nPolicies and Institutional Reforms\nEdited by Soocheol Lee, Hector Pollitt and Kiyoshi Fujikawa\n\nGreen Skills Research in South Africa\nModels, 